{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11484,11690p'\"","aggregated_output":" 11484\t## D745 · 2026-11-23 · The D744 re-read calibrates the readers but stops on a qualifier-list gap and on S's unsupported share; council 59 recommends a targeted $0 test of one clarification, for Alex to authorize\n 11485\t\n 11486\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md` §5; `REREAD2-TALLY.json`):\n 11487\t  - **The readers are calibrated.**\n 11488\t    - All five controls passed: midpoint, 108.5 misread, overlap, disjoint, unsupported assumption.\n 11489\t    - No defect was marked by both readers on the 12 replies.\n 11490\t  - **STOP on two lines, as written:**\n 11491\t    1. T10 (S, Claude writer) F11: \"an estimated $750 billion\" recorded with qualifier \"none\". Both adjudicators upheld it. \"estimated\" is\n 11492\t       not in the closed qualifier list, so the format forced \"none\". This is 1 affected figure of 251 source figures in the three writer\n 11493\t       runs.\n 11494\t    2. S has zero usable replies. Both writers state a data-center share of total capex (90%; 95–100%) that the passages do not size, and\n 11495\t       the share decides the band.\n 11496\t  - **E's Codex reply** is now \"not supported\": its 15% cut from the guided floor decides the band. This is the same weakness.\n 11497\t- **Council round 59** (`codex-r59.md`) chose a targeted $0 test, (c), over Claude's lean to go to Alex for the paid re-send, (b):\n 11498\t  - Going straight to the paid re-send would measure the known weakness in gpt-6-luna without the request saying what a supported size\n 11499\t    is.\n 11500\t  - Skipping a clean result needs Alex to change the registered sequence anyway.\n 11501\t  - Claude agrees: the weakness is unsupported precision in a number that decides the rung, which is the goal's own failure (D725).\n 11502\t- **Proposed test (for Alex to authorize):**\n 11503\t  1. Add \"estimated\" to the qualifier list, and show readers the allowed values. Prove it on the saved replies, with a case and a\n 11504\t     mutant.\n 11505\t  2. Add one clarification to the existing instruction: when a deduction or cut decides the band, show the evidence for its size or a\n 11506\t     bound the passages support that justifies the band; if neither, answer unresolved.\n 11507\t  3. Test on S and on one stored case where the passages do support a narrower figure.\n 11508\t  4. Before the test, Alex rules whether a justified unresolved answer counts as usable.\n 11509\t  - Falsifier: a fresh S answer still picks a band on an unsupported share, or the supported case is withheld.\n 11510\t- **Alex's answers** (2026-11-23):\n 11511\t  - to \"怎么走？\": \"批准针对性小测 (推荐)\". The other options were \"不小测，直接议花钱重发\" and \"先停在这里\".\n 11512\t  - to whether a justified \"无法判断\" counts: \"算合格 (推荐)\". The other option was \"不算\".\n 11513\t- **Decided (Alex):**\n 11514\t  1. Run the targeted $0 test.\n 11515\t  2. An unresolved answer whose reason shows the passages cannot settle the rung counts as a usable answer.\n 11516\t- **Council round 60** (`codex-r60.md`) set the wording and the registration (`SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D745]):\n 11517\t  - Rung-deciding shares, deductions and discounts must show their passage support or a justifying bound, else unresolved.\n 11518\t  - The default discount is made subject to that rule.\n 11519\t  - The three controls are the saved E 15% cut, a vague P-v8 unresolved answer and a justified S unresolved answer (expected no, no,\n 11520\t    yes).\n 11521\t  - Pass: every S and E reply settled yes on a forecast or its reason; both P-v8 replies settled yes on a forecast. A support split\n 11522\t    is inconclusive.\n 11523\t\n 11524\t## D746 · 2026-11-23 · The D745 targeted test fails on E's unsized cut; S splits on how far an indirect comparison can support a forecast; council 61–61b: one general question to Alex, then a $0 stored-answer re-read; the paid re-send stays on hold\n 11525\t\n 11526\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md`; run `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/`,\n 11527\t  `TALLY.json`):\n 11528\t  - **FAIL on a registered line.** E's Codex reply (rung 3, $36B) realises 90% of the $40B reference and itself says \"the passages\n 11529\t    give no order sizes or shipment pace\". Both first readers marked it not supported.\n 11530\t  - **Four splits, all along model family** (8 of 8 adjudicator votes went with their own family): S Claude (98% share), S Codex ($25B\n 11531\t    deduction), E Claude (1.8x with a 90% Vera share) and the justified-unresolved S control.\n 11532\t  - **The rest:**\n 11533\t    - P-v8: 2 of 2 supported;\n 11534\t    - the unsupported-cut and vague-unresolved controls read as expected;\n 11535\t    - no refusal, no defect;\n 11536\t    - 0 of 6 replies withheld.\n 11537\t  - **One construction change, disclosed.** The justified-unresolved control was rebuilt on the third run's S Codex reply, because the\n 11538\t    S Claude reply is refused by the current code (S1). Its reason text is unchanged.\n 11539\t- **Council rounds 61 and 61b** (`claude-r61.md`, `codex-r61.md`, `codex-r61b.md`) reached consensus:\n 11540\t  - **The FAIL stands.** Resolving every split would still leave E's Codex reply failed.\n 11541\t  - **Two problems.**\n 11542\t    1. **The reading standard is not settled.** The open point is how far a related figure can support a forecast when the passages do\n 11543\t       not measure the asked quantity on the deciding side.\n 11544\t       - S has split this way in three readings: \"more than $730B on AI infrastructure\" for the same four companies, as a floor for\n 11545\t         their data-center spending.\n 11546\t       - E Claude's bracket fails on its own assumptions ($20B × 1.5 × 90% = $27B, rung 4). That point is arithmetic.\n 11547\t       - D741 ruling 3 still requires a stated deduction or a supported bound. The threshold for \"supported\" when the support is an\n 11548\t         indirect comparison is Alex's to set.\n 11549\t    2. **Writers size a risk without evidence.** 3 of 4 E replies across two runs carry a disputed cut or share, and D745's sentence\n 11550\t       did not stop it.\n 11551\t  - **Corrections.**\n 11552\t    - Claude withdrew two reasons for the lean. D725 does not promise that code catches every invented figure. AGENTS §8's \"no data to\n 11553\t      verify\" concerns outcomes after the fact.\n 11554\t    - The result page was corrected in three places: U05 had two first readers; unresolved singletons are published and inspected; the\n 11555\t      paid S asks about CY2027.\n 11556\t    - Whether a paid STOP is predictable: it is a conditional risk, not a measured rate (Codex; Claude agrees).\n 11557\t  - **The recommendation.** Ask Alex one general question (below), with a lean to allow the forecast when the answer explains why the\n 11558\t    comparison applies to the companies, the period and the quantity asked, and why, under its own assumptions, that side of the line\n 11559\t    is more likely.\n 11560\t  - **The one test after his ruling.** A $0 blinded re-read of nine stored answers under his words, added to the shared definitions:\n 11561\t    - the answers: U01–U06, U08, the third run's T10 (for its spending argument only), and T15 (the accepted E bracket);\n 11562\t    - fresh readers, one per family;\n 11563\t    - what his choice implies for each answer is recorded before reading.\n 11564\t    - It moves on to a writer repair only if both families apply his words alike. A continuing split leaves the publication question\n 11565\t      open in practice. A clean re-read does not turn D745 into a pass.\n 11566\t  - **The paid re-send stays on hold** until an E writer repair passes a clean $0 test. The proposed repair: the writer chooses between\n 11567\t    a forecast and \"can't tell\" before it assigns a discount, naming what makes one side more likely under its own assumptions. It is a\n 11568\t    new arm and goes to council before any send. Publishing management's figure whenever it sits on a cutoff would change the product,\n 11569\t    and needs Alex's own decision.\n 11570\t- **The question to Alex** (pending): \"资料没直接给出所问的数。用一个相关数字作比较，能解释为什么结果更可能在线的这一边，但另一边仍有可能。这时可以发布预测，还是要等到能从资料算出结果落在线的哪一边？\"\n 11571\t- **Not done.** Nothing was paid. Nothing was tuned on the 36 stored answers. The request was not changed after the registration.\n 11572\t\n 11573\t## D747 · 2026-11-23 · Alex rules that a forecast may rest on an explained indirect comparison\n 11574\t\n 11575\t- **The question** (after D746): \"资料没直接给出题目问的那个数，只能拿一个相关的数字来比。比如问四大云厂商今年的数据中心开支能不能过 7000 亿，资料只说“这四家 AI 基础设施开支超过 7300 亿”。这种比较能说明结果更可能落在线的某一边，但另一边也还有可能。这时系统可以发布预测吗，还是要等资料能直接算出落在哪边？\"\n 11576\t  - Options:\n 11577\t    - \"可以发布，要讲清楚 (推荐)\": the answer must explain why the comparison applies to the companies, the year and the quantity\n 11578\t      asked, and why, under its own assumptions, that side is more likely; an answer that cannot explain it is not supported;\n 11579\t    - \"先说“还判断不了”\";\n 11580\t    - \"先停在这里\".\n 11581\t- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-23): \"可以发布，要讲清楚 (推荐)\".\n 11582\t- **Decided (Alex).** When the passages do not give the asked quantity, a forecast may rest on a related figure. It is supported\n 11583\t  only when the answer explains two things: why the comparison applies to the companies, the period and the quantity asked; and why,\n 11584\t  under the answer's own stated assumptions, the chosen side of the line is more likely. An answer that cannot explain this is not\n 11585\t  supported.\n 11586\t  - D741 ruling 3 still holds: a total used for a narrower quantity states the deduction as its own figure, or gives a supported\n 11587\t    bound.\n 11588\t  - D745's ruling still holds: a justified unresolved answer counts as usable.\n 11589\t- **Next (D746).** The ruling's words go into the shared reader definitions (`blind_rules.py`). Then one $0 blinded re-read of nine\n 11590\t  stored answers, registered with its expected readings and reviewed by the council before any packet is read.\n 11591\t\n 11592\t## D748 · 2026-11-23 · The D747 re-read fails on a self-contradicting floor; three of four earlier splits settle under the ruling; council 63: ask Alex to authorize the E writer repair; the paid re-send stays on hold\n 11593\t\n 11594\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md` §6; `REREAD-TALLY.json`, `reread-reasons.json`):\n 11595\t  - **FAIL on the stop line.** On U02 (S, Claude writer), both adjudicators upheld \"part above or added into its whole\". The answer\n 11596\t    calls \"more than $730B\" of AI infrastructure \"the supported floor\" for data-center spending, while its own total spending is $720B\n 11597\t    and its data-center outcome $705.6B.\n 11598\t    - The code did not catch it. The figure is linked only to the 98% share, and the floor is claimed in prose. D738 leaves that\n 11599\t      reading to readers.\n 11600\t    - In the 18 stored blind replies this explicit defect occurs once (Codex, council 63).\n 11601\t  - **Six of nine settle with compatible reasons**, and each fixed reading that settled matched its registration:\n 11602\t    - U01, U03 and U05: no;\n 11603\t    - U06: no;\n 11604\t    - U04 (justified unresolved): yes;\n 11605\t    - U08 ($25B deduction explained by the same companies' AI-infrastructure figure and Meta's share): yes.\n 11606\t  - **Three of the four answers that split in the targeted test now settle** (U04, U06, U08).\n 11607\t  - **Three splits remain:** T10, T15 and U02's support vote.\n 11608\t    - All six adjudicator votes followed their own family's first vote. Which family was stricter changed with the question: Codex on\n 11609\t      S, Claude on E.\n 11610\t    - T15 had been accepted in all three earlier readings.\n 11611\t- **A process error** (Claude and council 62). The registration required five boundary answers to settle, or to settle as expected,\n 11612\t  without estimating how often readers split on boundary answers (AGENTS §7.1, \"a conjunction over many items fails by chance\"). The\n 11613\t  stop on U02 does not depend on it.\n 11614\t- **Council round 63** (`claude-r63.md`, `codex-r63.md`) reached consensus:\n 11615\t  - **The FAIL stands. No further reader round.** Another vote on the same nine answers leaves the writers untouched.\n 11616\t  - **Claude conceded three points.**\n 11617\t    - The remaining splits are not shown to be random variation. T10 and T15 turn on judgement over \"explains why\" and \"more likely\".\n 11618\t      On U02, one side accepted the band and the other required the writer's stated route. D747 already rejects U02's route. No\n 11619\t      missing general clause was found.\n 11620\t    - A writer test with registered lines only on stable readings would let a repeat of U05's invented cut through. Each fresh E and\n 11621\t      S answer must earn agreement for its forecast or for its specific reason for \"can't tell\".\n 11622\t    - Changing the calculation format for prose-claimed bounds is deferred. One case does not establish it, and U08 shows that an\n 11623\t      indirect comparison must stay able to inform a forecast without becoming a binding minimum. U02 becomes a control instead.\n 11624\t  - **The recommendation.** Ask Alex to authorize the E writer repair despite the three disclosed splits. D746 made the repair\n 11625\t    conditional on reader agreement, so going ahead needs his decision.\n 11626\t    - The repair: the writer decides whether the evidence favours one side of the line before it chooses a discount. If it can\n 11627\t      explain only why sales might fall short, it says what remains unknown.\n 11628\t    - The changed request goes to the council before any send.\n 11629\t  - **The one test after approval.** A $0 blind test:\n 11630\t    - fresh Codex and Claude writers on E, S and P-v8;\n 11631\t    - the saved U05 and U02 mixed in as controls for an invented cut and an impossible floor.\n 11632\t    - To pass:\n 11633\t      - each fresh E and S answer earns agreement for its forecast or its specific \"can't tell\" reason;\n 11634\t      - both P-v8 answers are supported forecasts;\n 11635\t      - the code accepts the fresh replies;\n 11636\t      - no confirmed defect survives.\n 11637\t    - A settled unsupported answer or a contradictory floor fails. A persistent split is inconclusive. Only a clean result supports\n 11638\t      asking Alex for the paid re-send.\n 11639\t- **Not done.** Nothing was paid. No request text was changed.\n 11640\t\n 11641\t## D749 · 2026-11-23 · Alex authorizes the E writer repair and its $0 writer test\n 11642\t\n 11643\t- **The question** (after D748): \"下一步怎么走？\"\n 11644\t  - Options:\n 11645\t    - \"批准修写手再小测 (推荐)\": the writer first decides whether the evidence favours one side of the line, then whether to discount;\n 11646\t      if it can only say sales might fall short, it answers \"can't tell\" and says what is missing. The changed request goes to the\n 11647\t      council, then one $0 writer test on E, S and P with the two failed answers as controls. A clean pass comes back to Alex for the\n 11648\t      paid re-send.\n 11649\t    - \"不修，直接议花钱重发\".\n 11650\t    - \"先停在这里\".\n 11651\t- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-23): \"批准修写手再小测 (推荐)\".\n 11652\t- **Decided (Alex).** Repair the writer request as council 63 described. Review it in council before any send. Then run one $0\n 11653\t  blind writer test on E, S and P-v8, with the saved U05 (invented cut) and U02 (impossible floor) as reader controls, under D748's\n 11654\t  pass lines. The paid re-send stays on hold until that test passes clean.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; nl -ba $d/SMALL-DESIGN.md | tail -75; nl -ba $d/BLIND5-RESULT.md; nl -ba $d/judging-v13s.txt'","aggregated_output":"   436\t    $0 writer test, then Alex for the paid re-send.\n   437\t  - A split: Alex's words do not yet fix the reading. The split answers go back to the council, and to Alex as boundary cases.\n   438\t  - A failed fixed reading goes back to the council.\n   439\t  - A clean re-read does not turn D745 into a pass.\n   440\t\n   441\t**[D749] The $0 writer test of the E writer repair (Alex: \"批准修写手再小测\"; council round 63), registered before any writer is\n   442\tsent.**\n   443\t- **The request change** (`judging-v13s.txt`; the D745 text is kept in `judging-v13s-d745.txt`; the registered file's sha256 is\n   444\t  `ac707fa9…`, checked by `blind5.py build` against every built system text). This is the wording after council round 64\n   445\t  (`codex-r64.md`).\n   446\t  - The last bullet's two D745 sentences are replaced by: \"Before sizing a share, deduction, discount or pace that decides the band,\n   447\t    explain which band the evidence favours. Show support for the size or a justifying bound. If the passages lack the quantity asked,\n   448\t    explain why a related figure fits the companies, period and quantity, and why your assumptions favour that band. If you can say\n   449\t    only that the outcome might fall short or overshoot, not which band is more likely, answer unresolved and name what is unknown.\"\n   450\t  - Bullet 3's \"is an input to be discounted for time and execution (…), not an answer\" becomes \"is an input, not an answer: weigh\n   451\t    time and execution (…) under the evidence requirement below\". Council 64 found that the old imperative invited the old order\n   452\t    (discount first) on E.\n   453\t  - Sources:\n   454\t    - D745: show the support (council 64: \"give its size\" was narrower than D745, so \"show support for the size\");\n   455\t    - D747: an explained related figure counts, only when the passages lack the quantity asked (council 64: the first wording was\n   456\t      wider);\n   457\t    - D749: decide the band before sizing, else unresolved, naming the unknown;\n   458\t    - D745's ruling: a justified unresolved answer is usable.\n   459\t  - Size: 4,540 → 4,794 characters, 910 → 961 tokens (o200k_base). The writer's request now carries the same routes the readers judge\n   460\t    by, and the decision order Alex approved.\n   461\t  - Checks at $0: cases 111 of 111, rehearsal 19 of 19, and the pinned v13 plan unchanged (`paid-plan.sha256`). The schema and the code\n   462\t    are unchanged.\n   463\t- **Scope** (`blind5.py`; run folder `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/`):\n   464\t  - cases S, E and P-v8, built from the repaired request;\n   465\t  - one fresh Claude subagent and one fresh read-only Codex session per case, 6 writers, each reading only `system.txt`, `user.txt` and\n   466\t    `schema.json`.\n   467\t- **Readers.** 8 blinded packets, shuffled X01–X08, with the key in `reader-key.json`:\n   468\t  - the 6 fresh replies;\n   469\t  - 2 controls carried unchanged from the targeted test, each shown with the request it was written for;\n   470\t  - each packet read by one fresh Claude and one fresh Codex reader under the D747 definitions.\n   471\t\n   472\t  Where they disagree, on support or on any defect, two adjudicators read the packet, one per family. The reasons behind every\n   473\t  settled vote are recorded by Claude as compatible or not (`reasons.json`). Matching votes with incompatible reasons go to\n   474\t  adjudication.\n   475\t\n   476\t  | Control | Built from | Expected reading |\n   477\t  |---|---|---|\n   478\t  | K-invented-cut | blind4 U05, E Codex: 90% of $40B with no order size or shipment pace | not supported |\n   479\t  | K-impossible-floor | blind4 U02, S Claude: \"more than $730B\" called a floor above its own $720B total | \"part above or added into its whole\" on F12, settled; its support is not scored |\n   480\t- **Pass:**\n   481\t  - every fresh S and E reply has a settled yes, on its forecast or on its unresolved reason;\n   482\t  - both fresh P-v8 replies have a settled yes on a forecast;\n   483\t  - every fresh reply is accepted and publishable by the code;\n   484\t  - no settled defect on a fresh reply;\n   485\t  - both controls read as expected.\n   486\t- **Otherwise (tally rules after council 64):**\n   487\t  - **Fail:**\n   488\t    - a fresh reply refused or held by the code (decided before any reading; an unreadable reply builds no packet);\n   489\t    - a fresh P-v8 reply that is not a forecast (decided from its shape alone);\n   490\t    - a settled no on a fresh reply;\n   491\t    - a settled defect on a fresh reply;\n   492\t    - K-invented-cut not settled no;\n   493\t    - the floor defect marked by neither reader, or rejected by both adjudicators.\n   494\t  - **Inconclusive:** a support split, a defect split on a fresh reply, or a floor split left after adjudication.\n   495\t  - **The floor control** is matched on its type and on naming F12, at both stages, so readers who list the other figures differently\n   496\t    still agree. Adjudicator votes are validated like first votes.\n   497\t  - Either outcome goes back to the council, and the paid re-send stays on hold.\n   498\t  - Only a clean pass supports asking Alex to authorize the paid re-send.\n   499\t- **Checked on a scratch copy before the send** (the targeted test's six replies stood in as fresh replies):\n   500\t  - a clean set passes, with the floor marked by readers who listed its figures differently;\n   501\t  - a fresh defect marked by one reader waits for adjudication;\n   502\t  - a fresh defect still split after adjudication is inconclusive;\n   503\t  - the invented-cut control read as supported fails;\n   504\t  - the floor missed by both readers fails;\n   505\t  - a fresh P-v8 answered unresolved fails;\n   506\t  - a fresh reply that is not JSON fails before any reading.\n   507\t- **Known limits, disclosed now:**\n   508\t  - Readers split on boundary answers (D748). A fresh answer on a boundary can leave the run inconclusive even when the repair works.\n   509\t  - All four fresh E and S replies may pass as justified \"unresolved\". A pass therefore shows the repair stops invented sizes. It\n   510\t    does not show how often the writer withholds on boundary cases (council 64).\n     1\t# The D749 writer test of the E writer repair: result\n     2\t\n     3\tRun folder: `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/`. It was registered in `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D749], with council round 64's\n     4\tchanges, and scored by `blind5.py tally` (`TALLY.json`, `reasons.json`). The request is `judging-v13s.txt`, sha256 `ac707fa9…`.\n     5\t\n     6\t## 1. Verdict: INCONCLUSIVE (one support split); no line failed\n     7\t\n     8\t| Packet | Answer | Shape, rung | First readers (Codex, Claude) | Adjudicators (Codex, Claude) | Settled | Line |\n     9\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    10\t| X06 | fresh E Codex | unresolved | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    11\t| X05 | fresh E Claude | forecast, rung 2 ($42B: 2.1x, 100% Vera) | no, yes | no, yes | split | inconclusive |\n    12\t| X02 | fresh S Codex | forecast, rung 4 ($730B estimate from the same four companies' AI-infrastructure plan) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    13\t| X08 | fresh S Claude | forecast, rung 4 ($740B × 96% = $710.4B) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    14\t| X01 | fresh P-v8 Claude | forecast, rung 2 ($383.9B) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    15\t| X04 | fresh P-v8 Codex | forecast, rung 2 ($380.8B) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    16\t| X03 | control: invented 10% cut (blind4 U05) | forecast | no, no | (none) | no | ok |\n    17\t| X07 | control: impossible floor (blind4 U02) | forecast | floor marked by Codex only | floor upheld by both | floor settled | ok |\n    18\t\n    19\t- **The code** accepted all six fresh replies, and all six are publishable.\n    20\t- **No defect** was marked on a fresh reply.\n    21\t- **The reasons** behind the six settled pairs were read by Claude and recorded as compatible (`reasons.json`).\n    22\t\n    23\t## 2. What changed against the targeted test (D745), same cases\n    24\t\n    25\t- **E.** The Codex writer no longer invents a cut. It answers unresolved and names the gap: both the FY2027 base and the FY2028\n    26\t  increase are projections, and no passage gives a shipment run-rate, an order with units and prices, or a deployment schedule. Both\n    27\t  readers accept it. In the targeted test, the same writer type cut the reference by 10% and was settled not supported.\n    28\t- **S.** Both fresh forecasts are supported by both families. In the targeted test both split.\n    29\t- **P-v8.** Both fresh replies are supported forecasts, as before. The repair did not cause withholding on the supported case.\n    30\t- **The controls.** Both read as expected.\n    31\t\n    32\t## 3. The one split, read against its passages (Claude's own trace reading)\n    33\t\n    34\t- **The answer.** E's Claude writer takes management's \"more than double\" in full, at 2.1x on $20B with Vera as 100%, giving $42B,\n    35\t  rung 2. It argues the FY2027 base \"is reaffirmed for the current year, so the CPU line is already selling at volume rather than a\n    36\t  product with no shipments\".\n    37\t- **The passages it saw (15).** None shows Vera shipments, reported Vera revenue or orders:\n    38\t  - e4689: \"will be deployed\";\n    39\t  - e5044: Anthropic \"is evaluating\";\n    40\t  - e4856: the $20B is a \"potential\";\n    41\t  - e3660: management \"still expects\" $20B, which is an outlook;\n    42\t  - the AMD benchmarks (e5399–e5404) show only that a Vera platform exists to be tested.\n    43\t- **The request's own rule** (bullet 2) says a target for a product with no shipments yet \"is taken in full only when the evidence\n    44\t  shows the volume in hand\".\n    45\t- **On the passages, the premise that decides this answer is not shown.**\n    46\t  - The Codex reader and the Codex adjudicator say so.\n    47\t  - The Claude adjudicator accepted the reaffirmed outlook and the benchmarked platform as enough.\n    48\t  - This is a question of fact against an existing rule, not a gap in Alex's D747 words. Where the families differ is whether a\n    49\t    reaffirmed outlook counts as \"volume in hand\".\n    50\t\n    51\t## 4. A trace note the readers did not mark\n    52\t\n    53\t- **What the answer says.** S's Claude reply (X08) says data-center capex \"contains all AI capex plus non-AI cloud\n    54\t  capacity\". With e1947's more than $730B of AI infrastructure, that implies data-center capex above $730B, while its own outcome is\n    55\t  $710.4B.\n    56\t- **What it means.** The tension runs in the conservative direction and does not move the band (both figures are in rung 4).\n    57\t  Neither reader marked it. It is disclosed, not scored.\n    58\t\n    59\t## 5. Not done\n    60\t\n    61\tNothing was paid. Nothing was tuned on the 36 stored answers. The request was not changed after the registration.\n     1\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n     2\t\n     3\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n     4\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted subject to the evidence requirement below; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n     5\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input, not an answer: weigh time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say) under the evidence requirement below; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n     6\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n     7\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n     8\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n     9\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. Before sizing a share, deduction, discount or pace that decides the band, explain which band the evidence favours. Show support for the size or a justifying bound. If the passages lack the quantity asked, explain why a related figure fits the companies, period and quantity, and why your assumptions favour that band. If you can say only that the outcome might fall short or overshoot, not which band is more likely, answer unresolved and name what is unknown. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23; nl -ba $d/E/user.txt; for f in $d/{E,S,P-v8}/answers/*.json; do echo $f; nl -ba $f; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23; nl -ba $d/E/user.txt; for f in $d/{E,S,P-v8}/answers/*.json; do echo $f; nl -ba $f; done'","aggregated_output":"     1\t[Family] members:\n     2\tThesis v41 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA Vera CPU revenue\", FY2028, due 2028-02-29): What will NVIDIA report as Vera CPU revenue for FY2028?\n     3\tLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $50 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $40 billion and less than $50 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is less than $20 billion.\n     4\t\n     5\tRelations: none.\n     6\tShared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: none.\n     7\tOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v41 = NVIDIA | revenue | Vera CPU | FY2028.\n     8\t\n     9\t[Evidence in view for the family, 15 items]\n    10\t- e3660 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com, reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:fd25a8ea6f60290264bfbe55 2026-08-30] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double that amount.\n    11\t    Quote: The bad news for AMD and Intel is that Kress said Nvidia not only still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027, but expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028.\n    12\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3302, e4691, e4856; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    13\t- e4687 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source AMD ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] AMD estimates that server CPU market revenue could grow at a 50% annual rate through 2030, reaching a $220 billion opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    14\t    Quote: AMD notes that the server CPU market's revenue could increase at an annual rate of 50% through 2030, creating a $220 billion revenue opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    15\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4084; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    16\t- e4689 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), quoting Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera CPU deployments would include every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\n    17\t    Quote: Kress added that the Vera CPU will be deployed by \"every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\"\n    18\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2769; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    19\t- e4691 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA expects Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 to be double its FY2027 level.\n    20\t    Quote: What's more, Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year.\n    21\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660, e4764; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    22\t- e4692 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst), citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] Tom's Hardware reported that Arm-based servers represented 45% of data-center market revenue share in June.\n    23\t    Quote: Tom's Hardware noted in June that Arm-based servers account for 45% of the data center market's revenue share.\n    24\t- e4764 [party_statement; fool.com (Keith Noonan); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:0ba93692dc5df4b999fe3729 2026-09-11] Jensen Huang forecast that NVIDIA CPU revenue would more than double in FY2028.\n    25\t    Quote: Huang expects CPU revenue to more than double next fiscal year\n    26\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4691; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    27\t- e4856 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] NVIDIA management said Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 could be more than double the $20 billion potential it cited for FY2027.\n    28\t    Quote: with the potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028.\n    29\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    30\t- e4857 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Bank of America analysts ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] Bank of America analysts forecast that the CPU market would grow fivefold, from $35 billion in 2026 to $170 billion in 2030.\n    31\t    Quote: Analysts at Bank of America also released a research report in June, saying they believe the CPU market will grow fivefold between this year and 2030, from $35 billion to $170 billion.\n    32\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e935; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    33\t- e5044 [party_statement; Insider Monkey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a1af1bb8840fa4ec9e52bcb0 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said Anthropic is evaluating NVIDIA’s Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads.\n    34\t    Quote: Nvidia recently said Anthropic is evaluating its Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads,\n    35\t- e5399 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs delivered 20% higher per-core performance than NVIDIA’s 88-core Vera CPU in SPEC 2026.\n    36\t    Quote: Here, the AMD 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs provide a 20% uplift over NVIDIA's 88-Core Vera CPU.\n    37\t- e5400 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9996 delivered 1.2 times Vera-based platform per-core performance and 2.24 times its platform-level performance in SPECrate 2026 Integer testing.\n    38\t    Quote: In SPECrate 2026 Integer testing, the AMD EPYC 9996 server CPU delivers 1.2 times the per-core performance of an Nvidia Vera-based platform and 2.24 times its platform-level performance.\n    39\t- e5401 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD estimated that an EPYC 9996 system in a modeled 100-kilowatt rack would deliver 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n    40\t    Quote: In a modeled 100-kilowatt rack, it delivers an estimated 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n    41\t- e5402 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores had a 37% performance gain over NVIDIA Vera, while its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores had a 2.37-times gain in the overall SPEC 2026 suite.\n    42\t    Quote: Here, the 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores provide a 37% gain over NVIDIA Vera & the 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores provides a 2.37x gain.\n    43\t- e5403 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9006 server CPUs averaged 12% higher per-core performance in Agentic AI-specific benchmarks, with a4 subtests averaging 20%.\n    44\t    Quote: Moving over to Agentic AI-specific benchmark tests, AMD's EPYC 9006 Server CPUs drive a 12% gain in per-core performance benchmarks, with the a4 subtests' performance averaging at 20%.\n    45\t- e5404 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that EPYC Venice had 18% faster memory bandwidth than NVIDIA Vera, with 1.6 TB/s versus Vera’s 1.2 TB/s, in STREAM Triad testing.\n    46\t    Quote: AMD's EPYC Venice delivered 18% faster memory bandwidth versus NVIDIA's Vera thanks to its 16-channel memory design, which supports up to 12,800 MT/s RDIMM modules. This provides the platform with 1.6 TB/s of memory bandwidth versus 1.2 TB/s bandwidth on the NVIDIA Vera CPUs (using LPDDR5x).\n    47\t\n    48\t[Themes in force, 10]\n    49\t- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities formally authorize purchases of Nvidia H200 chips by Chinese AI companies by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-17; reasons: The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus, company guidance, or stated deadline for Chinese authorization is supplied, so the status-quo reference is no authorization by January 31, 2027. The evidence now makes rung 1 more likely: reports describe Chinese approval of H200 purchases for selec\n    50\t- v18 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the Remote Access Security Act be enacted by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: The reference is the latest supplied legislative status: the Act passed the House but had not passed the Senate as of August 19, 2026, placing the reference at rung 2 (No); no market-consensus estimate is supplied. With about six weeks remaining at the due date, the implied outcome remains non-enact\n    51\t- v25 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Taiwan adopt restrictions on AI chip sales to China by December 31, 2026?: now rung 1 No; judged 2026-08-25; reasons: The reference is Taiwan's stated current policy status: no Taiwan-specific restrictions on AI chip sales to China, which is rung 1; no separate market consensus is supplied. The investigation and reports that officials are considering a criminal ban, followed by indictments and seizure of additional\n    52\t- v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Taiwan adopt performance-threshold controls on exports of advanced AI chips by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: No market consensus or formal target is supplied; the reference is the status-quo baseline of no adopted controls, rung 2. Consultations and active consideration make adoption more plausible than before, but the Ministry confirmed that no timeline is set. With the deadline only five months away, the\n    53\t- v32 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the Commerce Department announce or adopt restrictions on Chinese AI developers' access to advanced chips through overseas data centers by December 31, 2026?: now rung 1 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: The reference is the status quo—no stated market consensus or official timetable—and sits at rung 1. The active review and the reported use of overseas data centers make restrictions more plausible, but the evidence still describes examination and alleged access, not a proposed rule or a dated commi\n    54\t- v40 (ai-capex, due 2026-10-05) Will the relevant regulator approve CME Group’s planned futures contracts tied to H100 and B200 hourly rental costs by October 5, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-29; reasons: The stated plan points to rung 1: CME intends to launch on October 5, conditional on approval. But the evidence in force makes approval by that date less likely than not: the CFTC is still seeking comment on whether the market is sufficiently standardized and transparent, and that comment period run\n    55\t- v49 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-02-28) How much will global semiconductor sales total in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 High; judged 2026-09-16; reasons: The market reference is the WSTS and Gartner forecasts, both of which place 2026 sales in rung 4. Their quantified forecasts imply $1.51–$1.56 trillion, within rung 4's $1.50 trillion to below $1.60 trillion range; neither crosses a rung boundary. IDC's 52.8% growth forecast lacks a sales base, so i\n    56\t- v6 (ai-capex, due 2027-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 Upper-middle range; judged 2026-09-19; reasons: The market reference is roughly $720–$745 billion for the four hyperscalers, which sits in rung 4 (e5014). The evidence still implies about $750 billion: the four-company tracking estimate near $745 billion and the separate top-four estimate of $760 billion bracket that level; company capex revision\n    57\t- v7 (ai-capex, due 2028-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 4 Upper-middle range; judged 2026-09-19; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is about $1.08 trillion for 2027 hyperscaler capex, which is rung 3. My implied estimate is just above the $1.1 trillion rung-4 boundary: NVIDIA's $1.3 trillion top-five forecast supports a higher spending path, while the four-company, data-center-only scope requires a discount;\n    58\t- v9 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Congress enact legislation phasing out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-07; reasons: The reference is No (rung 2): no market consensus or enacted legislation is supplied, and the stated deadline remains the test. The House committee’s proposal is a concrete step toward enactment, but it is not enactment by Congress; with only this evidence, it raises the chance of Yes without making\n    59\t\n    60\tAnswer per the JSON schema: one calculation for the family and one answer per member.poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json\n     1\t{\"figures\":[{\"id\":\"F1\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2027\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":20,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e3660\",\"quote_number\":\"$20 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F2\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue multiple\",\"scope\":\"CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":2,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"x\",\"evidence_id\":\"e3660\",\"quote_number\":\"double\",\"qualifier\":\"more than\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F3\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":40,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F1\",\"F2\"],\"operation\":\"product\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[\"v41\"]},{\"id\":\"F4\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue multiple\",\"scope\":\"CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":2.1,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"x\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":\"My forecast pace for FY2028 CPU revenue over FY2027. The benchmark is NVIDIA's guidance that FY2028 CPU revenue will more than double (F2, repeated by the CEO), which sets a floor of 2.0x. The base is reaffirmed for the current year, so the line is already selling; stated deployment at every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM and Anthropic's evaluation of Vera for agentic work support a small overshoot. AMD's benchmark claims against Vera trim the upside, so the pace stays well short of the 2.5x that $50 billion would need.\",\"rests_on\":[\"F2\",\"e3660\",\"e4764\",\"e4689\",\"e5044\",\"e5400\",\"e5401\",\"e5402\"],\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F5\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":42,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F1\",\"F4\"],\"operation\":\"product\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F6\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue share\",\"scope\":\"Vera CPU within CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":100,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"%\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":\"NVIDIA describes the FY2028 doubling of its CPU revenue as Vera CPU revenue, doubling the $20 billion it cited for FY2027 (e4691, e4856). No passage sizes any other NVIDIA CPU line for FY2028, so I take Vera as the whole of FY2028 CPU revenue.\",\"rests_on\":[\"e4691\",\"e4856\",\"F5\"],\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F7\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"Vera CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":42,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F5\",\"F6\"],\"operation\":\"share\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[\"v41\"],\"reference_for\":[]}],\"answers\":[{\"view_id\":\"v41\",\"shape\":\"forecast\",\"rung\":2,\"reference_unavailable\":null,\"reasons\":\"Reference: NVIDIA's guidance. The CFO said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 to more than double it (e3660); the CEO and management repeat the same claim (e4764, e4856), and e4691 and e4856 state it as Vera CPU revenue. Two times $20 billion is $40 billion (F3), and 'more than double' puts the reference just inside rung 2 ($40 billion to under $50 billion). Which band the evidence favours: rung 2. The FY2027 base is reaffirmed for the current year, so the CPU line is already selling at volume rather than a product with no shipments; the FY2028 growth rests on stated deployment by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM (e4689) and new demand for CPU-heavy agentic work (Anthropic is evaluating Vera, e5044). Against it, AMD's benchmark claims (1.2x per-core and 2.24x platform performance, e5400; 3.4x throughput in a modeled rack, e5401; 37% and 2.37x suite gains, e5402) show a competitor attacking Vera, but they are AMD's own performance measurements and name no lost customer or volume, so they trim the upside rather than cut the year below the guided doubling. I forecast a pace of 2.1x (my estimate, F4): the guided floor of 2.0x plus a small overshoot, well short of the 2.5x that $50 billion would need. 2.1 x $20 billion = $42 billion of CPU revenue (F5). NVIDIA describes the FY2028 figure as Vera CPU revenue and no passage sizes another CPU line for FY2028, so Vera is taken as all of it (F6), giving $42 billion (F7), rung 2. The band holds for any pace from 2.0x to just under 2.5x; it would fall to rung 3 only if NVIDIA missed its own doubling or another CPU line took part of FY2028 CPU revenue, and the evidence shows neither. The CPU market forecasts (Bank of America: $35 billion in 2026 to $170 billion in 2030, e4857; AMD: 50% annual growth to a $220 billion opportunity, e4687) describe a fast-growing market but do not reconcile with NVIDIA's own figure (NVIDIA's $20 billion would already be 57% of Bank of America's 2026 market), so they do not size NVIDIA's revenue and move nothing; the Arm server share (e4692) is a past share with no NVIDIA figure. Themes: the 2027 hyperscaler capex theme (v7, updated, above the $1.1 trillion boundary and above the market's reference) and the 2026 capex theme (v6, upper-middle) support the spending behind the Vera platform and push toward higher rungs.\",\"why\":null,\"cites\":[\"e3660\",\"e4691\",\"e4764\",\"e4856\",\"e4689\",\"e5044\",\"e5400\",\"e5401\",\"e5402\",\"e4857\",\"e4687\"],\"pushes\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e3660\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4691\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4764\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4856\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4689\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5044\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4687\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4857\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4692\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5399\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5400\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5401\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5402\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5403\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5404\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"}],\"theme_pushes\":[{\"view_id\":\"v7\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"view_id\":\"v6\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"}]}]}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"figures\": [\n     3\t    {\n     4\t      \"id\": \"F1\",\n     5\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2027\"},\n     6\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n     7\t      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n     8\t      \"value\": 20,\n     9\t      \"low\": null,\n    10\t      \"high\": null,\n    11\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n    12\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e3660\",\n    13\t      \"quote_number\": \"$20 billion\",\n    14\t      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n    15\t      \"inputs\": null,\n    16\t      \"operation\": null,\n    17\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n    18\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n    19\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n    20\t      \"reference_for\": []\n    21\t    },\n    22\t    {\n    23\t      \"id\": \"F2\",\n    24\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue multiple\", \"scope\": \"CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028 relative to FY2027\"},\n    25\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n    26\t      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n    27\t      \"value\": 2,\n    28\t      \"low\": null,\n    29\t      \"high\": null,\n    30\t      \"unit\": \"x\",\n    31\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e3660\",\n    32\t      \"quote_number\": \"double\",\n    33\t      \"qualifier\": \"more than\",\n    34\t      \"inputs\": null,\n    35\t      \"operation\": null,\n    36\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n    37\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n    38\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n    39\t      \"reference_for\": []\n    40\t    },\n    41\t    {\n    42\t      \"id\": \"F3\",\n    43\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"CPU, threshold management expects to exceed\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"},\n    44\t      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n    45\t      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n    46\t      \"value\": 40,\n    47\t      \"low\": null,\n    48\t      \"high\": null,\n    49\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n    50\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n    51\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n    52\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n    53\t      \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F2\"],\n    54\t      \"operation\": \"product\",\n    55\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n    56\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n    57\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n    58\t      \"reference_for\": []\n    59\t    }\n    60\t  ],\n    61\t  \"answers\": [\n    62\t    {\n    63\t      \"view_id\": \"v41\",\n    64\t      \"shape\": \"unresolved\",\n    65\t      \"rung\": null,\n    66\t      \"reference_unavailable\": null,\n    67\t      \"reasons\": \"Management's Vera-specific outlook points to $40 billion, the lower edge of rung 2. The CFO expects CPU revenue to more than double from a projected $20 billion base. The Vera-specific accounts describe the same outlook as doubling or potentially more than doubling. They give no amount above $40 billion. NVIDIA expects broad deployment, and Anthropic's evaluation identifies a customer and a use for Vera. Strong hyperscaler spending, particularly in 2027, supports demand during the selling period. AMD's agentic-workload tests identify a reason that same customer might choose EPYC. Neither the evaluation nor the tests disclose a purchasing decision. Industry growth forecasts and Arm's server revenue share cannot tell us how many Vera CPUs customers will buy.\",\n    68\t      \"why\": \"Both the FY2027 base and the FY2028 increase are projections. No passage gives a Vera shipment run rate, an order with units and pricing, or a customer deployment schedule from which to estimate FY2028 sales. A modest shortfall from doubling the projected base could put Vera below $40 billion, while delivery on the plan could put it above. The evidence does not favor a particular revenue band strongly enough to size that difference.\",\n    69\t      \"cites\": [\"e3660\", \"e4691\", \"e4856\", \"e4689\", \"e5044\", \"e5403\", \"e4687\", \"e4857\", \"e4692\"],\n    70\t      \"pushes\": [\n    71\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e3660\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n    72\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4687\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n    73\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4689\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n    74\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4691\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n    75\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4692\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n    76\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4764\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n    77\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4856\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n    78\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4857\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n    79\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5044\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n    80\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5399\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n    81\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5400\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n    82\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5401\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n    83\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5402\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n    84\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5403\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n    85\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5404\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}\n    86\t      ],\n    87\t      \"theme_pushes\": [\n    88\t        {\"view_id\": \"v6\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n    89\t        {\"view_id\": \"v7\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}\n    90\t      ]\n    91\t    }\n    92\t  ]\n    93\t}poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json\n     1\t{\"figures\":[{\"id\":\"F1\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Alphabet\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"total company\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":null,\"low\":195,\"high\":205,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e1254\",\"quote_number\":\"$195 billion to $205 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F2\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Meta Platforms\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"total company\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":null,\"low\":125,\"high\":145,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e1549\",\"quote_number\":\"$125 billion to $145 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F3\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Amazon\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"total company\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":220,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e1649\",\"quote_number\":\"$220 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F4\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Microsoft\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"total company\",\"period\":\"CY2026 Q1\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"reported\",\"value\":30.88,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e922\",\"quote_number\":\"$30.88 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F5\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Alphabet, Meta Platforms and Amazon\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":null,\"low\":540,\"high\":570,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F1\",\"F2\",\"F3\"],\"operation\":\"sum\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F6\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta Platforms\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":null,\"low\":720,\"high\":745,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e5014\",\"quote_number\":\"$720 billion to $745 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"roughly\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[\"v6\"]},{\"id\":\"F7\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Microsoft\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"total company\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":null,\"low\":150,\"high\":205,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F6\",\"F5\"],\"operation\":\"difference\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F8\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Microsoft\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"total company\",\"period\":\"CY2026 Q2-Q4\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":null,\"low\":119.12,\"high\":174.12,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F7\",\"F4\"],\"operation\":\"difference\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F9\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"four largest hyperscalers\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":745,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e1856\",\"quote_number\":\"$745 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"near\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F10\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"top four hyperscalers\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":760,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e2458\",\"quote_number\":\"$760 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F11\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft and Alphabet\",\"measure\":\"AI infrastructure spending\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":730,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e1947\",\"quote_number\":\"$730 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"more than\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F12\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"tech giants\",\"measure\":\"data-center spending\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":750,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e1416\",\"quote_number\":\"$750 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"estimated\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F13\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"top 5 hyperscalers\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":800,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e2759\",\"quote_number\":\"$800 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"nearly\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F14\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta and Oracle\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":795,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e5055\",\"quote_number\":\"$795 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"roughly\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F15\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Meta Platforms\",\"measure\":\"AI capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"total company\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":130,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e3686\",\"quote_number\":\"$130 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F16\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta Platforms\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":740,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":\"My forecast of the four companies' total 2026 capital expenditure, inside the guided $720-745bn range (F6) and a little below the latest trackers (F9, F10). Upward: every guidance update this year was a raise (Alphabet, Meta, Amazon), Amazon's raise came from memory prices that are still rising, and NVIDIA's nearly $800bn for the top five (F13) agrees with BofA's roughly $795bn for these four plus Oracle (F14). Downward: the aggregate implies Microsoft at $150-205bn (F7), which needs $119-174bn over the last three quarters (F8) against $30.88bn in the first (F4), and blocked or delayed data-center projects can slip some spending into 2027. Fourth-quarter spending is largely committed, so the year lands near the guided range rather than far from it.\",\"rests_on\":[\"F4\",\"F5\",\"F6\",\"F7\",\"F8\",\"F9\",\"F10\",\"F11\",\"F13\",\"F14\",\"e1254\",\"e1549\",\"e1649\",\"e4567\",\"e4566\",\"e122\"],\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F17\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta Platforms\",\"measure\":\"data-center share of capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":96,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"%\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":\"Share of the four companies' total capital expenditure that goes to data centers (servers, chips, networking, buildings, power). Data-center capex contains all AI capex plus non-AI cloud capacity. Meta's AI capex alone is $130bn (F15) against the $135bn midpoint of its $125-145bn guidance (F2), about 96% before non-AI data centers; Amazon's raise was mostly server memory cost, i.e. data-center spending; the companies' indicated AI-infrastructure spending exceeds $730bn (F11) and an outside estimate puts the tech giants' data-center spending at $750bn (F12), both close to the whole total. The non-data-center remainder (chiefly Amazon's logistics, offices and devices) is not sized in the evidence; I take it at about 4%.\",\"rests_on\":[\"F2\",\"F11\",\"F12\",\"F15\",\"e1649\",\"e4567\"],\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F18\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"four largest AI hyperscalers\",\"measure\":\"data-center capex\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":710.4,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F16\",\"F17\"],\"operation\":\"share\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[\"v6\"],\"reference_for\":[]}],\"answers\":[{\"view_id\":\"v6\",\"shape\":\"forecast\",\"rung\":4,\"reference_unavailable\":null,\"reasons\":\"Reference: the market expects the four companies' combined 2026 capital expenditure at roughly $720-745bn (F6, Zacks; the same aggregated guidance appears in e2079 and, as more than $730bn of AI infrastructure, in e1947). That range sits in rung 4 ($700-800bn). It is total capital expenditure, so the data-center outcome sits at or slightly below it.\\n\\nBuild of the total: Alphabet guides $195-205bn (raised in July), Meta $125-145bn (raised) and Amazon $220bn (raised, mostly on memory-chip costs); all three report on the calendar year, so together $540-570bn (F5). The aggregate therefore implies Microsoft at $150-205bn for CY2026 (F7). Microsoft's fiscal Q3 2026 (January-March 2026) capex was $30.88bn, up 84% (F4), so the implied range needs $119-174bn over the remaining three quarters (F8): continued quarterly step-ups, consistent with an 84% growth pace but the main downside risk to the total. Upward pressure: every guidance update this year has been a raise, the memory prices behind Amazon's raise are still rising (e4567, e4566), and later trackers sit near $745bn (F9) and at $760bn (F10); NVIDIA's nearly $800bn for the top five (F13) agrees with BofA's roughly $795bn for these four plus Oracle (F14). Downward pressure: Microsoft's first-quarter run-rate and data-center projects blocked or delayed (e122) that can push some spending into 2027. I forecast the four's total capital expenditure at $740bn (F16), inside the guided range and a little below the latest trackers.\\n\\nWhich band the evidence favours before the deduction: rung 4. Every figure for this population labelled as AI-infrastructure or data-center spending sits in rung 4: more than $730bn of AI infrastructure indicated by the companies (F11) and an estimated $750bn of data-center spending by the tech giants (F12; e4082 also gives about $750bn). Rung 3 would need the non-data-center part of a $740bn total to exceed about $40bn, above 5% of the total.\\n\\nDeduction: data-center capital expenditure contains all AI capex plus non-AI cloud capacity. Meta alone plans $130bn of AI capex (F15) against the $135bn midpoint of its guidance (F2), about 96% before counting non-AI data centers; Amazon's raise came from server memory costs, which is data-center spending; the cloud businesses that use the capacity are accelerating (e4404, e4405, e4406), so the spending is not being redirected. The non-data-center remainder (chiefly Amazon's logistics, offices and devices) is not sized in the evidence; I take it at about 4% (F17, 96%), below the roughly 5% that would cross into rung 3.\\n\\nOutcome: $740bn x 96% = about $710bn (F18), rung 4. The margin over the $700bn boundary is thin: rung 3 is the main alternative if the total lands near the bottom of the guided range together with a non-data-center share above about 3%, or if Microsoft's spending stays close to its first-quarter pace. Rung 5 would need a total above about $830bn, which no figure for these four supports (the nearly $800bn figures include a fifth company).\",\"why\":null,\"cites\":[\"e1254\",\"e1549\",\"e1649\",\"e4567\",\"e922\",\"e5014\",\"e2079\",\"e1947\",\"e1856\",\"e2458\",\"e1416\",\"e4082\",\"e2759\",\"e5055\",\"e3686\",\"e4566\",\"e122\",\"e4404\",\"e4405\",\"e4406\"],\"pushes\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1254\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e1549\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e1649\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4567\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e922\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5014\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2079\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e1947\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e1856\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2458\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e1416\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4082\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2759\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5055\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3686\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4566\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e122\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4404\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4405\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4406\",\"push\":\"none\"}],\"theme_pushes\":[]}]}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"figures\": [\n     3\t    {\n     4\t      \"id\": \"F1\",\n     5\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta\", \"measure\": \"AI infrastructure spending\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n     6\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n     7\t      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n     8\t      \"value\": 730,\n     9\t      \"low\": null,\n    10\t      \"high\": null,\n    11\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n    12\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e1947\",\n    13\t      \"quote_number\": \"$730 billion\",\n    14\t      \"qualifier\": \"more than\",\n    15\t      \"inputs\": null,\n    16\t      \"operation\": null,\n    17\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n    18\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n    19\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n    20\t      \"reference_for\": [\"v6\"]\n    21\t    },\n    22\t    {\n    23\t      \"id\": \"F2\",\n    24\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta\", \"measure\": \"total capital expenditures\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n    25\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n    26\t      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n    27\t      \"value\": null,\n    28\t      \"low\": 720,\n    29\t      \"high\": 745,\n    30\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n    31\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e5014\",\n    32\t      \"quote_number\": \"$720 billion to $745 billion\",\n    33\t      \"qualifier\": \"roughly\",\n    34\t      \"inputs\": null,\n    35\t      \"operation\": null,\n    36\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n    37\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n    38\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n    39\t      \"reference_for\": []\n    40\t    },\n    41\t    {\n    42\t      \"id\": \"F3\",\n    43\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"AI data-center industry\", \"measure\": \"AI data-center infrastructure spending\", \"scope\": \"industry estimate without a named company set\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n    44\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n    45\t      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n    46\t      \"value\": 750,\n    47\t      \"low\": null,\n    48\t      \"high\": null,\n    49\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n    50\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e4082\",\n    51\t      \"quote_number\": \"$750 billion\",\n    52\t      \"qualifier\": \"around\",\n    53\t      \"inputs\": null,\n    54\t      \"operation\": null,\n    55\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n    56\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n    57\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n    58\t      \"reference_for\": []\n    59\t    },\n    60\t    {\n    61\t      \"id\": \"F4\",\n    62\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"five largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center capex\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n    63\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n    64\t      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n    65\t      \"value\": 800,\n    66\t      \"low\": null,\n    67\t      \"high\": null,\n    68\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n    69\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e5376\",\n    70\t      \"quote_number\": \"$800 billion\",\n    71\t      \"qualifier\": \"nearly\",\n    72\t      \"inputs\": null,\n    73\t      \"operation\": null,\n    74\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n    75\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n    76\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n    77\t      \"reference_for\": []\n    78\t    },\n    79\t    {\n    80\t      \"id\": \"F5\",\n    81\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center capex\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n    82\t      \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n    83\t      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n    84\t      \"value\": 730,\n    85\t      \"low\": null,\n    86\t      \"high\": null,\n    87\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n    88\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n    89\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n    90\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n    91\t      \"inputs\": null,\n    92\t      \"operation\": null,\n    93\t      \"basis_text\": \"I favor the $700 billion to below $800 billion band. The four companies' AI infrastructure plan covers the right buyers and year. Amazon's memory purchases and the capacity needed to serve Azure's backlog point to servers and the facilities that house them as the main use of that money. For data-center capex to fall below $700 billion, spending outside data centers and spending deferred beyond year-end would together have to absorb more than $30 billion of the indicated plan, even before allowing for conventional cloud equipment bought for the same facilities. The project-delay tally does not identify that much spending due from these buyers this year. I use $730 billion as a rounded estimate near the stated floor of the AI plan. The four-company total capex estimate limits the likely upside, and the broader data-center estimates support the scale without supplying a precise split.\",\n    94\t      \"rests_on\": [\"F1\", \"F2\", \"F3\", \"F4\", \"e1649\", \"e4405\", \"e122\"],\n    95\t      \"outcome_for\": [\"v6\"],\n    96\t      \"reference_for\": []\n    97\t    }\n    98\t  ],\n    99\t  \"answers\": [\n   100\t    {\n   101\t      \"view_id\": \"v6\",\n   102\t      \"shape\": \"forecast\",\n   103\t      \"rung\": 4,\n   104\t      \"reference_unavailable\": null,\n   105\t      \"reasons\": \"I forecast about $730 billion. The four companies' stated AI infrastructure plan has its lower edge in rung 4. Amazon raised its budget as memory became more expensive, so buying the same hardware can now require more dollars. Microsoft's Azure backlog gives it a reason to keep buying capacity. U.S. construction delays weigh against full execution, although the tally measures whole projects across owners and years. On the upside, $800 billion would already exceed the outside estimate of these four companies' entire capital budgets. NVIDIA approaches that figure only by including a fifth buyer.\",\n   106\t      \"why\": null,\n   107\t      \"cites\": [\"e1947\", \"e1649\", \"e4405\", \"e122\", \"e5014\", \"e4082\", \"e5376\"],\n   108\t      \"pushes\": [\n   109\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1947\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   110\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1649\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   111\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4405\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   112\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e122\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n   113\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5014\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n   114\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4082\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   115\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5376\", \"push\": \"none\"}\n   116\t      ],\n   117\t      \"theme_pushes\": []\n   118\t    }\n   119\t  ]\n   120\t}poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/P-v8/answers/claude.json\n     1\t{\"figures\":[{\"id\":\"F1\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"Data Center\",\"period\":\"FY2027 Q1\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"reported\",\"value\":75.25,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e109\",\"quote_number\":\"$75.25 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F2\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"Data Center\",\"period\":\"FY2027 Q2\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"reported\",\"value\":89.0,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e2650\",\"quote_number\":\"$89.0 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F3\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"Data Center\",\"period\":\"FY2027 H1\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"reported\",\"value\":164.25,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F1\",\"F2\"],\"operation\":\"sum\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F4\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"company\",\"period\":\"FY2027 Q1\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"reported\",\"value\":81.62,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e111\",\"quote_number\":\"$81.62 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F5\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"company\",\"period\":\"FY2027 Q2\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"reported\",\"value\":96.2,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e2649\",\"quote_number\":\"$96.2 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F6\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"company\",\"period\":\"FY2027 Q3\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":108.0,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e2652\",\"quote_number\":\"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F7\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"company\",\"period\":\"FY2027 Q3\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":113.6,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e5056\",\"quote_number\":\"$113.6 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"roughly\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F8\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"company\",\"period\":\"FY2027 Q3\"},\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":112.0,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":\"My estimate: Q3 revenue lands above the $110.16B top of the $108.0B plus-or-minus 2% guidance, as NVIDIA has beaten the top of its range in each of the past 12 quarters (e5054; Q2 beat the midpoint by $5.2B, e3200; Q4 FY2026 came in at $68B against $65B guided, e2452). I take slightly less than the 3.1%-above-the-top average beat that would give about $113.6B (F7), because capacity is sold out and supply covers only about 70% of demand (e4911, e3733), which limits further upside.\",\"rests_on\":[\"F5\",\"F6\",\"F7\",\"e3346\",\"e5054\",\"e5055\",\"e3200\",\"e2452\",\"e4911\",\"e3733\"],\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F9\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue share\",\"scope\":\"Data Center\",\"period\":\"FY2027 H2\"},\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":92.5,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"%\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":\"My estimate: Data Center keeps Q2's 92.5% share of company revenue in Q3 and Q4. The share rose every quarter for five quarters, from 87.9% to 92.5% (e3399, F10), and the Vera Rubin ramp falls in Data Center (e2721), so holding the share flat is a conservative assumption.\",\"rests_on\":[\"F10\",\"e3399\",\"e2721\"],\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F10\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue share\",\"scope\":\"Data Center\",\"period\":\"FY2027 Q2\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":92.5,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"%\",\"evidence_id\":\"e3399\",\"quote_number\":\"92.5%\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F11\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"Data Center\",\"period\":\"FY2027 Q3\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":103.6,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F8\",\"F9\"],\"operation\":\"share\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F12\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue growth\",\"scope\":\"company\",\"period\":\"FY2027 Q4\"},\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":12.0,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"%\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":\"My estimate: Q4 company revenue grows 12% sequentially from the Q3 forecast, the pace NVIDIA guided for Q3 at its midpoint (F13). Hyperscale growth is expected to reaccelerate in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds (e4283, e3406), and supply commitments rose from $119B to $279B (e2759), which supports at least this pace; supply serving only about 70% of demand and constraints expected through FY2028 (e3733, e5067) argue against a sharper acceleration.\",\"rests_on\":[\"F13\",\"e4899\",\"e4283\",\"e3406\",\"e2759\",\"e3733\",\"e5067\"],\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F13\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue growth\",\"scope\":\"company\",\"period\":\"FY2027 Q3\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":12.0,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"%\",\"evidence_id\":\"e4899\",\"quote_number\":\"12%\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F14\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"company\",\"period\":\"FY2027 Q4\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":125.44,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F8\",\"F12\"],\"operation\":\"apply_growth\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F15\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"Data Center\",\"period\":\"FY2027 Q4\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":116.03,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F14\",\"F9\"],\"operation\":\"share\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F16\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"Data Center\",\"period\":\"FY2027\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":383.88,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F3\",\"F11\",\"F15\"],\"operation\":\"sum\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[\"v8\"],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F17\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"company\",\"period\":\"FY2027\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":415.26,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F4\",\"F5\",\"F8\",\"F14\"],\"operation\":\"sum\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F18\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"company\",\"period\":\"FY2027\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"consensus\",\"value\":411.0,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e4333\",\"quote_number\":\"$411 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F19\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"Data Center\",\"period\":\"FY2027\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":361.0,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e2295\",\"quote_number\":\"$361 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[\"v8\"]},{\"id\":\"F20\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"Data Center\",\"period\":\"FY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"reported\",\"value\":193.7,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e436\",\"quote_number\":\"$193.7 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F21\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue growth\",\"scope\":\"Data Center\",\"period\":\"FY2027\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":98.18,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"%\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F20\",\"F16\"],\"operation\":\"growth\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]}],\"answers\":[{\"view_id\":\"v8\",\"shape\":\"forecast\",\"rung\":2,\"reference_unavailable\":null,\"reasons\":\"Reference: the only direct market figure for this quantity is Morningstar's FY2027 Data Center estimate of $361B (F19, e2295), which sits on rung 2 ($350B to below $400B); the $411B consensus for total company revenue (F18, e4333) points to the same band given Data Center's roughly 92% mix. Build: H1 Data Center revenue is reported at $164.25B ($75.25B in Q1 plus $89.0B in Q2; F3, matching the $164B in e5309). For Q3, the $108.0B plus-or-minus 2% guidance (F6, e2652) is the strongest input; NVIDIA has beaten the top of its range in each of the last 12 quarters (e5054) and Q2 beat its midpoint by $5.2B (e3200), but capacity is sold out (e4911, e3733), so I forecast $112B (F8), a little under the $113.6B a full average beat would give (F7). Holding Q2's 92.5% Data Center share (F9; the share has risen five quarters running, e3399) gives Q3 Data Center revenue of $103.6B (F11). For Q4 I apply the 12% sequential pace NVIDIA guided for Q3 (F12, F13): hyperscale growth is expected to reaccelerate in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds (e4283, e3406) and supply commitments jumped to $279B (e2759), while supply serving only about 70% of demand and constraints through FY2028 (e3733, e5067) argue against a faster pace. That gives Q4 company revenue of $125.44B (F14) and Q4 Data Center revenue of $116.03B (F15), so FY2027 Data Center revenue of about $383.88B (F16), up about 98% on FY2026's $193.7B (F20, F21), with company revenue of about $415.26B (F17), close to the $411B consensus. Which band: the outcome sits inside rung 2 with room on both sides. Falling below $350B would require Q4 company revenue to drop below Q2's reported $96.2B even with Q3 at the bottom of its guidance range, and no evidence points to a sequential decline. Reaching $400B would require Q4 sequential growth more than twice the 12% pace guided for Q3, while NVIDIA says it is supply-constrained; SemiAnalysis's July view that second-half Data Center revenue would beat estimates by 20% (e1135) predates the Q2 report and Q3 guidance that consensus has since absorbed, and does not by itself close that gap. Items not used as inputs: the '70% full-year growth' readings (e3280, e4628, e3890) refer to FY2028 according to the CFO's own words on the call (e2691) and e5227; applied to FY2027 they would imply Q4 revenue far below the Q3 guidance, which nothing supports. The just-under-$330B run-rate (e5310) annualises H1 flat. The $83.7B Q2 Data Center figure in e4907 conflicts with NVIDIA's reported $89.0B (e2650), which I use. AMD's 6 GW deals and hyperscalers' custom chips (e1040, e1041, e4314) mostly affect deployments after FY2027 while NVIDIA is supply-limited, so they do not move FY2027. Themes: the Q3 guidance and my forecast include no China Data Center compute revenue (e2681); theme v9 judges limited Chinese authorisation of H200 purchases likely by January 31, 2027, which could add only a small amount in the last weeks of FY2027: upside within rung 2, not enough to reach $400B. Theme v7's above-reference calendar-2027 hyperscaler capex supports the Q4 hyperscale reacceleration (toward_first); theme v6's calendar-2026 capex is consistent with the guided run-rate already in the forecast (none).\",\"why\":null,\"cites\":[\"e109\",\"e2650\",\"e5309\",\"e436\",\"e111\",\"e2649\",\"e2652\",\"e3346\",\"e5054\",\"e5055\",\"e5056\",\"e3200\",\"e2452\",\"e3399\",\"e4899\",\"e2721\",\"e4283\",\"e3406\",\"e2759\",\"e3733\",\"e5067\",\"e4911\",\"e2295\",\"e4333\",\"e1135\",\"e3280\",\"e4628\",\"e3890\",\"e2691\",\"e5227\",\"e5310\",\"e4907\",\"e1040\",\"e1041\",\"e4314\",\"e2681\"],\"pushes\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e2650\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e109\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5309\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2652\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5054\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5055\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5056\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3200\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2452\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3399\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4899\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2721\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4283\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3406\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2759\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3733\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5067\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4911\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2295\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4333\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e1135\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3280\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4628\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3890\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2691\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5227\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5310\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4907\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e1040\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e1041\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4314\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2681\",\"push\":\"none\"}],\"theme_pushes\":[{\"view_id\":\"v6\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"view_id\":\"v7\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"view_id\":\"v9\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"}]}]}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/P-v8/answers/codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"figures\": [\n     3\t    {\n     4\t      \"id\": \"F1\",\n     5\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Data Center\", \"period\": \"FY2027\"},\n     6\t      \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"third_party\", \"value\": 361, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n     7\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e2295\", \"quote_number\": \"$361 billion\", \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n     8\t      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": [\"v8\"]\n     9\t    },\n    10\t    {\n    11\t      \"id\": \"F2\",\n    12\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Data Center\", \"period\": \"FY2027 H1\"},\n    13\t      \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"reported\", \"value\": 164, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n    14\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e5309\", \"quote_number\": \"$164 billion\", \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n    15\t      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []\n    16\t    },\n    17\t    {\n    18\t      \"id\": \"F3\",\n    19\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Data Center\", \"period\": \"FY2027 Q2\"},\n    20\t      \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"reported\", \"value\": 89, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n    21\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e2723\", \"quote_number\": \"$89.0 billion\", \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n    22\t      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []\n    23\t    },\n    24\t    {\n    25\t      \"id\": \"F4\",\n    26\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"company\", \"period\": \"FY2027 Q3\"},\n    27\t      \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"guidance\", \"value\": null, \"low\": 105.84, \"high\": 110.16, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n    28\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e2652\", \"quote_number\": \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\", \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n    29\t      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []\n    30\t    },\n    31\t    {\n    32\t      \"id\": \"F5\",\n    33\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"company\", \"period\": \"FY2027 Q3\"},\n    34\t      \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"third_party\", \"value\": 113.6, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n    35\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e5056\", \"quote_number\": \"$113.6 billion\", \"qualifier\": \"roughly\",\n    36\t      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []\n    37\t    },\n    38\t    {\n    39\t      \"id\": \"F6\",\n    40\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"company\", \"period\": \"FY2027 Q3\"},\n    41\t      \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 111, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n    42\t      \"evidence_id\": null, \"quote_number\": null, \"qualifier\": null, \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null,\n    43\t      \"basis_text\": \"I expect revenue just above the high end of guidance. NVIDIA has begun Rubin production shipments against customer orders and beat its previous outlook even with China excluded. The outside estimate of $113.6 billion assumes the recent average beat repeats. Memory and site constraints, together with management placing the hyperscaler acceleration in Q4, favor a smaller beat this quarter.\",\n    44\t      \"rests_on\": [\"F4\", \"F5\", \"e2693\", \"e3200\", \"e3115\", \"e4283\"], \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []\n    45\t    },\n    46\t    {\n    47\t      \"id\": \"F7\",\n    48\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"sequential revenue growth\", \"scope\": \"company\", \"period\": \"FY2027 Q3\"},\n    49\t      \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"guidance\", \"value\": 12, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"%\",\n    50\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e5314\", \"quote_number\": \"12%\", \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n    51\t      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []\n    52\t    },\n    53\t    {\n    54\t      \"id\": \"F8\",\n    55\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"sequential revenue growth\", \"scope\": \"company\", \"period\": \"FY2027 Q4\"},\n    56\t      \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 10, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"%\",\n    57\t      \"evidence_id\": null, \"quote_number\": null, \"qualifier\": null, \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null,\n    58\t      \"basis_text\": \"I expect another quarterly dollar increase comparable to the one in the Q3 outlook. Rubin supply is building, and NVIDIA expects hyperscalers to take more deliveries after enterprise, sovereign and cloud customers lead Q3 growth. A similar dollar gain on the higher Q3 base implies a slightly slower percentage pace than the guided 12%. Power and memory constraints limit how quickly the larger orders can ship.\",\n    59\t      \"rests_on\": [\"F6\", \"F7\", \"e4283\", \"e2693\", \"e3115\"], \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []\n    60\t    },\n    61\t    {\n    62\t      \"id\": \"F9\",\n    63\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"company\", \"period\": \"FY2027 Q4\"},\n    64\t      \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 122.1, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n    65\t      \"evidence_id\": null, \"quote_number\": null, \"qualifier\": null, \"inputs\": [\"F6\", \"F8\"], \"operation\": \"apply_growth\",\n    66\t      \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []\n    67\t    },\n    68\t    {\n    69\t      \"id\": \"F10\",\n    70\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"company\", \"period\": \"FY2027 H2\"},\n    71\t      \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 233.1, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n    72\t      \"evidence_id\": null, \"quote_number\": null, \"qualifier\": null, \"inputs\": [\"F6\", \"F9\"], \"operation\": \"sum\",\n    73\t      \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []\n    74\t    },\n    75\t    {\n    76\t      \"id\": \"F11\",\n    77\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"share of company revenue\", \"scope\": \"Data Center\", \"period\": \"FY2027 Q2\"},\n    78\t      \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"reported\", \"value\": 92.5, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"%\",\n    79\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e5116\", \"quote_number\": \"92.5%\", \"qualifier\": \"roughly\",\n    80\t      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []\n    81\t    },\n    82\t    {\n    83\t      \"id\": \"F12\",\n    84\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"share of company revenue\", \"scope\": \"Data Center\", \"period\": \"FY2027 H2\"},\n    85\t      \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 93, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"%\",\n    86\t      \"evidence_id\": null, \"quote_number\": null, \"qualifier\": null, \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null,\n    87\t      \"basis_text\": \"The $350 billion to below $400 billion band is favored even if the mix holds at its Q2 level. I put the second-half share slightly higher at 93% because it has risen each quarter and management identifies Data Center customers as the main source of the next sales increase. Rubin replaces some Blackwell sales within that share.\",\n    88\t      \"rests_on\": [\"F11\", \"e3399\", \"e4283\", \"e5223\"], \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []\n    89\t    },\n    90\t    {\n    91\t      \"id\": \"F13\",\n    92\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Data Center\", \"period\": \"FY2027 H2\"},\n    93\t      \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 216.783, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n    94\t      \"evidence_id\": null, \"quote_number\": null, \"qualifier\": null, \"inputs\": [\"F10\", \"F12\"], \"operation\": \"share\",\n    95\t      \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []\n    96\t    },\n    97\t    {\n    98\t      \"id\": \"F14\",\n    99\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Data Center\", \"period\": \"FY2027\"},\n   100\t      \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": 380.783, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   101\t      \"evidence_id\": null, \"quote_number\": null, \"qualifier\": null, \"inputs\": [\"F2\", \"F13\"], \"operation\": \"sum\",\n   102\t      \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [\"v8\"], \"reference_for\": []\n   103\t    }\n   104\t  ],\n   105\t  \"answers\": [\n   106\t    {\n   107\t      \"view_id\": \"v8\",\n   108\t      \"shape\": \"forecast\",\n   109\t      \"rung\": 2,\n   110\t      \"reference_unavailable\": null,\n   111\t      \"reasons\": \"I forecast about $381 billion of FY2027 Data Center revenue, in rung 2. Morningstar's $361 billion estimate is a direct full-year segment reference in the same rung. With $164 billion already reported, the segment needs another $186 billion to reach $350 billion. Q2 alone delivered $89 billion, and the Q3 company outlook points to a substantial sequential increase led by Data Center. Reaching $400 billion would require $236 billion of second-half segment sales, more than my $233.1 billion forecast for the entire company in that period. Rubin is already shipping, and the expected Q4 pickup in hyperscaler deliveries supports continued growth. The hyperscaler spending themes strengthen that demand outlook, though most calendar 2027 spending falls beyond this fiscal year. Limited H200 authorization adds some late-year upside. The time needed to supply systems and bring powered sites online makes the middle of rung 2 more likely than its upper edge.\",\n   112\t      \"why\": null,\n   113\t      \"cites\": [\"e2295\", \"e5309\", \"e2723\", \"e2652\", \"e5314\", \"e3200\", \"e5056\", \"e2693\", \"e4283\", \"e3115\", \"e5116\", \"e3399\", \"e5223\"],\n   114\t      \"pushes\": [\n   115\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e2295\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   116\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5309\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n   117\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e2723\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n   118\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e2652\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n   119\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5314\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   120\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e3200\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n   121\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5056\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n   122\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e2693\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n   123\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4283\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n   124\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e3115\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   125\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5116\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   126\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e3399\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n   127\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5223\", \"push\": \"none\"}\n   128\t      ],\n   129\t      \"theme_pushes\": [\n   130\t        {\"view_id\": \"v6\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n   131\t        {\"view_id\": \"v7\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n   132\t        {\"view_id\": \"v9\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n   133\t        {\"view_id\": \"v16\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   134\t        {\"view_id\": \"v22\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   135\t        {\"view_id\": \"v26\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   136\t        {\"view_id\": \"v27\", \"push\": \"none\"}\n   137\t      ]\n   138\t    }\n   139\t  ]\n   140\t}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
