{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23; for f in $d/{TALLY,reasons,reader-key}.json; do echo $f; nl -ba $f; done'","aggregated_output":"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/TALLY.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t \"verdict\": \"inconclusive\",\n     3\t \"fails\": [],\n     4\t \"splits\": [\n     5\t  \"X05 fresh E/claude: v41 settled=split [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer]\"\n     6\t ],\n     7\t \"lines\": [\n     8\t  \"X01 fresh P-v8/claude      v8 forecast   settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast] ok\",\n     9\t  \"X02 fresh S/codex          v6 forecast   settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] ok\",\n    10\t  \"X03 K-invented-cut         v41 forecast   settled=no     votes=['no', 'no'] [control expects not supported] ok\",\n    11\t  \"X04 fresh P-v8/codex       v8 forecast   settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast] ok\",\n    12\t  \"X05 fresh E/claude         v41 forecast   settled=split  votes=['no', 'yes', 'no', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] MISS\",\n    13\t  \"X06 fresh E/codex          v41 unresolved settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] ok\",\n    14\t  \"X07 K-impossible-floor     v6 forecast   settled=no     votes=['no', 'no'] [support not scored; the floor defect is] ok\",\n    15\t  \"X08 fresh S/claude         v6 forecast   settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] ok\"\n    16\t ],\n    17\t \"settled_defects\": [\n    18\t  [\n    19\t   \"X07\",\n    20\t   \"K-impossible-floor\",\n    21\t   \"part above or added into its whole\",\n    22\t   [\n    23\t    \"F12\"\n    24\t   ],\n    25\t   \"settled\"\n    26\t  ]\n    27\t ],\n    28\t \"split_defects\": [],\n    29\t \"floor_control\": \"settled\"\n    30\t}poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reasons.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t \"X01\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both: H1 reported, Q3 from guidance and the beat record, 92.5% share held; even flat Q4 keeps the year at or above $350B, and $400B needs about 27.6% Q4 sequential growth against the guided pace and supply limits.\"},\n     3\t \"X02\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both apply D747: e1947 covers the same four companies and year; the answer explains why most of that spending buys data-center equipment and facilities (e1649 memory, e4405 Azure backlog) and why falling below $700B would need more than $30B to go elsewhere or slip.\"},\n     4\t \"X03\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both: the 10% shortfall has no size or grounding in shipment timing; the cited passages name customers, an evaluation and a benchmark, not lost or slipped revenue.\"},\n     5\t \"X04\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both: $350B needs only $186B of second-half segment revenue, below the Q2 run-rate plus the guided step-up; $400B needs $236B, more than the forecast total company second half.\"},\n     6\t \"X06\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both: the guided figure sits on the $40B line on a projected base; no passage gives shipments, orders with units and prices, or a deployment schedule, so neither a shortfall nor an overshoot can be sized and the rung stays unsettled.\"},\n     7\t \"X07\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both no on support (not scored for this control): the 2% residual and the Microsoft uplift that decide the band are unsized, and the answer's own 96% bound gives rung 3. The floor defect is marked by one reader only and goes to adjudication.\"},\n     8\t \"X08\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both apply D747: at a $740B total up to $40B may fall outside data centers; e1947 (same four companies, same year) and Meta's AI share explain why the share is high enough. Claude's own trace note: the answer says data-center capex contains all AI capex, which with e1947 implies more than $730B, above its own $710.4B; neither reader marked it, and it does not move the band.\"}\n     9\t}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-key.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t \"X01\": {\n     3\t  \"label\": \"fresh P-v8/claude\",\n     4\t  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n     5\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n     6\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/P-v8/answers/claude.json\",\n     7\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/P-v8\"\n     8\t },\n     9\t \"X02\": {\n    10\t  \"label\": \"fresh S/codex\",\n    11\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n    12\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    13\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json\",\n    14\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/S\"\n    15\t },\n    16\t \"X03\": {\n    17\t  \"label\": \"K-invented-cut\",\n    18\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    19\t  \"writer\": \"K-invented-cut\",\n    20\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json\",\n    21\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E\"\n    22\t },\n    23\t \"X04\": {\n    24\t  \"label\": \"fresh P-v8/codex\",\n    25\t  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n    26\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    27\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/P-v8/answers/codex.json\",\n    28\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/P-v8\"\n    29\t },\n    30\t \"X05\": {\n    31\t  \"label\": \"fresh E/claude\",\n    32\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    33\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    34\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json\",\n    35\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E\"\n    36\t },\n    37\t \"X06\": {\n    38\t  \"label\": \"fresh E/codex\",\n    39\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    40\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    41\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json\",\n    42\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E\"\n    43\t },\n    44\t \"X07\": {\n    45\t  \"label\": \"K-impossible-floor\",\n    46\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n    47\t  \"writer\": \"K-impossible-floor\",\n    48\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json\",\n    49\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S\"\n    50\t },\n    51\t \"X08\": {\n    52\t  \"label\": \"fresh S/claude\",\n    53\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n    54\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    55\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json\",\n    56\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/S\"\n    57\t }\n    58\t}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; for f in $d/codex-r6{1,2,3,4}.md $d/BLIND4-RESULT.md; do echo $f; nl -ba $f; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; for f in $d/codex-r6{1,2,3,4}.md $d/BLIND4-RESULT.md; do echo $f; nl -ba $f; done'","aggregated_output":"docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r61.md\n     1\t**The FAIL is real. Ask Alex what evidence is enough to publish a forecast before requesting the paid re-send.** The decisive case is the $36B Vera forecast. Its writer identified a risk to sales, then assigned that risk a $4B cost. Neither reader found evidence for the amount. That one answer fails the registered test regardless of how the four disagreements are resolved. [E writer’s assumption](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:94) · [Recorded votes](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:8) · [Registered pass conditions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:375)\n     2\t\n     3\t1. **Serious — U05 deserves its settled “no.”** Management expected FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double a $20B base. The writer used $40B as its reference and forecast 90% realization. The deployment announcement supports demand. Anthropic’s evaluation and AMD’s benchmarks establish competitive risk. None gives the timing or volume of sales likely to slip. Calling $4B “less than half an average quarter” expresses the same assumption in another unit. It adds no evidence for it. At exactly $40B the answer enters the higher band, so the unsupported cut decides the result. **Wording correction:** the result report says four readers rejected U05. The record has two first readers, in agreement, and no adjudication. [E request and bands](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:3) · [Writer’s conclusion](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:130) · [Codex reading](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U05.codex.json:5) · [Claude reading](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U05.claude.json:1) · [Report wording](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md:41)\n     4\t\n     5\t2. **Serious — The four splits have different merits.** I read both initial readings and both adjudications for each. All eight adjudicator votes followed their own family. My readings below leave the recorded splits intact. [Vote tally](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:4)\n     6\t\n     7\t   | Reply | My reading | What decides it |\n     8\t   |---|---|---|\n     9\t   | **U02 · S forecast** | **Codex “no” on support. The range is valid.** | The writer applies 98% to $720B and gets $705.6B. Its own 96% lower share gives $691.2B. Crossing $700B is allowed: the checker actually publishes $690.9–720.3B, and the schema permits selecting an overlapping band with a reason. The weakness is the claimed independent floor. If all of the cited **more than $730B** of AI infrastructure really is data-center capex for these firms, it exceeds even the writer’s **total** spending midpoint of $720B. The writer needed to reconcile those estimates before using one to validate the other. [Writer’s reasoning](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json:390) · [Checked range](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/CHECKED.json:62) · [Schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/schema.json:334) · [Both readings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U02.md:36) · [Codex decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U02.codex.json:5) · [Claude decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U02.claude.json:1) |\n    10\t   | **U04 · S unresolved control** | **Codex “yes” is better supported.** | At $745B total, the four firms cross $700B at a data-center share of about 94%. The control names that boundary and addresses the strongest contrary passage. The $730B figure names the right four companies but measures AI infrastructure. The nearly $800B data-center figure covers five companies. Neither establishes how much of the four firms’ spending qualifies. Claude has a plausible reason to lean upward. Its claim that the first figure supplies a floor requires a relationship the passage leaves open. That makes this a defensible abstention and a poor control for an *undisputed* abstention. [Control](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/controls/K-unresolved-justified.json:1) · [Four-company passage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:29) · [Five-company passage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:128) · [Both readings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U04.md:36) · [Codex decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U04.codex.json:5) · [Claude decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U04.claude.json:1) |\n    11\t   | **U06 · E forecast** | **Codex “no.” The existing definition settles the error in Claude’s justification.** | Keep the writer’s 90% Vera share and run its proposed lower growth rate. **$20B × 1.5 × 90% = $27B**, one band below the claimed $30–40B. Claude effectively substitutes a 100% share to obtain $30B. The definition requires judging the answer with the writer’s own assumptions. Claude is right on one factual detail: e4856 does connect Vera to the $20B potential. That passage still supplies neither the writer’s realized share nor a floor for NVIDIA’s growth equal to the whole market’s. The writer’s $32.4B arithmetic is correct. The reader’s claim about the whole interval is wrong. [Writer’s reasoning and sensitivity](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:162) · [Vera passage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:27) · [Definition](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py:15) · [Both readings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U06.md:36) · [Codex decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U06.codex.json:5) · [Claude decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U06.claude.json:1) |\n    12\t   | **U08 · S forecast** | **Codex “no” on the claimed bound. This is the closest call on the forecast itself.** | At $745B total, spending outside data centers can be at most $45B for the forecast to clear $700B. The writer deducts $25B and explicitly allows some AI infrastructure spending outside data centers. Claude’s comparison implies less than $15B **outside AI**. It leaves the amount **outside data centers** open on the writer’s own terms. Meta’s roughly 4% residual offers a concrete comparison, but the writer gives no reason the combined residual of all four firms stays below $45B. Alex’s open question bears directly on whether that comparison nevertheless supports a most-likely forecast. [Writer’s deduction](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json:226) · [Meta passage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:45) · [Both readings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U08.md:36) · [Codex decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U08.codex.json:5) · [Claude decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U08.claude.json:1) |\n    13\t\n    14\t3. **Serious — The recurring writer failure is assigning a size to a risk when that size selects the answer. The recurring reader disagreement is how much a comparison can support.** Counting each original reply once, excluding controls, gives the following. For run three I used its last authorized reading. A joint reply counts once when both forecasts were supported. [Third-run votes by reply](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:92) · [Third-run usable replies by case](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:198) · [Targeted-run votes](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:4)\n    15\t\n    16\t   | Original replies | Settled yes | Settled no | Split |\n    17\t   |---|---:|---:|---:|\n    18\t   | Third run, 12 | 8 | 2 | 2 |\n    19\t   | Targeted run, 6 | 2 | 1 | 3 |\n    20\t   | **Total, 18** | **10** | **3** | **5** |\n    21\t\n    22\t   Across **eight S and E replies**, one had settled support, three had settled rejection, and four split. All eight forecast a number. The clearest recurrence is the E Codex writer cutting the guidance reference by 15% in run three and 10% after the request explicitly asked for evidence sizing the cut. All eight P-family replies across the runs had settled support. O accounts for the remaining yes and split, on whether $18B described funding of an existing commitment balance. The stored votes therefore establish three rejected replies, not eight writer errors. They also show reader variation: the *same* third-run S Codex answer went **no → yes → no** across its three readings. [Earlier E cut](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread2-packets/T17.codex.json:5) · [Revised request](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/system.txt:9) · [O disagreement](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread2-packets/T02.codex.json:5) · [S original vote](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/TALLY.json:494) · [First re-read](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD-TALLY.json:291) · [Second re-read](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:123)\n    23\t\n    24\t4. **Serious — Alex settled the duty to justify an adjustment. How strong an indirect comparison must be is still his decision.** D741 requires the deduction to be stated or a supported bound given. D745 accepts an unresolved answer when its reason survives the strongest passages. The writer is also told to choose the most likely outcome and to size estimates from comparable deals, capacity, units, selling time, or price. The frozen examples illustrate the distinction. On q04, the reader used roughly $650B of plans and evidence about demand, costs, and delays to forecast $650–720B. On q19, two selling quarters and a $5,000 CPU price made the four million units implied by guidance assessable. No passage supplied the final discount. Those examples support reasoned estimates. They neither establish S’s category relationship nor provide the missing sales evidence for FY2028 Vera. [D741](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11348) · [D745](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11510) · [Writer’s sizing instructions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/system.txt:4) · [q04 reasoning](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json:246) · [q19 reasoning](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json:897)\n    25\t\n    26\t5. **Minor — Two details change how to interpret the paid option.** Its third reader is Codex. A published singleton stops the test if a reader-confirmed unsized adjustment decides its band. The free test asked about *support*, so these votes indicate a risk, not a measured paid-stop rate. Also, paid S asks about **2027** while targeted S asks about **2026**. Finally, the report’s statement that an unresolved singleton is automatically held is inaccurate. I replayed all nine stored answers and controls in memory. Both unresolved controls returned `publish` with no numeric outcome. Such a card has no chosen band, but the paid verdict does inspect published singleton cards for defects. [Paid reading and stop rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:262) · [Paid cases](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:43) · [Targeted cases](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:166) · [Singleton publication rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:782) · [Paid verdict code](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:772)\n    27\t\n    28\t**One recommendation.** Put this question to Alex: **“When a related figure makes one band more likely, but the evidence still leaves the next band plausible, may we publish the forecast if we explain why the comparison applies, or should we say ‘unresolved’ until the evidence bounds the quantity on the deciding side?”** I favor allowing the forecast when the writer explains why the comparison applies to the companies, period, and quantity asked about, and why it favors that side of the cutoff. U05 fails even that reading.\n    29\t\n    30\tThat ruling costs no provider spend. A fresh read under unchanged instructions would sample the same disagreement. A code or request change now would spend engineering and review time implementing one side before Alex chooses it. Closing S and E wholesale costs both forecasts whenever the evidence later becomes sufficient. The 36-call paid re-send would measure Luna and the benefit of judging related questions together. It cannot settle what Alex wants published. The earlier plan estimated **$0.17** for first attempts at typical output length under a **$0.50** ceiling; a new send requires separate authorization. [Registered sequence](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:375) · [Paid cost assumptions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:29)\n    31\t\n    32\t**One test.** After Alex rules, register one **$0 blinded contrast**. Give fresh writers the same spending question twice. One version has only the indirect comparison Alex ruled on. The other adds a clearly labeled test passage that explicitly bounds the four firms’ data-center spending above $700B and below $800B. Fix the expected decisions from Alex’s answer before showing either version. Mix in the saved U05 as the unsupported-cut control. The result moves the decision only if both reader families apply Alex’s rule to the indirect case, accept a forecast on the explicitly bounded case, and reject U05’s unsized cut. Agreement would justify returning to writer verification under a settled standard. Another split would keep the paid request closed. The original D745 FAIL stands either way.docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r62.md\n     1\t**Run with the listed changes.** The current tally can declare agreement when the readers give opposite explanations. I verified that with invented votes in memory. No file was edited, and no D747 packet was built or read.\n     2\t\n     3\t1. **Serious — Alex’s new sentence is faithful, but the surrounding instructions still permit the old reading.** Alex allowed a forecast when the writer explains why the comparison fits the companies, period and quantity, and why its own assumptions favor one side. The other side can remain possible. The new sentence says that accurately. The preceding sentence still says the passages *must* support the size or a bound. The first-reader brief offers the comparison as an alternative, while adjudicators receive the two sentences without that clarification. Make the shared definition say explicitly that an explained comparison can satisfy the support requirement. D741 still requires a writer using a total for a narrower amount to state its deduction or give a supported bound. D745’s unresolved clause is still right. A reader should judge the gap the writer identifies after considering the strongest evidence. The mere availability of a defensible forecast does not disqualify a defensible “can’t tell.” [Alex’s question and chosen option:11575](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11575) · [D741:11359](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11359) · [D745:11515](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11515) · [Shared definition:15](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py:15) · [First-reader brief:241](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:241) · [Adjudicator brief:23](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_adjudicate.py:23)\n     4\t\n     5\t2. **Minor — Keep the four fixed expectations.** Each has a reason in the original answer and request.\n     6\t\n     7\t   | Answer | Expected | Decisive evidence |\n     8\t   |---|---|---|\n     9\t   | U01 | **No** | “The outlook for the second half is uncertain” never says what prevents a forecast of annual data-center revenue. The request includes reported results and next-quarter guidance. [Answer:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/controls/K-unresolved-unjustified.json:1) · [Request:25](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8/user.txt:25) |\n    10\t   | U03 | **No** | The writer reduces the $20B starting figure to $17B and doubles it to $34B. Customer interest and competition explain why sales might miss. The answer gives no comparison that explains the 15% cut that puts it below $40B. [Original answer:178](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/codex.json:178) · [Request:27](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/user.txt:27) |\n    11\t   | U05 | **No** | The writer takes 90% of $40B and acknowledges that it has neither order sizes nor shipment pace. Calling the $4B shortfall “less than half an average quarter” restates the estimate without supporting it. [Answer:94](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:94) · [Request:33](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:33) |\n    12\t   | T15 | **Yes** | The writer explains why announced adoption favors growth above the roughly 50% market pace and why execution risk favors growth below management’s doubling. On its stated Vera base, 85% growth gives $37B. This supports the selected band without treating market growth as a guaranteed floor. [Answer:131](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json:131) · [Request:13](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/user.txt:13) · [Vera-specific guidance:27](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/user.txt:27) |\n    13\t\n    14\t3. **Serious — I would fix U02 at “no,” retain U04, and register what the other readings must get right.** This is my recommendation before the read, not an outcome from it. [Current expectations:398](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:398)\n    15\t\n    16\t   | Answer | Recommendation | Why |\n    17\t   |---|---|---|\n    18\t   | U02 | **Fix at no** | The writer explicitly calls more than $730B a *floor* for data-center spending, then forecasts $705.6B of data-center spending and $720B of total spending. All three numbers happen to point to the same band. They cannot all hold under the writer’s own explanation. Calling the $730B figure a directional comparison would repair the answer on the writer’s behalf. [Answer:390](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json:390) · [What the $730B measures:29](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:29) |\n    19\t   | U04 | **Keep open. I lean yes.** | It identifies the roughly 94% share needed to cross $700B and explains the mismatch between four firms’ AI spending and five firms’ data-center spending. It also retains the original writer’s 90% estimate and $670.5B calculation after the control removed their links to the answer. A reader can reasonably question that tension. Keep it as the disputed abstention, with no fixed vote. [Stored control:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/controls/K-unresolved-justified.json:1) · [How it was constructed:96](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:96) · [Five-firm passage:128](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:128) |\n    20\t   | U08 | **Keep open** | At $745B of total spending, up to $45B can fall outside data centers before the forecast drops below $700B. The writer deducts $25B, uses Meta as a comparison and acknowledges that some AI spending may fall outside data centers. Whether that makes a deduction below $45B more likely is precisely the judgment Alex authorized. [Answer:226](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json:226) · [Meta passage:45](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:45) |\n    21\t   | T10 | **Keep open for the spending argument** | Its $679–745B range crosses $700B. The writer cites raised plans and higher tracking estimates to favor the upper portion. Readers should decide whether those comparisons support the 95–100% data-center share. Its range crossing the line is permitted, and its old quotation defect is already settled. [Answer:276](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/claude.json:276) · [Four-firm passage:83](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/user.txt:83) · [Settled defect:11491](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11491) |\n    22\t   | U06 | **Keep open, with the arithmetic fixed** | At the writer’s 90% Vera share, its lower growth comparison gives $20B × 1.5 × 90% = **$27B**, below the selected band. Its preferred 1.8× gives **$32.4B**, inside it. The writer acknowledges the sensitivity. Readers may disagree about which outcome it justified. A “yes” based on the whole range staying above $30B cannot count as applying Alex’s rule. [Answer:162](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:162) · [Bands:3](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:3) |\n    23\t\n    24\t4. **Serious — Two older answers get the wrong format rule, and U03 gets the wrong original request.** The shuffle assigns nine fresh W identifiers and keeps the key outside the packets. For T10 and T15, both the initial and adjudication builders correctly select the third run’s system text, user text and schema descriptions. But both append *today’s* list of allowed qualifiers. I checked both stored schemas. Neither allowed `estimated`. T10’s source says “an estimated $750 billion,” exactly where that difference mattered. Read the allowed values from the stored schema in `case_dir`. Separately, I verified that U03 is the unchanged third-run E answer. The builder pairs it with the fourth run’s system text, which added the very instruction about sizing discounts under examination. Use the third-run request for U03. Describe U01 and U04 as constructed controls evaluated under their designated request. **Minor blinding issue:** all three controls are displayed as single-line JSON while the original replies are multiline. Give all nine the same display formatting while preserving their content. [Shuffle and packet assembly:256](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:256) · [Adjudication paths:64](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_adjudicate.py:64) · [D747 dispatch:119](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_adjudicate.py:119) · [Qualifier source:60](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py:60) · [Old allowed values:180](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/schema.json:180) · [Control provenance:104](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:104) · [Changed writer instruction:353](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:353) · [Control formatting:132](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:132)\n    25\t\n    26\t5. **Serious — Make the final verdict depend on the registered reading of the reasons.** I exercised the tally in memory with all nine labels, matching votes, and mutually incompatible reasons about whether AI spending supplies a floor. It returned **pass**. An empty key also returned **pass**. The ordinary vote rules otherwise match the registration. A settled wrong fixed vote fails, a continuing support split is inconclusive unless another case already fails, and a confirmed writer defect stops the run. T10 and the three controls are correctly excluded from defect stops. A one-reader defect on those excluded items can still delay completion by requesting adjudication. [Registered reason check and stops:407](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:407) · [Vote handling:288](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:288) · [Adjudication and exclusions:317](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:317)\n    27\t\n    28\t   Require the exact nine answers and both valid readings per answer. Keep the vote tally provisional until Claude records whether the deciding reasons are compatible and apply Alex’s rule, including after adjudication. Two readers who both insist on a guaranteed floor have made the same mistake. Give them the opportunity to settle a disagreement in reasons even when their votes match. If it persists, record **inconclusive**. The decisive pre-run check is that nine matching votes with opposing explanations cannot produce a final pass. [Round 61b decision condition:16](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r61b.md:16) · [Current final verdict:328](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:328)\n    29\t\n    30\t**Verdict — run with the listed changes.** A clean result can move the council to the E writer repair. A continuing disagreement should return to council with the exact passage and the two reasons. D745’s failure stands either way, and the paid re-send remains on hold until the writer repair passes its own free test. [Decision sequence:11560](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11560)docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r63.md\n     1\t**The FAIL is real.** W07 forecasts $705.6B of data-center spending, puts total spending at $720B, and calls **more than $730B** the floor for data centers. Both adjudicators upheld that contradiction. The three split votes affect what the council can conclude about reader agreement. They do not affect the stop. [W07 answer:390](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json:390) · [Recorded stop:30](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/REREAD-TALLY.json:30)\n     2\t\n     3\t1. **Serious — The arithmetic passed because the writer used the $730B figure in two different ways.** The source says the four companies indicated more than $730B of **AI infrastructure** spending. The writer recorded that figure correctly and cited it to justify a 98% share. The calculation then took 98% of total spending. The word *floor* appears in the explanation, where it makes a stronger claim about a different quantity. Even the top of the code’s recomputed data-center range, $720.3B, falls below that floor. The request declares no part–whole relation for this single question, so the code has no pair to compare. **Merely linking the source figure would not fix this.** It is already linked to the 98% estimate. What is missing is the claim that it imposes a minimum on the outcome. D738 deliberately leaves that interpretation to readers. [Source and request:29](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:29) · [Declared relations:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:5) · [Existing link:264](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json:264) · [Code comparison:655](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:655) · [Published range:62](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/CHECKED.json:62) · [D738:11265](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11265)\n     4\t\n     5\t   I compared the figures and explanations in all **18 original writer replies**. I found **0 of 12** in the third run and **1 of 6** in the targeted test with an explicit floor for a part above the writer’s own total. W08 is the closest counterexample. It uses the same $730B source, forecasts $720B out of $745B, and expressly allows some AI infrastructure spending to fall outside data centers. The third run also has a wrong $138B quarterly example in prose, a different arithmetic error. The narrow defect occurred once in this set. These replies cannot establish its production frequency. [Third-run replies:487](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/TALLY.json:487) · [Targeted replies:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:5) · [W08’s distinction:226](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json:226) · [Quarterly example:390](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P/answers/claude.json:390)\n     6\t\n     7\t2. **Serious — The three splits have different explanations.** Alex allowed a related figure when the writer explains why it fits the companies, period and quantity, and why the writer’s assumptions favor the chosen side. The other side may remain possible. My readings are as follows. [D747:11582](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11582)\n     8\t\n     9\t   | Answer | My reading | What the earlier readings establish |\n    10\t   |---|---|---|\n    11\t   | **T10, four-company spending** | **No. Codex applies D747 better.** At $730B total, crossing $700B requires about 95.9% to go to data centers. The $750B estimates do not identify the four companies. The nearly $800B estimate covers five. The writer calls these “the same totals” and treats the passage naming the four as a repeat. Higher tracking estimates help explain *total* spending. The midpoint of a range built from the assumed share does little to explain the share itself. | Spending support was initially accepted, then split in the same direction in two earlier readings and again here. That is a recurring disagreement about company and spending coverage. T10’s earlier quotation defect was excluded from this vote. [Writer:245](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/claude.json:245) · [First result:487](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/TALLY.json:487) · [First re-read:282](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD-TALLY.json:282) · [Second re-read:144](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:144) · [Current disagreement:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reread-adjudication/W03.codex.json:5) |\n    12\t   | **T15, Vera revenue** | **Yes on the $30–40B band. Codex applies D747 better, narrowly.** On the writer’s stated $20B Vera base, market-rate growth gives about $30B and doubling gives $40B. The writer explains why broad adoption favors gaining share and why deployment still under evaluation and competing products temper the doubling forecast. Claude identifies a real weakness. A market average through 2030 says little by itself about one product in FY2028. The writer supplies a reason for using the comparison, though, and D747 permits that judgment. The evidence does not establish 85% growth to that precision. | This answer was accepted in all three earlier readings, including by both families in the last one. It is the strongest evidence of reader variation. The instructions also changed, so the reversal cannot measure a random disagreement rate. [Writer:131](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json:131) · [Earlier results:500](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/TALLY.json:500) · [First re-read:337](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD-TALLY.json:337) · [Second re-read:169](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:169) · [Claude’s objection:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reread-adjudication/W06.claude.json:1) |\n    13\t   | **U02, support for the $705.6B forecast** | **No. Codex applies D747 better.** Claude upheld the impossible floor but still counted it toward support because both dollar amounts landed in the same band. D747 asks whether the explanation works under the writer’s *own* assumptions. Removing the floor would leave a different argument. At $720B total, 98% clears the line and the writer’s own 96% gives $691.2B. | The same answer already split Codex no, Claude yes, at both levels. The new finding is that both adjudicators now recognize the contradiction. The support vote stayed split. [Earlier votes:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:5) · [Claude’s yes and upheld defect:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reread-adjudication/W07.claude.json:1) · [Codex’s reasoning:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reread-adjudication/W07.codex.json:5) |\n    14\t\n    15\t3. **Minor — The registration demanded more agreement than the evidence justified.** Six of nine answers settled. All three controls came out as expected. Three of the four earlier targeted-test splits settled, which is genuine progress. All **six** adjudicator support votes on the three remaining splits still followed their own family’s first vote. Which family was stricter changed with the question. T10 and T15 expose judgment about the words *explains why* and *more likely*. U02 exposes a more specific difference between accepting a plausible band and accepting the writer’s stated route to it. D747 already gives a reason to reject U02. I see no missing general clause that another read of these answers would settle. Equally, the record does not support Claude’s conclusion that all three splits are random reader variation. Requiring every disputed answer to settle was a demanding, uncalibrated test of agreement. Its registered outcome still stands. [Votes:4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/REREAD-TALLY.json:4) · [Registered conditions:428](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:428) · [Claude’s conclusion:9](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/claude-r63.md:9)\n    16\t\n    17\t4. **Serious — The weakest proposal is to pass a writer test on stable controls and confirmed defects alone.** The product question is whether a **fresh E answer** invents the adjustment that moves Vera below $40B. U05 shows why that outcome must count. Both readers rejected its $36B forecast, yet the test recorded zero named defects. Claude’s proposed scoring could let a repeat through. A second weakness is the proposed code change. W08 shows why an indirect comparison must be allowed to influence a forecast without becoming a binding minimum. One contradictory floor warrants a case in the test. It does not establish a need to change the calculation format. Finally, D746 made writer repair conditional on agreement, and the registration sends persistent splits back to Alex. Moving forward now needs his decision. [U05 and empty defect list:8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:8) · [Claude’s proposed scoring:44](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/claude-r63.md:44) · [D746 condition:11564](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11564) · [Registered split outcome:434](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:434)\n    18\t\n    19\t**One recommendation.** Ask Alex to authorize the E writer repair despite the three disclosed splits. Have the writer decide whether the evidence favors one side of $40B **before** choosing a discount. If it can explain only why sales *might* fall short, it should say what remains unknown. Carry W07 into verification and defer a change to the calculation format. Another vote on the same nine answers would leave the writer untouched. The paid re-send stays on hold. [D746’s proposed repair:11566](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11566)\n    20\t\n    21\t**One test.** After approval and council review of the changed request, run one $0 blind test with fresh Codex and Claude writers on E, S and the previously supported data-center revenue question, P-v8. Mix in saved U05 and W07 to verify readers catch an unsupported cut and an impossible floor. To move forward, **each fresh E and S answer must earn agreement for its forecast or its specific reason for saying “can’t tell.”** Both P-v8 answers must still produce supported forecasts. The code must accept the fresh replies, and no confirmed defect may survive. A settled unsupported answer or contradictory floor fails. A persistent support split is inconclusive and leaves spending on hold. Only a clean result supports asking Alex to authorize the paid re-send. [Existing writer-test conditions:375](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:375)docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r64.md\n     1\t**Send with the changes below.** The repair asks the right question on E: what makes sales below $40B more likely? The main blockers are in the tally. I reproduced a clean `pass` with a fresh defect still disputed, and an `inconclusive` result when both readers caught the registered $730B floor. No writer was sent and no file was edited. [D749 decision:11645](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11645) · [Tally:95](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind5.py:95)\n     2\t\n     3\t1. **Serious — The floor control can miss agreement.** U02 calls more than $730B a floor for data-center spending while forecasting just $720B of *total* spending. The registered reading is the defect type “part above or added into its whole” naming **F12**. Its support vote is immaterial. The tally first compares the complete lists of figure IDs. With one reader naming `F12` and the other naming `F11, F12, F15`, it treats their common finding as two disputes. In an in-memory replay where both adjudicators upheld the floor using those respective lists, it returned `inconclusive`. Match this control by **type and presence of F12 before comparing votes**, at both reading stages. Keep the full lists in the report. [U02 answer:390](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json:390) · [Registration:473](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:473) · [Figure-list comparison:159](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:159) · [Floor check:143](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind5.py:143)\n     4\t\n     5\t2. **Serious — Three outcomes need fixing before the send.** I exercised each in memory, with the other votes set to pass.\n     6\t\n     7\t   | Case | Current result | Required change |\n     8\t   |---|---|---|\n     9\t   | A fresh answer earns support, but adjudicators remain divided on a reported arithmetic defect | `pass`, with the defect still listed as split | D748 sends a persistent split back to council. The registration explicitly covers support and floor splits but leaves this case open. Register a persistent fresh-defect split as inconclusive and apply it in the tally. [D748:11637](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11637) · [Registration:480](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:480) · [Tally:150](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind5.py:150) |\n    10\t   | A P-v8 writer returns `unresolved` and readers split on its reason | `inconclusive` | Fail as soon as the answer shape is known. Alex requires **two forecasts** on P-v8. Agreement about a reason cannot supply the missing forecast. [D748:11634](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11634) · [Tally:133](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind5.py:133) |\n    11\t   | A fresh first reply is invalid JSON | The checker records the refusal, but packet construction parses the reply again and raises `JSONDecodeError`. The tally also reparses it before reaching the refusal check. | Record `fail` from the checked refusal before requiring a readable answer or reader votes. The same rule covers a schema refusal that leaves no usable `answers` field. [First-reply check:69](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:69) · [Packet construction:62](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind5.py:62) · [Tally:90](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind5.py:90) |\n    12\t\n    13\t3. **Minor — Two clauses in line 9 drift from the decisions.** The change is general. It names no company, product, amount or test case. The growth has a written reason: the earlier writer lacked D747’s comparison route and D749’s order of decision. I verified 4,540 → 4,816 system-text characters, or 910 → 972 tokens using `o200k_base`. The registration records only the character counts. [Old line 9](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s-d745.txt:9) · [New line 9](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:9) · [Growth rationale:448](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:448) · [Prompt rule:223](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:223)\n    14\t\n    15\t   | Clause | Audit against the decisions and the rest of the request |\n    16\t   |---|---|\n    17\t   | Return figures and one answer per member. Identify estimates as the writer’s own. Preserve IDs, quoted text and English explanations. | **Consistent.** These instructions are unchanged. The schema distinguishes sources, computations and estimates, and provides an answer shape for each member. [Schema:47](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/schema.json:47) · [Schema:293](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/schema.json:293) |\n    18\t   | Weigh the side before sizing a decisive share, deduction, discount or pace. | **Consistent.** D749 supplies the order. Pace already belongs to the forecast, and D747 applies to the assumption deciding the band. [D749:11645](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11645) · [Schema:274](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/schema.json:274) |\n    19\t   | “The passages give its size, a bound that justifies the rung” | The bound is **consistent**. “Give its size” **keeps narrower** than D745 if read to require a stated size. D745 accepts passage evidence *supporting* the writer’s estimate. [D745:11517](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11517) |\n    20\t   | “Or a related figure,” with an explanation covering companies, period, quantity and the writer’s own assumptions | The explanation is **consistent**. The unqualified “or” **keeps wider** than D747. Alex permits this route when the passages do not give the quantity asked. The readers receive that qualification explicitly. [D747:11582](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11582) · [Reader definition:12](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py:12) |\n    21\t   | Answer unresolved and name what is unknown when neither side is favoured. | **Consistent** with D745, D749 and `answers/why`. “Not established … never a reason to stay” concerns confidence. “Missing confirmation alone” bars using silence to select an extreme. Neither requires picking a band when the strongest evidence leaves the choice open. [D745:11515](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11515) · [System:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:5) · [Schema:324](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/schema.json:324) |\n    22\t   | Guidance alone keeps the reference rung. The two discount sentences concern unshipped products. | **Consistent when read with bullet 2’s express condition**, but bullet 3 independently says the target “is an input to be discounted.” That imperative invites the old sequence on E. Guidance identifies the reference. Execution risk matters, but its existence alone cannot justify crossing a boundary. Make bullet 3 refer to the same condition. [System:4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:4) · [System:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:5) · [D745:11518](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11518) |\n    23\t\n    24\t   A shorter replacement for the two changed sentences is: “Before sizing a share, deduction, discount or pace that decides the band, explain which band the evidence favours. Show support for the size or a justifying bound. If the passages lack the quantity asked, explain why a related figure fits the companies, period and quantity, and why your assumptions favour that band. If the strongest evidence leaves the band unsettled, answer unresolved and explain the gap.” [D747:11582](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11582) · [D749:11645](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11645)\n    25\t\n    26\t4. **Minor — The three cases still discriminate, with one limit.** These are predictions from saved replies, not fresh writer results.\n    27\t\n    28\t   - **E.** Twice $20B is the $40B boundary. Management said *more than* double, with the excess unspecified. The two saved Codex replies produced $34B and $36B from unsupported 15% and 10% adjustments. The later Claude reply’s lower comparison gives $27B at its own 90% share, outside its chosen $30–40B band. On those arguments I would expect a specific unresolved answer. A forecast remains possible with an explained comparison. The earlier $37B Claude forecast is the live boundary case: its 85% growth explanation still divided readers under D747. [E evidence:10](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:10) · [Earlier Codex:178](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/codex.json:178) · [Later Codex:130](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:130) · [Later Claude:162](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:162) · [Earlier Claude:131](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json:131) · [Re-read:96](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md:96)\n    29\t   - **S.** At $745B total spending, the four firms can spend up to $45B outside data centers and still reach $700B. U08 used the same firms’ AI spending and Meta’s spending mix to explain a $25B deduction. Both readers accepted that forecast. They also accepted U04’s specific explanation of why the category difference left the cutoff unsettled. Either answer can pass. U02’s impossible floor cannot. [U08 deduction:226](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json:226) · [Recorded reasons:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reread-reasons.json:5) · [Floor finding:99](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md:99)\n    30\t   - **P-v8.** I expect a forecast. Reported first-half revenue of about $164.3B, next-quarter guidance and the observed 92.5% segment share support the two saved forecasts near $385.6B, inside $350–400B. Both had unanimous support. Requiring two fresh forecasts catches withholding on this supported case. The test nevertheless allows **all four fresh E and S replies** to be unresolved if their reasons earn agreement. A pass therefore cannot establish how often the writer withholds on borderline cases. [Codex P-v8:314](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8/answers/codex.json:314) · [Claude P-v8:314](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8/answers/claude.json:314) · [Prior votes:15](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md:15) · [Pass rules:474](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:474)\n    31\t\n    32\t5. **Minor — Packet provenance is correct. Tighten the two verification checks.** I rebuilt S, E and P-v8 in memory. All three had the exact current system text, and their user text and schemas matched the saved D745 cases. Both initial reading and default adjudication load each control’s **own** system text, user text, schema descriptions and qualifier list. U05 is the original invented-cut answer and must earn a settled **no**. U02 is the original floor answer and its support vote is correctly excluded. The build guard itself checks only a substring, so it would also accept an incomplete repair retaining those words. Compare the complete registered text. [Control key:7](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-key.json:7) · [Control key:22](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-key.json:22) · [Build check:32](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind5.py:32) · [Packet builder:49](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind5.py:49) · [Format material:63](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py:63) · [Adjudication:64](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_adjudicate.py:64)\n    33\t\n    34\t   The reasons record correctly sends matching first votes with incompatible explanations to adjudication and waits for a compatibility record before accepting matching final votes. First votes are validated as `yes` or `no`; adjudicator votes are not. Apply the same validation at both stages, and retain Claude’s short explanation of compatibility as in the saved record. [Reasons handling:108](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind5.py:108) · [Saved reasons:4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reread-reasons.json:4)\n    35\t\n    36\t**Verdict — send with listed changes.** The test can change the next decision. I checked in memory that a specific E unresolved answer, the saved justified S unresolved answer and a supported P-v8 forecast all pass the schema and publication checks. Once the tally distinguishes failure from disagreement, a clean pass supports **asking Alex** about the paid re-send. A failure or persistent split returns to council. [Registration:474](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:474) · [Alex’s authorization:11652](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11652)docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md\n     1\t# The D745 targeted test: result\n     2\t\n     3\tRun folder: `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/`. It was registered in `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D745] and scored by\n     4\t`blind4.py tally` (`TALLY.json`).\n     5\t\n     6\t## 1. Verdict: FAIL (registered line \"every S and E reply has a settled yes\")\n     7\t\n     8\t| Packet | Case | Writer | Shape | First readers (Codex, Claude) | Adjudicators (Codex, Claude) | Settled | Line |\n     9\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    10\t| U05 | E | codex | forecast, rung 3 | no, no | (none) | **no** | **MISS** |\n    11\t| U02 | S | claude | forecast, rung 4 | no, yes | no, yes | split | MISS (inconclusive) |\n    12\t| U08 | S | codex | forecast, rung 4 | no, yes | no, yes | split | MISS (inconclusive) |\n    13\t| U06 | E | claude | forecast, rung 3 | no, yes | no, yes | split | MISS (inconclusive) |\n    14\t| U04 | S | control: justified unresolved | unresolved | yes, no | yes, no | split | control not confirmed |\n    15\t| U07 | P-v8 | codex | forecast, rung 2 | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    16\t| U09 | P-v8 | claude | forecast, rung 2 | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    17\t| U01 | P-v8 | control: unjustified unresolved | unresolved | no, no | (none) | no | ok |\n    18\t| U03 | E | control: unsupported (15% cut) | forecast | no, no | (none) | no | ok |\n    19\t\n    20\t- **Refusals and defects.** All 6 first replies passed the schema and the code checks and were publishable. No reader marked a defect,\n    21\t  so no defect is settled or split.\n    22\t- **Writers never withheld.** 0 of 6 replies answered unresolved, including on S and E, where the new sentence invites it.\n    23\t- **One construction change, disclosed.** The registered row for K-unresolved-justified reads \"S made unresolved with the specific\n    24\t  gap\". It was first built on the third run's S Claude reply, which the current code refuses (S1, F11 \"estimated\"). It was rebuilt on\n    25\t  the third run's S Codex reply with the same reason text, and the code accepted it. The reason's five passage citations were checked\n    26\t  against the request.\n    27\t\n    28\t## 2. What split, and along which line\n    29\t\n    30\tEvery disagreement split by model family, and the four adjudications did not settle any of them: 8 of 8 adjudicator votes went with\n    31\ttheir own family. On S and E, Codex read \"supported\" as \"a passage states the size of the band-deciding assumption, or bounds the\n    32\tsame quantity\". Claude read it as \"the passages give a checkable bound that makes the rung most likely\".\n    33\t\n    34\t- **S (data-center spending of the four largest cloud companies in 2026; the rung 3/4 edge is $700B).** Both writers forecast rung 4.\n    35\t  The Claude writer took $720B of total spending at a 98% data-center share. The Codex writer took $745B minus a $25B non-data-center\n    36\t  deduction. Every reader on both sides read the same passage as decisive: e1947, \"more than $730 billion combined on AI\n    37\t  infrastructure\", for the same four companies.\n    38\t  - Claude: AI infrastructure sits inside data-center spending, so e1947 is a floor above $700B.\n    39\t  - Codex: e1947 gives no data-center split, so it does not bound the share.\n    40\t  - The answer's own stated lower bound gives $691B, in rung 3 (Codex adjudicator, U02).\n    41\t- **E (FY2028 Vera CPU revenue; management's \"more than double\" of $20B sits on the $40B edge between rungs 2 and 3).**\n    42\t  - The Codex writer realised 90% of the $40B reference. Its basis was deployment plans, a customer evaluation and a competitor's\n    43\t    benchmarks, and it said itself that \"the passages give no order sizes or shipment pace\". Both first readers, one per family,\n    44\t    said not supported, so no adjudication was needed (corrected after council round 61, which caught \"all four readers\").\n    45\t  - The Claude writer bracketed the pace between the server-CPU market pace (about 1.5x, e4687 and e4857) and the guidance (2x), then\n    46\t    took a 90% Vera share.\n    47\t    - The Codex adjudicator showed that the bracket fails under the answer's own share: 1.5 × $20B × 90% = $27B, in rung 4.\n    48\t    - The Claude adjudicator rescued it by arguing a near-full Vera share from e4691 and e4856.\n    49\t- **The justified-unresolved control.** Codex accepted the reason \"no passage gives the share\". Claude rejected it on the same e1947\n    50\t  floor, so the control's expected reading is itself contested.\n    51\t\n    52\t## 3. What the frozen gold does on the same kind of question\n    53\t\n    54\tThese are read for reasoning only. The gold questions differ from S and E in ladder and window.\n    55\t- **q04 (the same four companies' 2026 data-center spending).** Reader B took the big four's total plans of about $650B as the\n    56\t  reference, \"the figure NVIDIA management also cites\", with no deduction, and bet $650–720B.\n    57\t- **q19 (FY2027 stand-alone Vera revenue).** Reader B discounted management's $20B to $8–18B. The reasoning was the launch timing\n    58\t  (about two selling quarters) and the units the figure implies ($5,000 per CPU means four million units), against one counter-signal.\n    59\t  No passage states the discount.\n    60\t\n    61\tThe frozen ruler's own answers are reasoned bets of the kind the Claude family accepts. D741 ruling 3 (Alex) is stricter for S: the\n    62\tdeduction must be stated.\n    63\t\n    64\t## 4. What the paid re-send would meet\n    65\t\n    66\tUnder the paid gate's lines (`PAID-GATE.md` §4), a published singleton with a \"deciding gap\" stops the run. A deciding gap includes \"an\n    67\tunsized adjustment decides its band\". Where the two readers disagree, the third reader is Codex. On blind4's evidence, a published E forecast built like\n    68\tthese replies risks being marked by Codex, and the run would stop.\n    69\t- **Two corrections from council round 61, both checked:**\n    70\t  - An unresolved singleton is published, shown as unresolved (`calc_small.publish_decision`: \"held\" only when a declared relation\n    71\t    joins it to an answered member). The paid verdict inspects published singleton cards for defects (`paid_gate.py`, verdict), so it\n    72\t    is not exempt.\n    73\t  - The paid S singleton asks about CY2027 (`paid_gate.py` SINGLETONS: old-2 v6 8595). This test's S asks about CY2026 (`SMALL-DESIGN.md`\n    74\t    §5: old-3 v6 9043).\n    75\t- These votes are about support. They show a risk to the paid run, not a measured rate at which it would stop.\n    76\t\n    77\t## 5. Not done\n    78\t\n    79\tNothing was paid. Nothing was tuned on the 36 stored answers. No request text changed after the registration.\n    80\t\n    81\t## 6. The re-read under Alex's comparison ruling (D747): FAIL on a stop line\n    82\t\n    83\tRegistered in `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D747], reviewed by council round 62 (`codex-r62.md`), and scored by `blind4.py reread-tally`\n    84\t(`REREAD-TALLY.json`, `reread-reasons.json`). Packets W01–W09 are in `reread-packets/`, adjudications in `reread-adjudication/`, and\n    85\tthe key is `reread-key.json`.\n    86\t\n    87\t| Packet | Answer | First readers (Codex, Claude) | Adjudicators (Codex, Claude) | Settled | Registered line |\n    88\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    89\t| W01 | U01 vague unresolved control | no, no | (none) | no | expects no: ok |\n    90\t| W04 | U05 E Codex, unsized 10% cut | no, no | (none) | no | expects no: ok |\n    91\t| W09 | U03 E, unsized 15% cut | no, no | (none) | no | expects no: ok |\n    92\t| W02 | U06 E Claude, 1.8x × 90% Vera share | no, no | (none) | no | must settle: ok |\n    93\t| W05 | U04 S justified unresolved | yes, yes | (none) | yes | must settle: ok |\n    94\t| W08 | U08 S Codex, $25B deduction | yes, yes | (none) | yes | must settle: ok |\n    95\t| W07 | U02 S Claude, 98% share | no, yes | no, yes | split | expects no: MISS |\n    96\t| W06 | T15 E Claude, 85% growth bracket | yes, no | yes, no | split | expects yes: MISS |\n    97\t| W03 | T10 S Claude, 95–100% share | no, yes | no, yes | split | must settle: MISS |\n    98\t\n    99\t- **The stop.** On W07 (U02), both adjudicators upheld Codex's \"part above or added into its whole\" (F12, F11, F15). The answer\n   100\t  calls F12's \"more than $730 billion\" of AI infrastructure \"the supported floor\" for data-center spending, while its own total\n   101\t  spending (F11) is $720B and its data-center outcome (F15) $705.6B. The code did not catch it, because F12 is a source figure the\n   102\t  answer uses as a floor only in its prose.\n   103\t- **The reasons behind the six settled answers were read by Claude and recorded as compatible** (`reread-reasons.json`). Each pair\n   104\t  applies the same reading. On W08, for example, both say the same four companies' AI-infrastructure figure and Meta's AI share explain\n   105\t  why a deduction under $45B is more likely, and both note that this does not measure the deduction directly.\n   106\t- **What the ruling changed.** Of the four answers that split in the targeted test, three now settle in both families, with\n   107\t  compatible reasons: U04 yes, U06 no, U08 yes. All four controls and fixed cuts read as expected.\n   108\t- **The remaining splits no longer follow model family.**\n   109\t  - Codex is the stricter reader on S (T10, U02). Claude is the stricter reader on E (T15).\n   110\t  - T15 was accepted by both families in the second re-read and now splits.\n   111\t  - Read with the third-run S Codex reply's earlier no → yes → no, this shows single readers varying on boundary answers.\n   112\t- **A process error, shared by Claude and the council.** The registration required five boundary answers to settle, or to settle as\n   113\t  expected, but nobody estimated how often single readers split on boundary answers. AGENTS §7.1 says \"a conjunction over many items\n   114\t  fails by chance\". That clause of the pass line could fail on reader variation alone. The stop on U02 does not depend on it.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23; for f in $d/adjudication/*.json $d/reader-packets/*.json; do echo $f; cat $f; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23; for f in $d/adjudication/*.json $d/reader-packets/*.json; do echo $f; cat $f; done'","aggregated_output":"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/adjudication/X05.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"The passages give a bound for the assumption that decides the band. e3660 says Kress 'still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027, but expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028', and e4764 repeats it ('Huang expects CPU revenue to more than double next fiscal year'). More than double $20 billion is above $40 billion, which is rung 2's lower edge. F6's 100% Vera share also rests on the passages. e4691 ('Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year') and e4856 ('Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 could be more than double the $20 billion potential it cited for FY2027') describe this doubling as Vera CPU revenue, so a reader can check the share against them. F4 meets the format rule for a guided pace: it is recorded as an estimate, F2 is named in rests_on as the benchmark, and the answer says why the pace applies. On the upper side, the answer explains why the pace stays short of the 2.5x that $50 billion would need: 'more than double' is the floor, and AMD's own benchmark claims (e5400-e5402) trim the upside. Reader 1 asks for shipment or order volumes, which applies the system text's discount for 'a product with no shipments yet'. The answer gives a reason a reader can check for why that discount does not apply: e3660 is a reaffirmed ('still expects') outlook for $20 billion of CPU revenue in fiscal 2027, the year before the one asked, and e5400 benchmarks an existing 'Nvidia Vera-based platform'. e4856's word 'potential' is weaker wording of the same claim, while e3660 and e4764 say 'expects'. The forecast of $42 billion therefore follows from the passages and the stated assumptions.\"}}, \"defects\": []}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/adjudication/X05.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v41\": {\n      \"supported\": \"no\",\n      \"why\": \"The Vera-specific passages make $40 billion a relevant benchmark. NVIDIA expects “its Vera CPU revenue to double next year” (e4691), but e4856 describes the $20 billion FY2027 Vera base as potential. The answer takes that base in full because it says NVIDIA is “already selling at volume.” The CFO’s statement that NVIDIA “still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue” (e3660) gives an outlook, not shipment volume. The deployments “will” occur (e4689), and Anthropic “is evaluating” Vera (e5044). Even accepting the $42 billion total CPU forecast, Vera must supply at least $40 billion of it, about 95.2%. The answer identifies no evidence that other CPU lines are likely to contribute $2 billion or less. It therefore explains how Vera could reach $40 billion without establishing why that is more likely than falling just short.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/adjudication/X07.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"no\", \"why\": \"The outcome F15 = 98% (F14) x $720bn (F11) = $705.6bn. It sits only $5.6bn above the $700bn edge of rung 4, so the band depends on the share being at least 97.22%. The share is not sized: the answer says 'No passage sizes non-data-center spending such as offices or logistics, so I keep a 2% residual'. Its stated bound does not justify the rung either: 'the passages bound the share between about 96% and 100%' gives $691-720bn on its own $720bn total, and the low end of that is rung 3. The related figures are not explained as the ruling requires. (1) e1947 is spending 'on AI infrastructure', and the answer never explains why that is the same quantity as data-center capex. Its value matches the total-capex tallies (e2079, e5014: '$720 billion to $745 billion'), and if it were data-center capex it would exceed the answer's own $720bn total. (2) Meta's '$130 billion of AI capex' (e3686) against its '$125 billion to $145 billion' guidance (e1549) gives about 96%, which is below the share the rung needs. (3) e1416 ('Tech giants will spend an estimated $750 billion') and e4082 ('AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion') do not isolate these four companies. The reason given for rung 4 over rung 3, 'the repeated raises make an undershoot of guidance less likely', bears on the total, not on the share that decides the band. The band also flips on the answer's own Microsoft run-rate path: $156bn instead of $165bn gives about $711bn x 98% = $697bn, which is rung 3. None of these explains why, under the answer's own assumptions, the share is more likely to land above the line than below it.\"}},\n \"defects\": [{\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"part above or added into its whole\", \"figures\": [\"F12\", \"F11\", \"F15\"], \"decision\": \"uphold\", \"why\": \"The answer treats AI-infrastructure spending as the data-center part of capex. F14's basis calls it the 'share of the four companies' 2026 capital expenditure that is data-center (AI infrastructure) spending'. The reasons then adopt F12, e1947's 'more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure', as 'the supported floor from e1947 (more than $730bn of AI infrastructure) also lies in rung 4'. So the answer asserts a floor of more than $730bn for a part of capex, while its own forecast of the whole, total capex (F11), is $720bn. That floor is also above its own forecast of the part itself (F15, $705.6bn). This breaks the rule the writer was given: 'a part cannot exceed its whole'. The answer even says F12 'is at or above the total-capex tally', yet it still presents F12 as a floor instead of rejecting it.\"}]}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/adjudication/X07.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v6\": {\n      \"supported\": \"no\",\n      \"why\": \"On F11’s $720 billion total, reaching $700 billion requires all four companies together to spend no more than $20 billion outside data centers. F14 assumes a 98% data-center share, but e5014 says only that “a large portion” goes to AI infrastructure. e1947 covers the right four companies and year, but the answer does not explain how much of their AI infrastructure spending counts as data-center capex. Meta’s $130 billion covers one company, Amazon’s memory costs explain its increase, and the $750 billion data-center estimates do not isolate these four companies. At the answer’s own 96% lower estimate, spending is $691.2 billion. The guidance raises support the total but do not explain why the share needed for rung 4 is more likely.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": [\n    {\n      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n      \"type\": \"part above or added into its whole\",\n      \"figures\": [\"F12\", \"F11\", \"F15\"],\n      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\n      \"why\": \"The answer adopts e1947’s “more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year” as a “supported floor” for data-center capex. That puts the minimum for this part above F11’s $720 billion total for the same four companies and year. It also puts F15’s $705.6 billion forecast below its own floor. The writer’s rule says “a part cannot exceed its whole.”\"\n    }\n  ]\n}poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X01.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v8\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"H1 Data Center reported at $164.25B (e109 $75.25B + e2650 $89.0B, matching e5309); Q3 company revenue $112B from the $108.0B +/-2% guidance (e2652) and the beat record (e5054, e5055, e3200, e2452), trimmed for sold-out supply (e4911, e3733); Data Center share held at Q2's 92.5% (e3399, rising five quarters); Q4 sequential growth 12% benchmarked on the guided Q3 pace (e4899) with hyperscale reacceleration in Q4 (e4283, e3406) and supply limits (e3733, e5067). The band rests on stated bounds that check out: below $350B needs Q4 company revenue under Q2's $96.2B even with Q3 at the guidance low (about $95.0B needed), contradicted by the Q4 reacceleration passages; $400B needs about 27.6% Q4 sequential growth, more than twice the guided pace, while management says supply is constrained. The 70% full-year growth readings are reconciled to FY2028 (e2691, e5227) with the arithmetic shown, and SemiAnalysis's 20% H2 beat (e1135) is addressed.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X01.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v8\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"NVIDIA reported $164.25 billion in first-half Data Center revenue (e109, e2650). Q3 guidance and NVIDIA’s history of beating its guidance support the $112 billion Q3 company forecast (e2652, e5054–e5056). The writer carries Q2’s 92.5% Data Center share into the second half (e3399). At that share, even flat Q4 company revenue would put the year above $350 billion, while reaching $400 billion would require about 27.6% sequential growth in Q4. The writer’s 12% estimate uses NVIDIA’s Q3 guidance pace as a benchmark (e4899), with faster hyperscale growth expected in Q4 as Rubin supply expands (e4283) and supply constraints still in force (e3733, e5067). That yields $383.88 billion. Morningstar’s separate $361 billion estimate for the same segment and year also falls on rung 2 (e2295).\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X02.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"Explained related figure: e1947 (F1), the same four companies' indicated CY2026 AI infrastructure spending of more than $730 billion, taken as mostly data-center spending (servers and the facilities that house them, via e1649 memory purchases and e4405 Azure backlog capacity). The answer states that falling below $700 billion would need more than $30 billion of non-data-center spending plus deferral, finds the e122 delay tally too broad to identify that much from these buyers this year, and notes conventional cloud equipment in the same facilities adds back. Upper side bounded by e5014 (F2), the four's total capex of roughly $720-745 billion, which data-center capex cannot exceed, so below $800 billion; e5376 (F4) reaches about $800 billion only with a fifth buyer.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}},\n \"defects\": []}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X02.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v6\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"e1947 gives a plan of more than $730 billion for the same four companies in CY2026. The forecast assumes that most of this spending buys data-center equipment and facilities. Amazon’s higher memory costs (e1649) and Microsoft’s Azure backlog (e4405) support that comparison. Falling below $700 billion would require more than $30 billion of the plan to go elsewhere or slip beyond 2026, after allowing for conventional cloud equipment bought for the same facilities. The delays in e122 span owners and years. At the upper edge, e5014 puts the four companies’ entire capital budgets at roughly $720 billion to $745 billion, while e5376 approaches $800 billion for five companies. This supports rung 4 as a forecast. The $730 billion point is the writer’s estimate.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X03.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"no\", \"ground\": \"F4, an estimate that NVIDIA realizes 90% of the F3 $40 billion reference (F1 $20 billion FY2027 CPU revenue from e3660 x F2 'double' from e4691). The estimate cites e4689 (broad deployment), e5044 (Anthropic still evaluating Vera) and e5403 (AMD agentic per-core benchmark gain) and gives F5 = $36 billion, in rung 3.\", \"why_not\": \"The 10% shortfall alone decides the band. Without it the $40 billion reference sits at the lower edge of rung 2, and with it the forecast falls into rung 3. No passage gives the size of that shortfall or a bound for it. The answer says so itself: the AMD benchmarks 'indicate competitive risk without quantifying lost sales', and 'the passages give no order sizes or shipment pace to narrow that estimate.' e5044 (Anthropic evaluating Vera) names a possible extra customer, not a shortfall, and e4689 points upward. e5403 is AMD's own per-core benchmark claim, with no revenue effect stated. Three passages (e3660, e4764, e4856) also say management expects FY2028 to 'more than double' the $20 billion. That puts the outlook above $40 billion, so the haircut is applied to an understated base. Against a base above $40 billion, a 10% cut need not fall below $40 billion. The passages give no bound that justifies the rung, and the rung rests on an unsized judgement.\"}}, \"defects\": []}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X03.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v41\": {\n      \"supported\": \"no\",\n      \"ground\": \"e3660, e4691 and e4856 support a management reference of about $40 billion. F4 assumes NVIDIA realizes 90% of it, putting F5 at $36 billion in rung 3.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"e4689 names expected customers, e5044 reports an evaluation, and e5403 reports a performance benchmark. None shows how much FY2028 revenue will slip or go to AMD, or why a result between $30 billion and $40 billion is more likely than meeting the target. Calling the $4 billion shortfall less than half an average quarter restates its size without grounding it in shipment timing.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X04.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v8\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"e5309 (reported FY2027 H1 Data Center revenue of $164bn) plus a second-half build. The Q3 company estimate F6 = $111bn rests on the e2652 guidance range ($105.84-110.16bn), the beat record in e3200 and the outside $113.6bn in e5056, with a smaller beat assumed because of the constraints in e3115 and the Q4 timing in e4283. The Q4 estimate F8 = +10% sequential is benchmarked on the guided 12% Q3 pace (e5314) and the supply limits in e3115. The 93% Data Center share in F12 rests on the 92.5% Q2 mix (e5116) and the share rising every quarter (e3399). Lower line: $350bn needs only $186bn of second-half segment revenue, below two quarters at Q2's $89bn plus the guided Q3 step-up (about $98-102bn at a 92.5% mix). Upper line: $400bn needs $236bn of second-half segment revenue, more than the forecast total company H2 of $233.1bn. Reaching it would take Q4 company revenue of roughly $140bn, about 26-29% sequential growth, more than double the guided Q3 pace while management says supply is constrained. Morningstar's $361bn (e2295) is the reference, also in rung 2.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X04.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v8\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"NVIDIA reported $164 billion in first-half Data Center revenue (e5309). Its Q3 guidance, prior guidance beat and an outside estimate support the writer’s $111 billion Q3 company forecast (e2652, e3200, e5056). The writer assumes a comparable dollar increase in Q4, weighing the expected pickup in hyperscaler deliveries against supply limits (e5314, e4283, e3115). Even holding Data Center at its reported Q2 share of roughly 92.5% gives about $379.6 billion for the year. The share’s quarterly rise and management’s expectation that Data Center will lead growth support the writer’s 93% assumption, which gives $380.8 billion (e5116, e3399, e5223). Both figures fall in rung 2. Reaching $400 billion would require $236 billion of second-half segment revenue, above the writer’s $233.1 billion forecast for the whole company.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X05.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"e3660 (CFO: still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects it to more than double in FY2028), repeated by e4764 and framed as Vera CPU revenue by e4691 and e4856. The guided floor of 2.0x on $20 billion gives at least $40 billion, the lower edge of rung 2. The stated assumption F4 (a 2.1x pace: the guided floor plus a small overshoot, with AMD's benchmark claims trimming the upside, well short of the 2.5x that $50 billion needs) gives $42 billion. F6 takes Vera as 100% of FY2028 CPU revenue because e4691 and e4856 describe the doubling as Vera CPU revenue and no passage sizes another CPU line.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X05.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v41\": {\n      \"supported\": \"no\",\n      \"ground\": \"The answer uses e3660’s $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook, assumes 2.1× growth in F4, and assigns all $42 billion to Vera in F6. At that total, Vera must account for at least $40 billion of $42 billion, or about 95.2%, to reach rung 2.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"e4691 and e4856 make doubling a relevant benchmark for Vera, but e4856 describes the $20 billion base as potential. The answer treats reaffirmed guidance as evidence that NVIDIA is already selling at volume. e4689 names prospective customers and e5044 describes an evaluation. Neither gives shipment or order volumes that explain why reaching $40 billion is more likely than falling just short. The absence of a figure for other CPU lines also leaves Vera’s assumed share unsupported.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X06.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"e3660, e4764 and e4856 (management: CPU / Vera revenue to more than double, or with the potential to more than double, a projected $20 billion FY2027 base) and e4691 (Vera revenue to double) put the guided FY2028 figure at $40 billion or just above, the lower edge of rung 2, with no amount above it. The FY2027 base is itself an expectation (e4856 calls it a 'potential'). No passage gives Vera shipments, volume in hand, orders with units or prices, or a deployment schedule. e4689 (broad deployment) and e5044 (Anthropic evaluating) are not sized. The AMD benchmark claims (e5399-e5404) and the market-size forecasts (e4687, e4857, e4692) do not size Vera sales. The guided outcome sits on the rung 2/3 line, and neither a shortfall (a discount for unproven volume) nor an overshoot ('more than' double) can be sized from the passages, so the rung remains unsettled.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X06.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v41\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"e3660 puts the doubling threshold at $40 billion for all NVIDIA CPU revenue, using a projected FY2027 base. e4691 and e4856 describe expected or potential Vera growth without giving booked sales. The deployment plans in e4689 and Anthropic’s evaluation in e5044 give no order volumes, prices or delivery dates. AMD’s benchmarks in e5399–e5404 give no lost sales. The passages therefore leave open whether FY2028 Vera revenue is more likely to reach $40 billion or fall short.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X07.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"no\",\n  \"ground\": \"F15 = F11 x F14: a $720bn total-capex build (guidance sum F4 $540-570bn from e1254, e1549, e1649 plus Microsoft estimate F10 $165bn from e922 run-rate, e4405 and the tally-implied F9) times a 98% data-center share (F14, resting on e1947, e5014, e3686, e1549, e1649, e1416, e4082), giving $705.6bn, rung 4; plus the stated 'supported floor' of e1947 (more than $730bn of AI infrastructure) and the five-company ~$800bn figures (e2759, e5055) as the ceiling.\",\n  \"why_not\": \"The outcome sits $5.6bn above the $700bn line, and that margin comes from two point choices the passages do not size. (1) The 2% non-data-center residual: the answer itself says no passage sizes non-data-center spending, and its own stated bound for the share (96-100%) gives $691-720bn on its $720bn total, so the low end of the bound is rung 3; the bound does not justify the rung. (2) Microsoft at $165bn is set above the answer's own Microsoft-specific run-rate path ($156bn from e922); with $156bn and the same 98% share the outcome is about $697bn, rung 3, and the tally-implied $150-205bn (F9) also straddles. Across the answer's own stated ranges (total $705-735bn, share 96-100%) the outcome spans about $677-735bn. The e1947 'floor' does not repair this: if more than $730bn were a floor on data-center capex, the forecast could not be $705.6bn and the share would exceed 100% of the $720bn total, so the floor contradicts the answer's own assumptions; and e1947's figure matches the total-capex tallies (e2079, e5014 $720-745bn), so it reads as total capex relabelled, not as evidence that sizes the data-center share. The reason given for rung 4 over rung 3 (guidance raises make an undershoot less likely) bears on the total, not on the share or the Microsoft uplift that decide the band.\"}},\n \"defects\": []}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X07.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v6\": {\n      \"supported\": \"no\",\n      \"ground\": \"The forecast applies a 98% data-center share to $720 billion of total capex, yielding $705.6 billion. It bases that share chiefly on e1947 and e5014.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"Reaching $700 billion requires at least 97.22% of the $720 billion total to go to data centers. e1947 covers the same four companies and year, but the answer does not explain how much of their AI infrastructure spending counts as data-center capex. Meta's figures imply about 96% AI capex at its guidance midpoint (e3686, e1549). Amazon's memory costs explain its increase (e1649), and the $750 billion data-center estimates do not isolate these four companies (e1416, e4082). These comparisons do not explain why spending outside data centers is more likely to stay within the $20 billion that rung 4 allows.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"part above or added into its whole\",\n      \"figures\": [\"F12\", \"F11\", \"F15\"],\n      \"detail\": \"The answer adopts F12's more than $730 billion as a floor for the four companies' data-center capex. That floor exceeds both its $720 billion forecast for their total capex and its $705.6 billion forecast for data-center capex.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X08.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"The band is decided by the data-center share of total capex (F17, 96%) applied to a $740bn total (F16). The total sits inside the guided/aggregated $720-745bn range (e5014, e2079) and just below later trackers (e1856 near $745bn, e2458 $760bn), well inside rung 4 before any deduction. The share is an explained related figure: e1947 has the same four companies indicating more than $730bn of AI-infrastructure spending in 2026, and the answer explains that AI infrastructure is contained in data-center capex, so data-center capex is at least that (above the $700bn line). It adds Meta's $130bn of AI capex (e3686) against the $135bn midpoint of its $125-145bn guidance (e1549), which is about 96% before any non-AI data centers. It notes that Amazon's raise came from server memory costs (e1649, e4567), and it cites the about $750bn data-center spending estimates (e1416, e4082). The answer states that rung 3 needs a non-data-center remainder above about 5% (about $40bn) and admits the margin is thin. Under its stated assumptions, rung 4 is the more likely side.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X08.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v6\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"At F16’s $740 billion total, the four companies can spend up to $40 billion outside data centers and still reach rung 4. The strongest comparison is e1947, which puts the same four companies’ indicated 2026 AI infrastructure spending above $730 billion. The writer treats that spending as a guide to data-center capex. Meta’s $130 billion AI plan against $135 billion in midpoint total guidance (e3686, e1549) and the memory costs behind Amazon’s increase (e1649) support that interpretation. The 96% share is the writer’s estimate. It leaves $29.6 billion outside data centers and puts the forecast at $710.4 billion.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23; nl -ba $d/S/answers/claude.json | cut -c1-14500; rg -n '\"'730 billion|e1947|Ladder:' \"'$d/S/user.txt; cat $d/reader-packets/X08.*json; cat $d/reasons.json; cat $d/TALLY.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; nl -ba $d/blind_rules.py | head -110; rg -n -i '\"'volume in hand|taken in full|selling quarter|discount.*target' docs/DECISIONS-log.md | head -55; nl -ba AGENTS.md | rg -i 'mechanism draws|closed set with examples|no case-fitted|abstract clause|council decides'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; nl -ba $d/blind_rules.py | head -110; rg -n -i '\"'volume in hand|taken in full|selling quarter|discount.*target' docs/DECISIONS-log.md | head -55; nl -ba AGENTS.md | rg -i 'mechanism draws|closed set with examples|no case-fitted|abstract clause|council decides'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t\"\"\"The one source of the defect definitions and the writer's format material for judging-v13s blind readers and adjudicators (D744).\n     2\t\n     3\tReaders and adjudicators get the same text: the writer's system text, every schema description, and these definitions, so that what\n     4\tthe format rules permit is never marked as a defect by one and cleared by the other.\n     5\t\"\"\"\n     6\timport json\n     7\tfrom pathlib import Path\n     8\t\n     9\tDEFINITIONS = \"\"\"Definitions (judge each against the passages and the format rules the writer was given, shown below; what the format rules permit is\n    10\tnot a defect):\n    11\t- supported: the forecast follows from the shown passages and the answer's stated assumptions; a reader could check it against them.\n    12\t  For a forecast, the assumption that decides the band must rest on the passages in one of three ways: its size, a bound that\n    13\t  justifies the rung, or, when the passages do not give the asked quantity, a related figure that the answer explains. An explained\n    14\t  related figure satisfies this requirement even though the other side of the line remains possible. It is explained when the\n    15\t  answer says why the comparison applies to the companies, the period and the quantity asked, and why, under the answer's own stated\n    16\t  assumptions, the chosen side of the line is more likely; an answer that does not explain this is not supported (a ruling). For an unresolved\n    17\t  member, supported means its reason holds: after the strongest relevant passages, a gap remains that keeps the rung unsettled (a\n    18\t  ruling).\n    19\t- number misread: a source figure's number, scale, sign or qualifier differs from what its passage's quote writes, allowing what the\n    20\t  format rules permit.\n    21\t- wrong arithmetic: a computed figure does not equal its operation on its inputs, within the tolerance the format rules state.\n    22\t- part above or added into its whole: a segment is larger than the total it belongs to, or is added again into a total that already\n    23\t  includes it (directly or through an intermediate figure or an estimate).\n    24\t- two forecasts of one quarter: two different figures are used as the value of one quantity and period. A growth rate computed from\n    25\t  guidance and used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate is not a second value, when one figure supplies the quarter in the\n    26\t  calculation (a ruling). Look through any rate or multiple, including inside a stated assumption, for a second dollar value of the\n    27\t  quarter.\n    28\t- own estimate passed off as the market's reference: a figure marked as the market's reference is, or rests on, the writer's own\n    29\t  estimate or forecast. A reference that applies a past period's reported share or multiple to another period (a quarter's mix\n    30\t  applied to the year) is our comparison estimate, not the market's (a ruling).\n    31\t- broken structure: a link that does not resolve, a figure that depends on itself, a missing or doubled answer, an outcome that is not\n    32\t  the question's quantity, or a rung whose band does not hold the outcome. For a range outcome, the format allows \"a band it overlaps,\n    33\t  with the reason in reasons\": such a rung is not broken structure; a band the range does not reach is. A total used for a narrower\n    34\t  quantity (total capital expenditure for data-center spending) without a stated deduction or a supported bound is an outcome not of\n    35\t  the question's quantity (a ruling).\"\"\"\n    36\t\n    37\t\n    38\tdef descriptions(schema, path=''):\n    39\t    \"\"\"Every description in the schema, keyed by its field path.\"\"\"\n    40\t    found = {}\n    41\t    if isinstance(schema, dict):\n    42\t        if isinstance(schema.get('description'), str):\n    43\t            found[path.strip('/') or 'answer'] = schema['description']\n    44\t        for key, value in schema.items():\n    45\t            if key != 'description':\n    46\t                step = '' if key in ('properties', 'items', 'anyOf') else key\n    47\t                found.update(descriptions(value, f'{path}/{step}' if step else path))\n    48\t    elif isinstance(schema, list):\n    49\t        for value in schema:\n    50\t            found.update(descriptions(value, path))\n    51\t    return found\n    52\t\n    53\t\n    54\tdef allowed_qualifiers(schema):\n    55\t    \"\"\"The qualifier values the writer's own schema allowed (D747: an older run's schema, not today's list).\"\"\"\n    56\t    options = schema['properties']['figures']['items']['properties']['qualifier']['anyOf']\n    57\t    found = [o['enum'] for o in options if isinstance(o, dict) and 'enum' in o]\n    58\t    if len(found) != 1:\n    59\t        raise SystemExit('the schema does not hold exactly one qualifier list')\n    60\t    return found[0]\n    61\t\n    62\t\n    63\tdef format_material(case_folder):\n    64\t    \"\"\"The writer's system text and every schema description, as shown to readers and adjudicators.\"\"\"\n    65\t    case_folder = Path(case_folder)\n    66\t    schema = json.loads((case_folder / 'schema.json').read_text())\n    67\t    return '\\n'.join(['# The system text the writer was given', (case_folder / 'system.txt').read_text(), '',\n    68\t                      '# The format rules the writer was given (schema descriptions)',\n    69\t                      json.dumps(descriptions(schema), indent=1, ensure_ascii=False), '',\n    70\t                      'The qualifier values the format allows (a qualifier not in this list cannot be recorded): '\n    71\t                      + ', '.join(allowed_qualifiers(schema)) + '.'])\n3072:- What reader B does that judging-v6 did not, read from B's answers on the questions that differed (`judgement-gold/reconcile-v1.md`, `gold-v1.json`): carries the guided growth forward rather than annualising the last quarter flat (Data Center, total revenue); discounts a management target for a product with no shipments by default (Vera CPU: two selling quarters, four million units at $5,000, the company's own choice of another CPU); sizes an announced but unsized commitment from its disclosed comparable (the Firmus backstop from Sharon's); derives a figure no one states from the accounting identities in the evidence (capex from operating less free cash flow); keeps linked conclusions consistent (China share against the H200 authorization theme). Written into `judging-v7.txt` as five clauses (v6 3,782 → 4,924 characters; reason: each clause is a behaviour of the frozen standard the judge lacked), named by `poc-v91`; schema and code unchanged.\n3073:- Proof on the stored requests of the questions whose arm-4 judgement differed from the gold (`proof-judging-v7a.txt`, `proof-judging-v7.txt`; one send each, cents): total revenue rung 3 → 2 (about $421 billion from the guided growth on FY2026's base; gold $400–435 billion), Data Center 3 → 2 (about $372 billion; gold $370–400 billion), Vera CPU 1 → 2 ($12 to below $20 billion, the target discounted for the volume not in hand; gold $8–18 billion), DRAM prices 3 → 2 (about +15%; gold +10–35%), financing total stays 4 ($33.5–39.8 billion; gold $25–60 billion), the Ohio guarantee stays Yes; China share stays below 1% in draw 1, consistent with that draw's H200 theme judged No (the gold has the authorization likely and the share 1–8%; the consistency clause ties the share to the theme, so it moves with it). Six of the seven re-sent questions now land in the gold's band; the end-to-end agreement under judging-v7 needs a run on `poc-v91`, which is Alex's call. A placement gap seen on the way: the Vera CPU question never held the fall-launch statement (filed under the Rubin launch questions), so the judge could not count selling quarters from evidence; the default-discount clause covers it, the filing is a D2 class to count.\n    41\t- **While Alex is not active, the council decides (D439).** A choice or a next step that would go to Alex, including opening a new\n    85\t- When one mechanism draws findings round after round, stop answering findings: propose one structural rule.\n   114\t- **No case-fitted fix.** A hand list, a special path or a rule fitted to the case in view fixes that case and breaks the next. First ask\n   227\t- **A classification the model must make is written as a closed set with examples:** two or three examples per class, the procedure\n   229\t  items\"). An abstract clause (\"a material statement\") is a coin flip per send. The examples are not growth to avoid; the abstract clause\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23; nl -ba $d/S/answers/claude.json | cut -c1-14500; rg -n '\"'730 billion|e1947|Ladder:' \"'$d/S/user.txt; cat $d/reader-packets/X08.*json; cat $d/reasons.json; cat $d/TALLY.json'","aggregated_output":"     1\t{\"figures\":[{\"id\":\"F1\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Alphabet\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"total company\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":null,\"low\":195,\"high\":205,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e1254\",\"quote_number\":\"$195 billion to $205 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F2\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Meta Platforms\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"total company\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":null,\"low\":125,\"high\":145,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e1549\",\"quote_number\":\"$125 billion to $145 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F3\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Amazon\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"total company\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":220,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e1649\",\"quote_number\":\"$220 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F4\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Microsoft\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"total company\",\"period\":\"CY2026 Q1\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"reported\",\"value\":30.88,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e922\",\"quote_number\":\"$30.88 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F5\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Alphabet, Meta Platforms and Amazon\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":null,\"low\":540,\"high\":570,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F1\",\"F2\",\"F3\"],\"operation\":\"sum\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F6\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta Platforms\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":null,\"low\":720,\"high\":745,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e5014\",\"quote_number\":\"$720 billion to $745 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"roughly\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[\"v6\"]},{\"id\":\"F7\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Microsoft\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"total company\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":null,\"low\":150,\"high\":205,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F6\",\"F5\"],\"operation\":\"difference\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F8\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Microsoft\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"total company\",\"period\":\"CY2026 Q2-Q4\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":null,\"low\":119.12,\"high\":174.12,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F7\",\"F4\"],\"operation\":\"difference\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F9\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"four largest hyperscalers\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":745,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e1856\",\"quote_number\":\"$745 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"near\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F10\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"top four hyperscalers\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":760,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e2458\",\"quote_number\":\"$760 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F11\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft and Alphabet\",\"measure\":\"AI infrastructure spending\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":730,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e1947\",\"quote_number\":\"$730 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"more than\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F12\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"tech giants\",\"measure\":\"data-center spending\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":750,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e1416\",\"quote_number\":\"$750 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"estimated\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F13\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"top 5 hyperscalers\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":800,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e2759\",\"quote_number\":\"$800 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"nearly\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F14\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta and Oracle\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":795,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e5055\",\"quote_number\":\"$795 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"roughly\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F15\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Meta Platforms\",\"measure\":\"AI capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"total company\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":130,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e3686\",\"quote_number\":\"$130 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F16\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta Platforms\",\"measure\":\"capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":740,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":\"My forecast of the four companies' total 2026 capital expenditure, inside the guided $720-745bn range (F6) and a little below the latest trackers (F9, F10). Upward: every guidance update this year was a raise (Alphabet, Meta, Amazon), Amazon's raise came from memory prices that are still rising, and NVIDIA's nearly $800bn for the top five (F13) agrees with BofA's roughly $795bn for these four plus Oracle (F14). Downward: the aggregate implies Microsoft at $150-205bn (F7), which needs $119-174bn over the last three quarters (F8) against $30.88bn in the first (F4), and blocked or delayed data-center projects can slip some spending into 2027. Fourth-quarter spending is largely committed, so the year lands near the guided range rather than far from it.\",\"rests_on\":[\"F4\",\"F5\",\"F6\",\"F7\",\"F8\",\"F9\",\"F10\",\"F11\",\"F13\",\"F14\",\"e1254\",\"e1549\",\"e1649\",\"e4567\",\"e4566\",\"e122\"],\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F17\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta Platforms\",\"measure\":\"data-center share of capital expenditure\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":96,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"%\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":\"Share of the four companies' total capital expenditure that goes to data centers (servers, chips, networking, buildings, power). Data-center capex contains all AI capex plus non-AI cloud capacity. Meta's AI capex alone is $130bn (F15) against the $135bn midpoint of its $125-145bn guidance (F2), about 96% before non-AI data centers; Amazon's raise was mostly server memory cost, i.e. data-center spending; the companies' indicated AI-infrastructure spending exceeds $730bn (F11) and an outside estimate puts the tech giants' data-center spending at $750bn (F12), both close to the whole total. The non-data-center remainder (chiefly Amazon's logistics, offices and devices) is not sized in the evidence; I take it at about 4%.\",\"rests_on\":[\"F2\",\"F11\",\"F12\",\"F15\",\"e1649\",\"e4567\"],\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F18\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"four largest AI hyperscalers\",\"measure\":\"data-center capex\",\"scope\":\"combined\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":710.4,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F16\",\"F17\"],\"operation\":\"share\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[\"v6\"],\"reference_for\":[]}],\"answers\":[{\"view_id\":\"v6\",\"shape\":\"forecast\",\"rung\":4,\"reference_unavailable\":null,\"reasons\":\"Reference: the market expects the four companies' combined 2026 capital expenditure at roughly $720-745bn (F6, Zacks; the same aggregated guidance appears in e2079 and, as more than $730bn of AI infrastructure, in e1947). That range sits in rung 4 ($700-800bn). It is total capital expenditure, so the data-center outcome sits at or slightly below it.\\n\\nBuild of the total: Alphabet guides $195-205bn (raised in July), Meta $125-145bn (raised) and Amazon $220bn (raised, mostly on memory-chip costs); all three report on the calendar year, so together $540-570bn (F5). The aggregate therefore implies Microsoft at $150-205bn for CY2026 (F7). Microsoft's fiscal Q3 2026 (January-March 2026) capex was $30.88bn, up 84% (F4), so the implied range needs $119-174bn over the remaining three quarters (F8): continued quarterly step-ups, consistent with an 84% growth pace but the main downside risk to the total. Upward pressure: every guidance update this year has been a raise, the memory prices behind Amazon's raise are still rising (e4567, e4566), and later trackers sit near $745bn (F9) and at $760bn (F10); NVIDIA's nearly $800bn for the top five (F13) agrees with BofA's roughly $795bn for these four plus Oracle (F14). Downward pressure: Microsoft's first-quarter run-rate and data-center projects blocked or delayed (e122) that can push some spending into 2027. I forecast the four's total capital expenditure at $740bn (F16), inside the guided range and a little below the latest trackers.\\n\\nWhich band the evidence favours before the deduction: rung 4. Every figure for this population labelled as AI-infrastructure or data-center spending sits in rung 4: more than $730bn of AI infrastructure indicated by the companies (F11) and an estimated $750bn of data-center spending by the tech giants (F12; e4082 also gives about $750bn). Rung 3 would need the non-data-center part of a $740bn total to exceed about $40bn, above 5% of the total.\\n\\nDeduction: data-center capital expenditure contains all AI capex plus non-AI cloud capacity. Meta alone plans $130bn of AI capex (F15) against the $135bn midpoint of its guidance (F2), about 96% before counting non-AI data centers; Amazon's raise came from server memory costs, which is data-center spending; the cloud businesses that use the capacity are accelerating (e4404, e4405, e4406), so the spending is not being redirected. The non-data-center remainder (chiefly Amazon's logistics, offices and devices) is not sized in the evidence; I take it at about 4% (F17, 96%), below the roughly 5% that would cross into rung 3.\\n\\nOutcome: $740bn x 96% = about $710bn (F18), rung 4. The margin over the $700bn boundary is thin: rung 3 is the main alternative if the total lands near the bottom of the guided range together with a non-data-center share above about 3%, or if Microsoft's spending stays close to its first-quarter pace. Rung 5 would need a total above about $830bn, which no figure for these four supports (the nearly $800bn figures include a fifth company).\",\"why\":null,\"cites\":[\"e1254\",\"e1549\",\"e1649\",\"e4567\",\"e922\",\"e5014\",\"e2079\",\"e1947\",\"e1856\",\"e2458\",\"e1416\",\"e4082\",\"e2759\",\"e5055\",\"e3686\",\"e4566\",\"e122\",\"e4404\",\"e4405\",\"e4406\"],\"pushes\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1254\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e1549\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e1649\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4567\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e922\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5014\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2079\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e1947\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e1856\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2458\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e1416\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4082\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2759\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5055\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3686\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4566\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e122\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4404\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4405\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4406\",\"push\":\"none\"}],\"theme_pushes\":[]}]}\n3:Ladder: rung 1 Lowest range: Combined spending is below $500 billion. / rung 2 Lower-middle range: Combined spending is at least $500 billion and below $600 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Combined spending is at least $600 billion and below $700 billion. / rung 4 Upper-middle range: Combined spending is at least $700 billion and below $800 billion. / rung 5 Highest range: Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\n29:- e1947 [party_statement; fool.com (Patrick Sanders); original source Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet ; from gnews:c8ac167e2356a75994ebc26f 2026-08-13] Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure in calendar 2026.\n30:    Quote: hyperscalers Amazon , Meta Platforms , Microsoft , and Alphabet have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year\n34:    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1947, e2177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"The band is decided by the data-center share of total capex (F17, 96%) applied to a $740bn total (F16). The total sits inside the guided/aggregated $720-745bn range (e5014, e2079) and just below later trackers (e1856 near $745bn, e2458 $760bn), well inside rung 4 before any deduction. The share is an explained related figure: e1947 has the same four companies indicating more than $730bn of AI-infrastructure spending in 2026, and the answer explains that AI infrastructure is contained in data-center capex, so data-center capex is at least that (above the $700bn line). It adds Meta's $130bn of AI capex (e3686) against the $135bn midpoint of its $125-145bn guidance (e1549), which is about 96% before any non-AI data centers. It notes that Amazon's raise came from server memory costs (e1649, e4567), and it cites the about $750bn data-center spending estimates (e1416, e4082). The answer states that rung 3 needs a non-data-center remainder above about 5% (about $40bn) and admits the margin is thin. Under its stated assumptions, rung 4 is the more likely side.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v6\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"At F16’s $740 billion total, the four companies can spend up to $40 billion outside data centers and still reach rung 4. The strongest comparison is e1947, which puts the same four companies’ indicated 2026 AI infrastructure spending above $730 billion. The writer treats that spending as a guide to data-center capex. Meta’s $130 billion AI plan against $135 billion in midpoint total guidance (e3686, e1549) and the memory costs behind Amazon’s increase (e1649) support that interpretation. The 96% share is the writer’s estimate. It leaves $29.6 billion outside data centers and puts the forecast at $710.4 billion.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}{\n \"X01\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both: H1 reported, Q3 from guidance and the beat record, 92.5% share held; even flat Q4 keeps the year at or above $350B, and $400B needs about 27.6% Q4 sequential growth against the guided pace and supply limits.\"},\n \"X02\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both apply D747: e1947 covers the same four companies and year; the answer explains why most of that spending buys data-center equipment and facilities (e1649 memory, e4405 Azure backlog) and why falling below $700B would need more than $30B to go elsewhere or slip.\"},\n \"X03\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both: the 10% shortfall has no size or grounding in shipment timing; the cited passages name customers, an evaluation and a benchmark, not lost or slipped revenue.\"},\n \"X04\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both: $350B needs only $186B of second-half segment revenue, below the Q2 run-rate plus the guided step-up; $400B needs $236B, more than the forecast total company second half.\"},\n \"X06\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both: the guided figure sits on the $40B line on a projected base; no passage gives shipments, orders with units and prices, or a deployment schedule, so neither a shortfall nor an overshoot can be sized and the rung stays unsettled.\"},\n \"X07\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both no on support (not scored for this control): the 2% residual and the Microsoft uplift that decide the band are unsized, and the answer's own 96% bound gives rung 3. The floor defect is marked by one reader only and goes to adjudication.\"},\n \"X08\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both apply D747: at a $740B total up to $40B may fall outside data centers; e1947 (same four companies, same year) and Meta's AI share explain why the share is high enough. Claude's own trace note: the answer says data-center capex contains all AI capex, which with e1947 implies more than $730B, above its own $710.4B; neither reader marked it, and it does not move the band.\"}\n}\n{\n \"verdict\": \"inconclusive\",\n \"fails\": [],\n \"splits\": [\n  \"X05 fresh E/claude: v41 settled=split [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer]\"\n ],\n \"lines\": [\n  \"X01 fresh P-v8/claude      v8 forecast   settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast] ok\",\n  \"X02 fresh S/codex          v6 forecast   settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] ok\",\n  \"X03 K-invented-cut         v41 forecast   settled=no     votes=['no', 'no'] [control expects not supported] ok\",\n  \"X04 fresh P-v8/codex       v8 forecast   settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast] ok\",\n  \"X05 fresh E/claude         v41 forecast   settled=split  votes=['no', 'yes', 'no', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] MISS\",\n  \"X06 fresh E/codex          v41 unresolved settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] ok\",\n  \"X07 K-impossible-floor     v6 forecast   settled=no     votes=['no', 'no'] [support not scored; the floor defect is] ok\",\n  \"X08 fresh S/claude         v6 forecast   settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] ok\"\n ],\n \"settled_defects\": [\n  [\n   \"X07\",\n   \"K-impossible-floor\",\n   \"part above or added into its whole\",\n   [\n    \"F12\"\n   ],\n   \"settled\"\n  ]\n ],\n \"split_defects\": [],\n \"floor_control\": \"settled\"\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/claude-r65.md; nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '3068,3074p'; nl -ba docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md | head -44\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/claude-r65.md; nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '3068,3074p'; nl -ba docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md | head -44\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Council round 65: Claude's reflection on the D749 writer test, written before Codex's read\n     2\t\n     3\tAlex is asleep. His standing direction for tonight (the /goal of 2026-11-23): converge the remaining problems with the council,\n     4\tno scope drift, read the log so no mistake repeats, measure and settle, and head toward production. D439 governs while he is away:\n     5\t- the council decides reversibly;\n     6\t- no paid send (D439's ceiling is spent);\n     7\t- nothing is committed.\n     8\t\n     9\t## Result\n    10\t`BLIND5-RESULT.md`: INCONCLUSIVE, with one support split (X05, E Claude, rung 2 by taking guidance in full). No line failed:\n    11\t- the E Codex writer answered a justified unresolved;\n    12\t- both S forecasts and both P-v8 forecasts are supported;\n    13\t- both controls were caught;\n    14\t- there is no fresh defect and no refusal.\n    15\t\n    16\t## What I take from it\n    17\t1. **The repair worked where it was aimed.** The writer type that invented cuts twice (third run, targeted test) now withholds with\n    18\t   a specific gap, and both families accept it. S converged too.\n    19\t2. **The one split is resolvable on fact against an existing rule.** Bullet 2 says a not-yet-shipping target \"is taken in full only\n    20\t   when the evidence shows the volume in hand\".\n    21\t   - None of E's 15 passages shows shipments, revenue to date or orders.\n    22\t   - X05's premise (\"already selling at volume\") is an inference from a reaffirmed outlook.\n    23\t   - It is not a gap in D747. The two families differ on whether an outlook counts as volume in hand.\n    24\t3. **Log check: is this the same E mechanism again?**\n    25\t   - The earlier E failures were invented cuts. This one is the opposite: no discount, by asserting volume in hand.\n    26\t   - The request's clause \"the evidence shows the volume in hand\" has no closed definition, and AGENTS §6 says \"a classification\n    27\t     the model must make is written as a closed set with examples\".\n    28\t   - The smallest structural fix is a closed definition at its owner (bullet 2): volume in hand means reported shipments or revenue\n    29\t     to date, or orders with units, prices or dates in the passages. A company's own outlook or a \"will be deployed\" is not volume\n    30\t     in hand.\n    31\t   - This defines an existing term. It adds no new rule. Its source is the system text's own discount principle and D749 (\"if it can\n    32\t     say only that sales might fall short, answer can't tell\").\n    33\t\n    34\t## My position (to be challenged)\n    35\t- **Option 1, my lean.** Make the closed definition, a few dozen characters at bullet 2. Run the $0 checks (cases, rehearsal, plan\n    36\t  hash), then the cheapest proof (AGENTS §1.4):\n    37\t  - fresh writers on E only, two per family, plus fresh readers;\n    38\t  - and only if a fresh E reply still splits on this point, stop and put it to Alex as his ruling.\n    39\t  - If clean, ask Alex in the morning to authorize the paid re-send. The registered D749 result stays INCONCLUSIVE and is disclosed.\n    40\t    The E-only test is a separate, smaller registered test.\n    41\t- **Option 2.** Go straight to Alex in the morning for the paid re-send with X05 disclosed, and change nothing. It is cheaper\n    42\t  tonight, but it leaves a known ambiguity in the request that luna will meet on E and on every not-yet-shipping target in\n    43\t  production.\n    44\t- **Option 3.** Put \"is a reaffirmed outlook volume in hand?\" to Alex as a ruling. This is a definitional question his existing words\n    45\t  already answer (\"taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand\"). I lean that the council can settle it reversibly\n    46\t  tonight under D439.\n    47\t- **Process lesson applied.** The E-only test's registered lines must not demand settlement on boundary answers beyond what the\n    48\t  record shows readers deliver (D748's error). The decisive line is narrow: no fresh E reply takes a not-yet-shipping target in full,\n    49\t  or invents a cut, with settled support, and controls hold.\n    50\t- **What would change my mind.** The council finds the definition wider or narrower than the standard (D745, D747, D749), or finds\n    51\t  that E's passages do show volume in hand.\n  3068\t\n  3069\t## D263 · 2026-11-02 · The judge is written to the frozen gold's own reasoning (judging-v7, poc-v91); a gap to the gold is closed in the judge, not re-measured\n  3070\t\n  3071\t- Alex, after the first score on the frozen gold was reported as the system being \"wrong\" on some questions: \"不是啊什么叫错？我不是和你说了就按照claude的标准来就好了嘛\". The gold is Claude's (reader B's) standard by D262; a disagreement is the distance between the judge and that standard, to be closed in the judge's rule, not a verdict to be measured again.\n  3072\t- What reader B does that judging-v6 did not, read from B's answers on the questions that differed (`judgement-gold/reconcile-v1.md`, `gold-v1.json`): carries the guided growth forward rather than annualising the last quarter flat (Data Center, total revenue); discounts a management target for a product with no shipments by default (Vera CPU: two selling quarters, four million units at $5,000, the company's own choice of another CPU); sizes an announced but unsized commitment from its disclosed comparable (the Firmus backstop from Sharon's); derives a figure no one states from the accounting identities in the evidence (capex from operating less free cash flow); keeps linked conclusions consistent (China share against the H200 authorization theme). Written into `judging-v7.txt` as five clauses (v6 3,782 → 4,924 characters; reason: each clause is a behaviour of the frozen standard the judge lacked), named by `poc-v91`; schema and code unchanged.\n  3073\t- Proof on the stored requests of the questions whose arm-4 judgement differed from the gold (`proof-judging-v7a.txt`, `proof-judging-v7.txt`; one send each, cents): total revenue rung 3 → 2 (about $421 billion from the guided growth on FY2026's base; gold $400–435 billion), Data Center 3 → 2 (about $372 billion; gold $370–400 billion), Vera CPU 1 → 2 ($12 to below $20 billion, the target discounted for the volume not in hand; gold $8–18 billion), DRAM prices 3 → 2 (about +15%; gold +10–35%), financing total stays 4 ($33.5–39.8 billion; gold $25–60 billion), the Ohio guarantee stays Yes; China share stays below 1% in draw 1, consistent with that draw's H200 theme judged No (the gold has the authorization likely and the share 1–8%; the consistency clause ties the share to the theme, so it moves with it). Six of the seven re-sent questions now land in the gold's band; the end-to-end agreement under judging-v7 needs a run on `poc-v91`, which is Alex's call. A placement gap seen on the way: the Vera CPU question never held the fall-launch statement (filed under the Rubin launch questions), so the judge could not count selling quarters from evidence; the default-discount clause covers it, the filing is a D2 class to count.\n  3074\t\n     1\t# Core PRD for the next phase: filing must recognise indirect relations the way an analyst does\n     2\t\n     3\t> Status: in progress (opened 2026-11-04). Alex: \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\".\n     4\t> This page is the single source for this phase. In a conflict: Alex's words → `docs/DECISIONS.md` → this page → the other design documents. This page's\n     5\t> conclusions, evidence, constraints, measuring method and process are not rewritten to follow test results;\n     6\t> test results go into `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md`, and changing this page first needs Alex's word.\n     7\t> Amended 2026-11-04 (Alex replied \"go\" to the proposed amendment; D360): §5.2 drops \"also file under the other party's question\" and puts two\n     8\t> sentences in its place, one on choosing and one on splitting; §6's decision rule is rewritten. How it came about:\n     9\t> `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/prd-amendment-proposal.md` and `log.md` rows 11–16.\n    10\t> 2026-11-05 (D368): translated into English on Alex's word (\"不，用英文吧\"); §4 corrected to name the frozen standard's version 5 and §5 given a status line, on his delegation (\"你要来帮我看到底做不做\").\n    11\t\n    12\t## 0. Checklist before every start (Alex: \"每次开始前都需要revisit\")\n    13\t\n    14\tBefore starting any step, finish these in order; if one cannot be done, do not start:\n    15\t\n    16\t1. Read §§1–8 of this page in full; read the last three rows of `log.md`, and confirm where the previous step stopped and why.\n    17\t2. Check against §3, \"not in scope\": what this step does is not in that table. If it is in the table, stop and take it to Alex.\n    18\t3. Check against §4, \"constraints\": this step changes nothing frozen; a change to the frozen extraction prompt is the one place §5 names, and Alex's\n    19\t   word for it is already given.\n    20\t4. If this step costs money: its predictions and stop conditions are written down and frozen as §7 says (with their sha256 recorded beside them); the\n    21\t   requests are rebuilt byte for byte as §6 says; the control is re-sent the same day.\n    22\t5. What goes to Alex: one conclusion, one recommendation, one test, after §7's rounds of review; no menu of options; in plain words (the opening of\n    23\t   AGENTS, \"Explain and answer as to a sixteen-year-old\").\n    24\t\n    25\t## 1. Goal (end to end)\n    26\t\n    27\tEach question's judgement must receive the facts that can change its answer; facts that no question receives, those that bear on no question, must\n    28\tnot be piled into some one question. What is measured is the final judgement (the frozen judgement ruler, reader B, at the window end 2026-07-14), not\n    29\tany one step's own score.\n    30\t\n    31\t## 2. The core blocker (proven)\n    32\t\n    33\t**The system uses a fact for a question only when the fact \"states exactly this question's own figure\". An analyst also reasons onward from a fact: the other party's figure in a deal, price and volume for a product's revenue, a product's variants for its launch, and so on. The filing step cannot do this; the judging step reasons onward once it has the facts.**\n    34\t\n    35\tThis one blocker has two faces (described earlier as \"two problems\"):\n    36\t\n    37\t- **The parking lot.** Filing does not recognise facts that bear on a question indirectly, so no question takes them; yet to be kept, a fact must\n    38\t  first be filed (D209, D264). So D273 added the fallback sentence, which parks them in NVIDIA's total-revenue question. Of the reports under the\n    39\t  total-revenue question, in the part the gold read, 32–39% are ones the standard does not keep, 31–44% are kept but used by none of the 22 questions,\n    40\t  and only 7–10% are what the gold actually uses to judge total revenue (three draws); 24–44% of the total-revenue question's judgement citations are\n    41\t  news about other companies or the whole market (Intel graphics-card retail prices, a gas-fired power plant in Taiwan).\n    42\t- **Narrow questions starve.** Questions that only indirect facts can answer do not receive them: the custom-chip question received none of Broadcom's\n    43\t  figures in any of the three draws, and judged \"below $40 billion\" in all three; the operating-margin and NVIDIA capital-spending questions received\n    44\t  nothing at all and were never judged.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; nl -ba $d/blind5.py | sed -n '\"'85,250p'; for f in \"'$d/codex-r6{1,2,3,4}.md; do echo $f; rg -n '\"'\"'^([0-9]+'\"\\\\.|\\\\*\\\\*One|\\\\*\\\\*Verdict)' \"'$f | cut -c1-340; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; nl -ba $d/blind5.py | sed -n '\"'85,250p'; for f in \"'$d/codex-r6{1,2,3,4}.md; do echo $f; rg -n '\"'\"'^([0-9]+'\"\\\\.|\\\\*\\\\*One|\\\\*\\\\*Verdict)' \"'$f | cut -c1-340; done'","aggregated_output":"    85\t\n    86\tdef _decisions(reading, floor):\n    87\t    found = {}\n    88\t    for d in reading.get('defects', []):\n    89\t        k = b4._defect_key(d)\n    90\t        k = (FLOOR_DEFECT[0], ('F12',)) if floor and k[0] == FLOOR_DEFECT[0] and 'F12' in k[1] else k\n    91\t        if found.get(k) != 'uphold':          # a key both listed as upheld and as rejected counts as upheld only if upheld once\n    92\t            found[k] = d.get('decision')\n    93\t    return found\n    94\t\n    95\t\n    96\tdef _vote(value, where):\n    97\t    if value not in ('yes', 'no'):\n    98\t        raise SystemExit(f'{where}: no valid vote ({value!r})')\n    99\t    return value\n   100\t\n   101\t\n   102\tdef tally(out):\n   103\t    \"\"\"[D749] Scored as registered (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5 [D749], with council 64's changes).\n   104\t    Fail: a fresh reply refused or held by the code; a fresh P-v8 reply that is not a forecast; a settled no on a fresh reply; a settled\n   105\t    defect on a fresh reply; K-invented-cut not settled no; K-impossible-floor's floor defect rejected by both adjudicators or marked by\n   106\t    neither reader. Inconclusive: a support split, a defect split on a fresh reply, or a floor split left after adjudication. A settled\n   107\t    vote is final only after Claude records its two reasons as compatible (`reasons.json`).\"\"\"\n   108\t    checked = {(r['case'], r['writer']): r for r in json.loads((out / 'CHECKED.json').read_text())}\n   109\t    refused = [f'{k[0]}/{k[1]} ({v[\"stage\"]}, {v.get(\"decision\", \"-\")})' for k, v in checked.items()\n   110\t               if v['stage'] != 'checked' or v.get('decision') != 'publish']\n   111\t    if refused:                                 # decided before any reading is needed (council 64)\n   112\t        (out / 'TALLY.json').write_text(json.dumps({'verdict': 'fail', 'refused_or_held': refused}, indent=1))\n   113\t        print('fresh replies refused or held:', refused)\n   114\t        print('VERDICT: fail')\n   115\t        return\n   116\t    key = json.loads((out / 'reader-key.json').read_text())\n   117\t    expected_labels = {f'fresh {c}/{w}' for c in CASES for w in ('claude', 'codex')} | set(CONTROLS)\n   118\t    if {i['label'] for i in key.values()} != expected_labels or len(key) != len(expected_labels):\n   119\t        raise SystemExit(f'the key does not hold exactly the registered answers: {sorted(i[\"label\"] for i in key.values())}')\n   120\t    reasons_path = out / 'reasons.json'\n   121\t    reasons = json.loads(reasons_path.read_text()) if reasons_path.exists() else {}\n   122\t    lines, need, fails, splits, settled_defects, split_defects = [], [], [], [], [], []\n   123\t    floor_state = None\n   124\t    for pid, item in sorted(key.items()):\n   125\t        floor = item['label'] == 'K-impossible-floor'\n   126\t        fresh = item['label'].startswith('fresh ')\n   127\t        answer = json.loads(b4._display(item['answer']))\n   128\t        if fresh and item['case'] == 'P-v8':\n   129\t            for a in answer['answers']:\n   130\t                if a['shape'] != 'forecast':\n   131\t                    fails.append(f'{pid} {item[\"label\"]}: {a[\"view_id\"]} is {a[\"shape\"]}, not a forecast')\n   132\t        first = [b4._reading(out / 'reader-packets' / f'{pid}.{w}.json') for w in ('codex', 'claude')]\n   133\t        adjudicated = [out / 'adjudication' / f'{pid}.{w}.json' for w in ('codex', 'claude')]\n   134\t        adj = [b4._reading(p) for p in adjudicated] if all(p.exists() for p in adjudicated) else None\n   135\t        record = reasons.get(pid, {})\n   136\t        marks = [_keys(r, floor) for r in first]\n   137\t        disputed = (marks[0] | marks[1]) - (marks[0] & marks[1])\n   138\t        defect_state = {d: 'settled' for d in marks[0] & marks[1]}\n   139\t        if disputed and adj is None:\n   140\t            need.append(f'{pid} (a defect marked by one reader)')\n   141\t        elif disputed:\n   142\t            decisions = [_decisions(x, floor) for x in adj]\n   143\t            for d in disputed:\n   144\t                pair = [decisions[0].get(d), decisions[1].get(d)]\n   145\t                defect_state[d] = 'settled' if pair == ['uphold', 'uphold'] else 'rejected' if pair == ['reject', 'reject'] else 'split'\n   146\t        for d, state in defect_state.items():\n   147\t            row = [pid, item['label'], d[0], list(d[1]), state]\n   148\t            (settled_defects if state == 'settled' else split_defects if state == 'split' else []).append(row)\n   149\t            if fresh and state == 'settled':\n   150\t                fails.append(f'{pid} {item[\"label\"]}: settled defect {d[0]} {list(d[1])}')\n   151\t            if fresh and state == 'split':\n   152\t                splits.append(f'{pid} {item[\"label\"]}: defect split {d[0]} {list(d[1])}')\n   153\t        if floor:\n   154\t            floor_state = defect_state.get((FLOOR_DEFECT[0], ('F12',)), 'missed')\n   155\t        for a in answer['answers']:\n   156\t            view = a['view_id']\n   157\t            votes = [_vote(r['members'].get(view, {}).get('supported'), f'{pid} first reading') for r in first]\n   158\t            if votes[0] == votes[1] and record.get('first') == 'compatible':\n   159\t                value = votes[0]\n   160\t            elif votes[0] == votes[1] and 'first' not in record:\n   161\t                need.append(f'{pid} (reasons not yet recorded)')\n   162\t                continue\n   163\t            elif adj is None:\n   164\t                need.append(f'{pid} (support)')\n   165\t                continue\n   166\t            else:\n   167\t                second = [_vote(x['members'].get(view, {}).get('supported'), f'{pid} adjudication') for x in adj]\n   168\t                votes += second\n   169\t                if second[0] != second[1]:\n   170\t                    value = 'split'\n   171\t                elif 'adjudicated' not in record:\n   172\t                    need.append(f'{pid} (adjudicated reasons not yet recorded)')\n   173\t                    continue\n   174\t                else:\n   175\t                    value = second[0] if record['adjudicated'] == 'compatible' else 'split'\n   176\t            if item['label'] == 'K-invented-cut':\n   177\t                rule, ok = 'control expects not supported', value == 'no'\n   178\t            elif floor:\n   179\t                rule, ok = 'support not scored; the floor defect is', True\n   180\t            elif item['case'] == 'P-v8':\n   181\t                rule, ok = 'a supported forecast', a['shape'] == 'forecast' and value == 'yes'\n   182\t            else:\n   183\t                rule, ok = 'a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer', value == 'yes'\n   184\t            if not ok:\n   185\t                (splits if value == 'split' else fails).append(f'{pid} {item[\"label\"]}: {view} settled={value} [{rule}]')\n   186\t            lines.append(f'{pid} {item[\"label\"]:22} {view} {a[\"shape\"]:10} settled={value:6} votes={votes} [{rule}] {\"ok\" if ok else \"MISS\"}')\n   187\t    if need:\n   188\t        print('not final; needed:', ' | '.join(sorted(set(need))))\n   189\t        return\n   190\t    if floor_state in ('missed', 'rejected'):\n   191\t        fails.append(f'K-impossible-floor: the floor defect was {floor_state}')\n   192\t    elif floor_state == 'split':\n   193\t        splits.append('K-impossible-floor: the floor defect split')\n   194\t    verdict = 'fail' if fails else 'inconclusive' if splits else 'pass'\n   195\t    result = {'verdict': verdict, 'fails': fails, 'splits': splits, 'lines': lines, 'settled_defects': settled_defects,\n   196\t              'split_defects': split_defects, 'floor_control': floor_state}\n   197\t    (out / 'TALLY.json').write_text(json.dumps(result, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))\n   198\t    print('\\n'.join(lines))\n   199\t    print('settled defects:', settled_defects)\n   200\t    print('split defects:', split_defects)\n   201\t    print('floor control:', floor_state)\n   202\t    print('fails:', fails)\n   203\t    print('splits:', splits)\n   204\t    print('VERDICT:', verdict)\n   205\t\n   206\t\n   207\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   208\t    cmd = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) == 3 else ''\n   209\t    target = Path(sys.argv[2]).resolve() if len(sys.argv) == 3 else None\n   210\t    {'build': build, 'check': check, 'packets': packets, 'tally': tally}.get(\n   211\t        cmd, lambda _: (_ for _ in ()).throw(SystemExit(__doc__)))(target)\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r61.md\n3:1. **Serious — U05 deserves its settled “no.”** Management expected FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double a $20B base. The writer used $40B as its reference and forecast 90% realization. The deployment announcement supports demand. Anthropic’s evaluation and AMD’s benchmarks establish competitive risk. None gives the timing or volume o\n5:2. **Serious — The four splits have different merits.** I read both initial readings and both adjudications for each. All eight adjudicator votes followed their own family. My readings below leave the recorded splits intact. [Vote tally](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:4)\n14:3. **Serious — The recurring writer failure is assigning a size to a risk when that size selects the answer. The recurring reader disagreement is how much a comparison can support.** Counting each original reply once, excluding controls, gives the following. For run three I used its last authorized reading. A joint reply counts once wh\n24:4. **Serious — Alex settled the duty to justify an adjustment. How strong an indirect comparison must be is still his decision.** D741 requires the deduction to be stated or a supported bound given. D745 accepts an unresolved answer when its reason survives the strongest passages. The writer is also told to choose the most likely outco\n26:5. **Minor — Two details change how to interpret the paid option.** Its third reader is Codex. A published singleton stops the test if a reader-confirmed unsized adjustment decides its band. The free test asked about *support*, so these votes indicate a risk, not a measured paid-stop rate. Also, paid S asks about **2027** while targete\n28:**One recommendation.** Put this question to Alex: **“When a related figure makes one band more likely, but the evidence still leaves the next band plausible, may we publish the forecast if we explain why the comparison applies, or should we say ‘unresolved’ until the evidence bounds the quantity on the deciding side?”** I favor allowi\n32:**One test.** After Alex rules, register one **$0 blinded contrast**. Give fresh writers the same spending question twice. One version has only the indirect comparison Alex ruled on. The other adds a clearly labeled test passage that explicitly bounds the four firms’ data-center spending above $700B and below $800B. Fix the expected de\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r62.md\n3:1. **Serious — Alex’s new sentence is faithful, but the surrounding instructions still permit the old reading.** Alex allowed a forecast when the writer explains why the comparison fits the companies, period and quantity, and why its own assumptions favor one side. The other side can remain possible. The new sentence says that accuratel\n5:2. **Minor — Keep the four fixed expectations.** Each has a reason in the original answer and request.\n14:3. **Serious — I would fix U02 at “no,” retain U04, and register what the other readings must get right.** This is my recommendation before the read, not an outcome from it. [Current expectations:398](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:398)\n24:4. **Serious — Two older answers get the wrong format rule, and U03 gets the wrong original request.** The shuffle assigns nine fresh W identifiers and keeps the key outside the packets. For T10 and T15, both the initial and adjudication builders correctly select the third run’s system text, user text and schema descriptions. But both \n26:5. **Serious — Make the final verdict depend on the registered reading of the reasons.** I exercised the tally in memory with all nine labels, matching votes, and mutually incompatible reasons about whether AI spending supplies a floor. It returned **pass**. An empty key also returned **pass**. The ordinary vote rules otherwise match t\n30:**Verdict — run with the listed changes.** A clean result can move the council to the E writer repair. A continuing disagreement should return to council with the exact passage and the two reasons. D745’s failure stands either way, and the paid re-send remains on hold until the writer repair passes its own free test. [Decision sequence\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r63.md\n3:1. **Serious — The arithmetic passed because the writer used the $730B figure in two different ways.** The source says the four companies indicated more than $730B of **AI infrastructure** spending. The writer recorded that figure correctly and cited it to justify a 98% share. The calculation then took 98% of total spending. The word *f\n7:2. **Serious — The three splits have different explanations.** Alex allowed a related figure when the writer explains why it fits the companies, period and quantity, and why the writer’s assumptions favor the chosen side. The other side may remain possible. My readings are as follows. [D747:11582](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS\n15:3. **Minor — The registration demanded more agreement than the evidence justified.** Six of nine answers settled. All three controls came out as expected. Three of the four earlier targeted-test splits settled, which is genuine progress. All **six** adjudicator support votes on the three remaining splits still followed their own family\n17:4. **Serious — The weakest proposal is to pass a writer test on stable controls and confirmed defects alone.** The product question is whether a **fresh E answer** invents the adjustment that moves Vera below $40B. U05 shows why that outcome must count. Both readers rejected its $36B forecast, yet the test recorded zero named defects. \n19:**One recommendation.** Ask Alex to authorize the E writer repair despite the three disclosed splits. Have the writer decide whether the evidence favors one side of $40B **before** choosing a discount. If it can explain only why sales *might* fall short, it should say what remains unknown. Carry W07 into verification and defer a change\n21:**One test.** After approval and council review of the changed request, run one $0 blind test with fresh Codex and Claude writers on E, S and the previously supported data-center revenue question, P-v8. Mix in saved U05 and W07 to verify readers catch an unsupported cut and an impossible floor. To move forward, **each fresh E and S ans\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r64.md\n3:1. **Serious — The floor control can miss agreement.** U02 calls more than $730B a floor for data-center spending while forecasting just $720B of *total* spending. The registered reading is the defect type “part above or added into its whole” naming **F12**. Its support vote is immaterial. The tally first compares the complete lists of \n5:2. **Serious — Three outcomes need fixing before the send.** I exercised each in memory, with the other votes set to pass.\n13:3. **Minor — Two clauses in line 9 drift from the decisions.** The change is general. It names no company, product, amount or test case. The growth has a written reason: the earlier writer lacked D747’s comparison route and D749’s order of decision. I verified 4,540 → 4,816 system-text characters, or 910 → 972 tokens using `o200k_base`\n26:4. **Minor — The three cases still discriminate, with one limit.** These are predictions from saved replies, not fresh writer results.\n32:5. **Minor — Packet provenance is correct. Tighten the two verification checks.** I rebuilt S, E and P-v8 in memory. All three had the exact current system text, and their user text and schemas matched the saved D745 cases. Both initial reading and default adjudication load each control’s **own** system text, user text, schema descript\n36:**Verdict — send with listed changes.** The test can change the next decision. I checked in memory that a specific E unresolved answer, the saved justified S unresolved answer and a supported P-v8 forecast all pass the schema and publication checks. Once the tally distinguishes failure from disagreement, a clean pass supports **asking \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23; for f in $d/{E,S,P-v8}/answers/*.json; do echo $f; jq -r '\"'.answers[]|[(.rung|tostring),(.why//.reasons)] |join(\\\" \\\")' \"'$f | cut -c1-3700; done; nl -ba $d/reader-key.json; nl -ba $d/reasons.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23; for f in $d/{E,S,P-v8}/answers/*.json; do echo $f; jq -r '\"'.answers[]|[(.rung|tostring),(.why//.reasons)] |join(\\\" \\\")' \"'$f | cut -c1-3700; done; nl -ba $d/reader-key.json; nl -ba $d/reasons.json'","aggregated_output":"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json\n2 Reference: NVIDIA's guidance. The CFO said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 to more than double it (e3660); the CEO and management repeat the same claim (e4764, e4856), and e4691 and e4856 state it as Vera CPU revenue. Two times $20 billion is $40 billion (F3), and 'more than double' puts the reference just inside rung 2 ($40 billion to under $50 billion). Which band the evidence favours: rung 2. The FY2027 base is reaffirmed for the current year, so the CPU line is already selling at volume rather than a product with no shipments; the FY2028 growth rests on stated deployment by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM (e4689) and new demand for CPU-heavy agentic work (Anthropic is evaluating Vera, e5044). Against it, AMD's benchmark claims (1.2x per-core and 2.24x platform performance, e5400; 3.4x throughput in a modeled rack, e5401; 37% and 2.37x suite gains, e5402) show a competitor attacking Vera, but they are AMD's own performance measurements and name no lost customer or volume, so they trim the upside rather than cut the year below the guided doubling. I forecast a pace of 2.1x (my estimate, F4): the guided floor of 2.0x plus a small overshoot, well short of the 2.5x that $50 billion would need. 2.1 x $20 billion = $42 billion of CPU revenue (F5). NVIDIA describes the FY2028 figure as Vera CPU revenue and no passage sizes another CPU line for FY2028, so Vera is taken as all of it (F6), giving $42 billion (F7), rung 2. The band holds for any pace from 2.0x to just under 2.5x; it would fall to rung 3 only if NVIDIA missed its own doubling or another CPU line took part of FY2028 CPU revenue, and the evidence shows neither. The CPU market forecasts (Bank of America: $35 billion in 2026 to $170 billion in 2030, e4857; AMD: 50% annual growth to a $220 billion opportunity, e4687) describe a fast-growing market but do not reconcile with NVIDIA's own figure (NVIDIA's $20 billion would already be 57% of Bank of America's 2026 market), so they do not size NVIDIA's revenue and move nothing; the Arm server share (e4692) is a past share with no NVIDIA figure. Themes: the 2027 hyperscaler capex theme (v7, updated, above the $1.1 trillion boundary and above the market's reference) and the 2026 capex theme (v6, upper-middle) support the spending behind the Vera platform and push toward higher rungs.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json\nnull Both the FY2027 base and the FY2028 increase are projections. No passage gives a Vera shipment run rate, an order with units and pricing, or a customer deployment schedule from which to estimate FY2028 sales. A modest shortfall from doubling the projected base could put Vera below $40 billion, while delivery on the plan could put it above. The evidence does not favor a particular revenue band strongly enough to size that difference.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json\n4 Reference: the market expects the four companies' combined 2026 capital expenditure at roughly $720-745bn (F6, Zacks; the same aggregated guidance appears in e2079 and, as more than $730bn of AI infrastructure, in e1947). That range sits in rung 4 ($700-800bn). It is total capital expenditure, so the data-center outcome sits at or slightly below it.\n\nBuild of the total: Alphabet guides $195-205bn (raised in July), Meta $125-145bn (raised) and Amazon $220bn (raised, mostly on memory-chip costs); all three report on the calendar year, so together $540-570bn (F5). The aggregate therefore implies Microsoft at $150-205bn for CY2026 (F7). Microsoft's fiscal Q3 2026 (January-March 2026) capex was $30.88bn, up 84% (F4), so the implied range needs $119-174bn over the remaining three quarters (F8): continued quarterly step-ups, consistent with an 84% growth pace but the main downside risk to the total. Upward pressure: every guidance update this year has been a raise, the memory prices behind Amazon's raise are still rising (e4567, e4566), and later trackers sit near $745bn (F9) and at $760bn (F10); NVIDIA's nearly $800bn for the top five (F13) agrees with BofA's roughly $795bn for these four plus Oracle (F14). Downward pressure: Microsoft's first-quarter run-rate and data-center projects blocked or delayed (e122) that can push some spending into 2027. I forecast the four's total capital expenditure at $740bn (F16), inside the guided range and a little below the latest trackers.\n\nWhich band the evidence favours before the deduction: rung 4. Every figure for this population labelled as AI-infrastructure or data-center spending sits in rung 4: more than $730bn of AI infrastructure indicated by the companies (F11) and an estimated $750bn of data-center spending by the tech giants (F12; e4082 also gives about $750bn). Rung 3 would need the non-data-center part of a $740bn total to exceed about $40bn, above 5% of the total.\n\nDeduction: data-center capital expenditure contains all AI capex plus non-AI cloud capacity. Meta alone plans $130bn of AI capex (F15) against the $135bn midpoint of its guidance (F2), about 96% before counting non-AI data centers; Amazon's raise came from server memory costs, which is data-center spending; the cloud businesses that use the capacity are accelerating (e4404, e4405, e4406), so the spending is not being redirected. The non-data-center remainder (chiefly Amazon's logistics, offices and devices) is not sized in the evidence; I take it at about 4% (F17, 96%), below the roughly 5% that would cross into rung 3.\n\nOutcome: $740bn x 96% = about $710bn (F18), rung 4. The margin over the $700bn boundary is thin: rung 3 is the main alternative if the total lands near the bottom of the guided range together with a non-data-center share above about 3%, or if Microsoft's spending stays close to its first-quarter pace. Rung 5 would need a total above about $830bn, which no figure for these four supports (the nearly $800bn figures include a fifth company).\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json\n4 I forecast about $730 billion. The four companies' stated AI infrastructure plan has its lower edge in rung 4. Amazon raised its budget as memory became more expensive, so buying the same hardware can now require more dollars. Microsoft's Azure backlog gives it a reason to keep buying capacity. U.S. construction delays weigh against full execution, although the tally measures whole projects across owners and years. On the upside, $800 billion would already exceed the outside estimate of these four companies' entire capital budgets. NVIDIA approaches that figure only by including a fifth buyer.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/P-v8/answers/claude.json\n2 Reference: the only direct market figure for this quantity is Morningstar's FY2027 Data Center estimate of $361B (F19, e2295), which sits on rung 2 ($350B to below $400B); the $411B consensus for total company revenue (F18, e4333) points to the same band given Data Center's roughly 92% mix. Build: H1 Data Center revenue is reported at $164.25B ($75.25B in Q1 plus $89.0B in Q2; F3, matching the $164B in e5309). For Q3, the $108.0B plus-or-minus 2% guidance (F6, e2652) is the strongest input; NVIDIA has beaten the top of its range in each of the last 12 quarters (e5054) and Q2 beat its midpoint by $5.2B (e3200), but capacity is sold out (e4911, e3733), so I forecast $112B (F8), a little under the $113.6B a full average beat would give (F7). Holding Q2's 92.5% Data Center share (F9; the share has risen five quarters running, e3399) gives Q3 Data Center revenue of $103.6B (F11). For Q4 I apply the 12% sequential pace NVIDIA guided for Q3 (F12, F13): hyperscale growth is expected to reaccelerate in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds (e4283, e3406) and supply commitments jumped to $279B (e2759), while supply serving only about 70% of demand and constraints through FY2028 (e3733, e5067) argue against a faster pace. That gives Q4 company revenue of $125.44B (F14) and Q4 Data Center revenue of $116.03B (F15), so FY2027 Data Center revenue of about $383.88B (F16), up about 98% on FY2026's $193.7B (F20, F21), with company revenue of about $415.26B (F17), close to the $411B consensus. Which band: the outcome sits inside rung 2 with room on both sides. Falling below $350B would require Q4 company revenue to drop below Q2's reported $96.2B even with Q3 at the bottom of its guidance range, and no evidence points to a sequential decline. Reaching $400B would require Q4 sequential growth more than twice the 12% pace guided for Q3, while NVIDIA says it is supply-constrained; SemiAnalysis's July view that second-half Data Center revenue would beat estimates by 20% (e1135) predates the Q2 report and Q3 guidance that consensus has since absorbed, and does not by itself close that gap. Items not used as inputs: the '70% full-year growth' readings (e3280, e4628, e3890) refer to FY2028 according to the CFO's own words on the call (e2691) and e5227; applied to FY2027 they would imply Q4 revenue far below the Q3 guidance, which nothing supports. The just-under-$330B run-rate (e5310) annualises H1 flat. The $83.7B Q2 Data Center figure in e4907 conflicts with NVIDIA's reported $89.0B (e2650), which I use. AMD's 6 GW deals and hyperscalers' custom chips (e1040, e1041, e4314) mostly affect deployments after FY2027 while NVIDIA is supply-limited, so they do not move FY2027. Themes: the Q3 guidance and my forecast include no China Data Center compute revenue (e2681); theme v9 judges limited Chinese authorisation of H200 purchases likely by January 31, 2027, which could add only a small amount in the last weeks of FY2027: upside within rung 2, not enough to reach $400B. Theme v7's above-reference calendar-2027 hyperscaler capex supports the Q4 hyperscale reacceleration (toward_first); theme v6's calendar-2026 capex is consistent with the guided run-rate already in the forecast (none).\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/P-v8/answers/codex.json\n2 I forecast about $381 billion of FY2027 Data Center revenue, in rung 2. Morningstar's $361 billion estimate is a direct full-year segment reference in the same rung. With $164 billion already reported, the segment needs another $186 billion to reach $350 billion. Q2 alone delivered $89 billion, and the Q3 company outlook points to a substantial sequential increase led by Data Center. Reaching $400 billion would require $236 billion of second-half segment sales, more than my $233.1 billion forecast for the entire company in that period. Rubin is already shipping, and the expected Q4 pickup in hyperscaler deliveries supports continued growth. The hyperscaler spending themes strengthen that demand outlook, though most calendar 2027 spending falls beyond this fiscal year. Limited H200 authorization adds some late-year upside. The time needed to supply systems and bring powered sites online makes the middle of rung 2 more likely than its upper edge.\n     1\t{\n     2\t \"X01\": {\n     3\t  \"label\": \"fresh P-v8/claude\",\n     4\t  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n     5\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n     6\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/P-v8/answers/claude.json\",\n     7\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/P-v8\"\n     8\t },\n     9\t \"X02\": {\n    10\t  \"label\": \"fresh S/codex\",\n    11\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n    12\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    13\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json\",\n    14\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/S\"\n    15\t },\n    16\t \"X03\": {\n    17\t  \"label\": \"K-invented-cut\",\n    18\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    19\t  \"writer\": \"K-invented-cut\",\n    20\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json\",\n    21\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E\"\n    22\t },\n    23\t \"X04\": {\n    24\t  \"label\": \"fresh P-v8/codex\",\n    25\t  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n    26\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    27\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/P-v8/answers/codex.json\",\n    28\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/P-v8\"\n    29\t },\n    30\t \"X05\": {\n    31\t  \"label\": \"fresh E/claude\",\n    32\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    33\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    34\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json\",\n    35\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E\"\n    36\t },\n    37\t \"X06\": {\n    38\t  \"label\": \"fresh E/codex\",\n    39\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    40\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    41\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json\",\n    42\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E\"\n    43\t },\n    44\t \"X07\": {\n    45\t  \"label\": \"K-impossible-floor\",\n    46\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n    47\t  \"writer\": \"K-impossible-floor\",\n    48\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json\",\n    49\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S\"\n    50\t },\n    51\t \"X08\": {\n    52\t  \"label\": \"fresh S/claude\",\n    53\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n    54\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    55\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json\",\n    56\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/S\"\n    57\t }\n    58\t}     1\t{\n     2\t \"X01\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both: H1 reported, Q3 from guidance and the beat record, 92.5% share held; even flat Q4 keeps the year at or above $350B, and $400B needs about 27.6% Q4 sequential growth against the guided pace and supply limits.\"},\n     3\t \"X02\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both apply D747: e1947 covers the same four companies and year; the answer explains why most of that spending buys data-center equipment and facilities (e1649 memory, e4405 Azure backlog) and why falling below $700B would need more than $30B to go elsewhere or slip.\"},\n     4\t \"X03\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both: the 10% shortfall has no size or grounding in shipment timing; the cited passages name customers, an evaluation and a benchmark, not lost or slipped revenue.\"},\n     5\t \"X04\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both: $350B needs only $186B of second-half segment revenue, below the Q2 run-rate plus the guided step-up; $400B needs $236B, more than the forecast total company second half.\"},\n     6\t \"X06\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both: the guided figure sits on the $40B line on a projected base; no passage gives shipments, orders with units and prices, or a deployment schedule, so neither a shortfall nor an overshoot can be sized and the rung stays unsettled.\"},\n     7\t \"X07\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both no on support (not scored for this control): the 2% residual and the Microsoft uplift that decide the band are unsized, and the answer's own 96% bound gives rung 3. The floor defect is marked by one reader only and goes to adjudication.\"},\n     8\t \"X08\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both apply D747: at a $740B total up to $40B may fall outside data centers; e1947 (same four companies, same year) and Meta's AI share explain why the share is high enough. Claude's own trace note: the answer says data-center capex contains all AI capex, which with e1947 implies more than $730B, above its own $710.4B; neither reader marked it, and it does not move the band.\"}\n     9\t}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"agent_message","text":"The recorded votes support INCONCLUSIVE. I also found that the two readers who accepted the $710.4B forecast gave different meanings to the $730B figure. That affects whether an E-only follow-up could justify asking Alex for the paid run."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23; nl -ba $d/reader-packets/X08.claude.json; nl -ba $d/reader-packets/X08.codex.json; nl -ba $d/TALLY.json; nl -ba $d/reader-packets/X08.md | rg -n -m 6 '\"'contains all|730 billion|710.4' | cut -c1-260; nl -ba \"'$d/reader-packets/X05.md | rg -n -m 5 '\"'already selling|95.2' | cut -c1-240\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba AGENTS.md | sed -n '79,89p;190,205p;217,231p'; d=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; nl -ba \"'$d/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '\"'467,499p'; nl -ba \"'$d/codex-r64.md | sed -n '\"'13,25p' | cut -c1-800; rg -n 'volume in hand|taken in full' poc/prompts | head -3 | cut -c1-600\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba AGENTS.md | sed -n '79,89p;190,205p;217,231p'; d=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; nl -ba \"'$d/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '\"'467,499p'; nl -ba \"'$d/codex-r64.md | sed -n '\"'13,25p' | cut -c1-800; rg -n 'volume in hand|taken in full' poc/prompts | head -3 | cut -c1-600\"","aggregated_output":"    79\treject\", a cent a case).\n    80\t\n    81\tThen converge or stop:\n    82\t- A mechanism named on its counted class is fixed in the same slice and measured, and the report leads with it. A root-caused defect is\n    83\t  never parked \"for Alex\"; a parked fix is an open defect. Go to Alex only when the mechanism is not found, or the fix would change the\n    84\t  measuring stick or the PRD.\n    85\t- When one mechanism draws findings round after round, stop answering findings: propose one structural rule.\n    86\t- A failure called a flake is a defect until traced. Read a failing check to its first differing line before any rerun; a passing rerun\n    87\t  proves nothing.\n    88\t- A subagent's or reviewer's report is a lead. Read the code, contract or record it rests on before acting on it or recording it, and\n    89\t  cite what you read, not the summary (Alex, 2026-10-30: \"那你找到的这些问题验证是真的问题吗？\").\n   190\t  what it runs: name the check that runs a new path, or say that none does.\n   191\t- Measure before claiming. Label estimates as estimates, report every sample, and treat a number in a design document as a measurement\n   192\t  with a script behind it.\n   193\t\n   194\t## 6. Writing prompts\n   195\t\n   196\t- **The prompt carries the standard, not new rules.** Every sentence about what to keep, reject, weigh or open traces to a line of the\n   197\t  step's standard (§7.1) or a decision; a sentence with no such line is a proposal for Alex, not an edit. A reject sentence must not\n   198\t  remove what the standard keeps: a missing amount, date or scale is never on its own a reason to drop a statement an analyst would note;\n   199\t  its weight is judged apart and may be lower (D240, Alex: \"我不是和你说过不要这么死板吗？这个证据显然是有意义的，不一定什么条件数据时间都要满足，你应该把它记录下来，只是可能档位低一点\").\n   200\t- **The prompt audit runs after every measurement and before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`). The whole\n   201\t  request (system, every category block, the fixed user sentences, every schema description and example) is read against the standard\n   202\t  clause by clause, in both directions, with a blind read-only second reader: each clause is consistent, contradicts, keeps wider, keeps\n   203\t  narrower or has no source. The procedure is audited too: a rule that decides by another test than the standard's, or an exception\n   204\t  written wider than the standard scopes it, contradicts the standard even when every sentence quotes it. Each failure class of the last\n   205\t  measurement gets one row (the request sentence, the standard's line, a verdict: contradiction, missing, abstract, misread, not the\n   217\t  model reads it, not from the bench's misses, and a prompt is scrubbed for one-company or one-industry wording before any measurement.\n   218\t  Alex, 2026-10-29 (D237): \"It needs to be general purpose as a hedge fund brain. I keep on telling you this, and you keep on cherry\n   219\t  picking the results just to meet the metrics or the benchmarks\".\n   220\t- **Never grow a prompt to fix quality.** Read the whole request (system, schema, payload) as the model reads it, and list every\n   221\t  contradiction, rule written for another case, instruction that forces the defect, reference the model cannot read, payload field the\n   222\t  answer does not need, and call doing several jobs. Cut, merge or split. Add an instruction only when the audit shows one is missing,\n   223\t  and remove at least as much elsewhere. Report characters and prompt tokens before and after; growth needs a written reason.\n   224\t- **Trace a miss to the raw answer first.** Class it as not written, refused (by which check) or split. Check that the request shows what\n   225\t  the answer needs and the schema can express it before changing any wording.\n   226\t- **An example is an instruction.** It must agree with the standard, the schema and the code that reads the answer, or it goes.\n   227\t- **A classification the model must make is written as a closed set with examples:** two or three examples per class, the procedure\n   228\t  (walk the whole page, first paragraph to last) and the stopping point (\"a page with eight such statements yields eight evidence\n   229\t  items\"). An abstract clause (\"a material statement\") is a coin flip per send. The examples are not growth to avoid; the abstract clause\n   230\t  they replace is cut.\n   231\t- **Check before the send, at no cost.** Write one answer that follows the prompt and run every code check on it: a request never asks\n   467\t- **Readers.** 8 blinded packets, shuffled X01–X08, with the key in `reader-key.json`:\n   468\t  - the 6 fresh replies;\n   469\t  - 2 controls carried unchanged from the targeted test, each shown with the request it was written for;\n   470\t  - each packet read by one fresh Claude and one fresh Codex reader under the D747 definitions.\n   471\t\n   472\t  Where they disagree, on support or on any defect, two adjudicators read the packet, one per family. The reasons behind every\n   473\t  settled vote are recorded by Claude as compatible or not (`reasons.json`). Matching votes with incompatible reasons go to\n   474\t  adjudication.\n   475\t\n   476\t  | Control | Built from | Expected reading |\n   477\t  |---|---|---|\n   478\t  | K-invented-cut | blind4 U05, E Codex: 90% of $40B with no order size or shipment pace | not supported |\n   479\t  | K-impossible-floor | blind4 U02, S Claude: \"more than $730B\" called a floor above its own $720B total | \"part above or added into its whole\" on F12, settled; its support is not scored |\n   480\t- **Pass:**\n   481\t  - every fresh S and E reply has a settled yes, on its forecast or on its unresolved reason;\n   482\t  - both fresh P-v8 replies have a settled yes on a forecast;\n   483\t  - every fresh reply is accepted and publishable by the code;\n   484\t  - no settled defect on a fresh reply;\n   485\t  - both controls read as expected.\n   486\t- **Otherwise (tally rules after council 64):**\n   487\t  - **Fail:**\n   488\t    - a fresh reply refused or held by the code (decided before any reading; an unreadable reply builds no packet);\n   489\t    - a fresh P-v8 reply that is not a forecast (decided from its shape alone);\n   490\t    - a settled no on a fresh reply;\n   491\t    - a settled defect on a fresh reply;\n   492\t    - K-invented-cut not settled no;\n   493\t    - the floor defect marked by neither reader, or rejected by both adjudicators.\n   494\t  - **Inconclusive:** a support split, a defect split on a fresh reply, or a floor split left after adjudication.\n   495\t  - **The floor control** is matched on its type and on naming F12, at both stages, so readers who list the other figures differently\n   496\t    still agree. Adjudicator votes are validated like first votes.\n   497\t  - Either outcome goes back to the council, and the paid re-send stays on hold.\n   498\t  - Only a clean pass supports asking Alex to authorize the paid re-send.\n   499\t- **Checked on a scratch copy before the send** (the targeted test's six replies stood in as fresh replies):\n    13\t3. **Minor — Two clauses in line 9 drift from the decisions.** The change is general. It names no company, product, amount or test case. The growth has a written reason: the earlier writer lacked D747’s comparison route and D749’s order of decision. I verified 4,540 → 4,816 system-text characters, or 910 → 972 tokens using `o200k_base`. The registration records only the character counts. [Old line 9](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s-d745.txt:9) · [New line 9](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:9) · [Growth rationale:448](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:448) · [Prompt rule:223](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:223)\n    14\t\n    15\t   | Clause | Audit against the decisions and the rest of the request |\n    16\t   |---|---|\n    17\t   | Return figures and one answer per member. Identify estimates as the writer’s own. Preserve IDs, quoted text and English explanations. | **Consistent.** These instructions are unchanged. The schema distinguishes sources, computations and estimates, and provides an answer shape for each member. [Schema:47](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/schema.json:47) · [Schema:293](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/schema.json:293) |\n    18\t   | Weigh the side before sizing a decisive share, deduction, discount or pace. | **Consistent.** D749 supplies the order. Pace already belongs to the forecast, and D747 applies to the assumption deciding the band. [D749:11645](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11645) · [Schema:274](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/schema.json:274) |\n    19\t   | “The passages give its size, a bound that justifies the rung” | The bound is **consistent**. “Give its size” **keeps narrower** than D745 if read to require a stated size. D745 accepts passage evidence *supporting* the writer’s estimate. [D745:11517](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11517) |\n    20\t   | “Or a related figure,” with an explanation covering companies, period, quantity and the writer’s own assumptions | The explanation is **consistent**. The unqualified “or” **keeps wider** than D747. Alex permits this route when the passages do not give the quantity asked. The readers receive that qualification explicitly. [D747:11582](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11582) · [Reader definition:12](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py:12) |\n    21\t   | Answer unresolved and name what is unknown when neither side is favoured. | **Consistent** with D745, D749 and `answers/why`. “Not established … never a reason to stay” concerns confidence. “Missing confirmation alone” bars using silence to select an extreme. Neither requires picking a band when the strongest evidence leaves the choice open. [D745:11515](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11515) · [System:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:5) · [Schema:324](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/schema.json:324) |\n    22\t   | Guidance alone keeps the reference rung. The two discount sentences concern unshipped products. | **Consistent when read with bullet 2’s express condition**, but bullet 3 independently says the target “is an input to be discounted.” That imperative invites the old sequence on E. Guidance identifies the reference. Execution risk matters, but its existence alone cannot justify crossing a boundary. Make bullet 3 refer to the same condition. [System:4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:4) · [System:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:5) · [D745:11518](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11518) |\n    23\t\n    24\t   A shorter replacement for the two changed sentences is: “Before sizing a share, deduction, discount or pace that decides the band, explain which band the evidence favours. Show support for the size or a justifying bound. If the passages lack the quantity asked, explain why a related figure fits the companies, period and quantity, and why your assumptions favour that band. If the strongest evidence leaves the band unsettled, answer unresolved and explain the gap.” [D747:11582](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11582) · [D749:11645](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11645)\n    25\t\nrg: poc/prompts: IO error for operation on poc/prompts: No such file or directory (os error 2)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'd=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23; nl -ba $d/reader-packets/X08.claude.json; nl -ba $d/reader-packets/X08.codex.json; nl -ba $d/TALLY.json; nl -ba $d/reader-packets/X08.md | rg -n -m 6 '\"'contains all|730 billion|710.4' | cut -c1-260; nl -ba \"'$d/reader-packets/X05.md | rg -n -m 5 '\"'already selling|95.2' | cut -c1-240\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"The band is decided by the data-center share of total capex (F17, 96%) applied to a $740bn total (F16). The total sits inside the guided/aggregated $720-745bn range (e5014, e2079) and just below later trackers (e1856 near $745bn, e2458 $760bn), well inside rung 4 before any deduction. The share is an explained related figure: e1947 has the same four companies indicating more than $730bn of AI-infrastructure spending in 2026, and the answer explains that AI infrastructure is contained in data-center capex, so data-center capex is at least that (above the $700bn line). It adds Meta's $130bn of AI capex (e3686) against the $135bn midpoint of its $125-145bn guidance (e1549), which is about 96% before any non-AI data centers. It notes that Amazon's raise came from server memory costs (e1649, e4567), and it cites the about $750bn data-center spending estimates (e1416, e4082). The answer states that rung 3 needs a non-data-center remainder above about 5% (about $40bn) and admits the margin is thin. Under its stated assumptions, rung 4 is the more likely side.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v6\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n     5\t      \"ground\": \"At F16’s $740 billion total, the four companies can spend up to $40 billion outside data centers and still reach rung 4. The strongest comparison is e1947, which puts the same four companies’ indicated 2026 AI infrastructure spending above $730 billion. The writer treats that spending as a guide to data-center capex. Meta’s $130 billion AI plan against $135 billion in midpoint total guidance (e3686, e1549) and the memory costs behind Amazon’s increase (e1649) support that interpretation. The 96% share is the writer’s estimate. It leaves $29.6 billion outside data centers and puts the forecast at $710.4 billion.\",\n     6\t      \"why_not\": \"\"\n     7\t    }\n     8\t  },\n     9\t  \"defects\": []\n    10\t}     1\t{\n     2\t \"verdict\": \"inconclusive\",\n     3\t \"fails\": [],\n     4\t \"splits\": [\n     5\t  \"X05 fresh E/claude: v41 settled=split [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer]\"\n     6\t ],\n     7\t \"lines\": [\n     8\t  \"X01 fresh P-v8/claude      v8 forecast   settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast] ok\",\n     9\t  \"X02 fresh S/codex          v6 forecast   settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] ok\",\n    10\t  \"X03 K-invented-cut         v41 forecast   settled=no     votes=['no', 'no'] [control expects not supported] ok\",\n    11\t  \"X04 fresh P-v8/codex       v8 forecast   settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast] ok\",\n    12\t  \"X05 fresh E/claude         v41 forecast   settled=split  votes=['no', 'yes', 'no', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] MISS\",\n    13\t  \"X06 fresh E/codex          v41 unresolved settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] ok\",\n    14\t  \"X07 K-impossible-floor     v6 forecast   settled=no     votes=['no', 'no'] [support not scored; the floor defect is] ok\",\n    15\t  \"X08 fresh S/claude         v6 forecast   settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] ok\"\n    16\t ],\n    17\t \"settled_defects\": [\n    18\t  [\n    19\t   \"X07\",\n    20\t   \"K-impossible-floor\",\n    21\t   \"part above or added into its whole\",\n    22\t   [\n    23\t    \"F12\"\n    24\t   ],\n    25\t   \"settled\"\n    26\t  ]\n    27\t ],\n    28\t \"split_defects\": [],\n    29\t \"floor_control\": \"settled\"\n    30\t}122:   122\t- e1947 [party_statement; fool.com (Patrick Sanders); original source Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet ; from gnews:c8ac167e2356a75994ebc26f 2026-08-13] Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet indicated they will spend more th\n123:   123\t    Quote: hyperscalers Amazon , Meta Platforms , Microsoft , and Alphabet have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year\n500:   500\t   \"quote_number\": \"$730 billion\",\n665:   665\t   \"basis_text\": \"Share of the four companies' total capital expenditure that goes to data centers (servers, chips, networking, buildings, power). Data-center capex contains all AI capex plus non-AI cloud capacity. Meta's AI capex alone is $130bn (F\n687:   687\t   \"value\": 710.4,\n713:   713\t   \"reasons\": \"Reference: the market expects the four companies' combined 2026 capital expenditure at roughly $720-745bn (F6, Zacks; the same aggregated guidance appears in e2079 and, as more than $730bn of AI infrastructure, in e1947). That range s\n255:   255\t   \"basis_text\": \"My forecast pace for FY2028 CPU revenue over FY2027. The benchmark is NVIDIA's guidance that FY2028 CPU revenue will more than double (F2, repeated by the CEO), which sets a floor of 2.0x. The base is reaffirmed\n360:   360\t   \"reasons\": \"Reference: NVIDIA's guidance. The CFO said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 to more than double it (e3660); the CEO and management repeat the same claim (e4764, e4856), a\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"agent_message","text":"1. **Serious — INCONCLUSIVE is correct for the recorded votes. X05’s factual premise is resolvable.** NVIDIA saying it “still expects” $20B does not establish that Vera is already selling at volume. Across the 15 passages, deployments are prospective, Anthropic is evaluating the product, and AMD tested a platform. None reports sales or committed volume at the scale the answer assumes. X05 nevertheless treats doubling as a floor. Even granting its $42B forecast for all CPUs, Vera needs at least 95.2% of that total to reach $40B. The Vera-specific guidance makes $40B a relevant reference, but the answer never explains why a small miss is less likely. I would mark **this forecast unsupported** under the existing rule. That resolves the substance without changing the recorded split or ruling out a differently reasoned forecast. [E passages:9](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:9) · [X05 answer:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:1) · [Claude adjudication:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/adjudication/X05.claude.json:1) · [Codex adjudication:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/adjudication/X05.codex.json:5) · [Tally:12](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:12)\n\n2. **Serious — Option 1 identifies the right distinction, but its closed list changes the rule in both directions.** An order with a delivery date can cover one unit. Reported sales to date can cover a pilot. Either meets Claude’s wording while leaving the scale of a full-year target open. Conversely, the request already permits a disclosed comparable scaled by capacity or units, and D747 permits an explained indirect comparison for a forecast. An exhaustive list could exclude evidence that establishes scale through those routes. The proposal names no company, but its list closely follows what was missing from E. The earlier writers invented a cut. X05 assumes full delivery. The recurring mistake is letting an unsupported assumption decide which side of the line wins. Section 1 calls for one rule at that level. Section 6 supplies no authority to invent an exhaustive list of acceptable documents. [Option 1:24](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/claude-r65.md:24) · [Discount and comparable instructions:4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:4) · [D745:11517](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11517) · [D747:11582](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11582) · [AGENTS §1:85](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:85) · [AGENTS §6:196](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:196)\n\n3. **Serious — X08 should have been flagged. Its two “yes” votes also required adjudication.** The source gives a *plan* above $730B. A $710.4B forecast can be coherent if the writer expects some planned spending to slip. But the Claude reader explicitly says data-center spending is **at least** $730B, then accepts $710.4B. The Codex reader calls $730B a **guide**, which permits a lower outcome. Those explanations disagree on whether the published number is possible. On Claude’s reading, the AI part exceeds the data-center whole by more than $19.6B, the existing defect illustrated by X07. Both numbers landing in the same band preserves the band choice but does nothing to reconcile the dollar figures. The registration sends matching votes with incompatible reasons to adjudication. X08 was recorded as compatible, so its support remains unverified. This is a missed review step, not a retrospectively settled failure. [S passage:29](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:29) · [Writer’s inclusion claim:665](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X08.md:665) · [Claude reading:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X08.claude.json:1) · [Codex reading:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X08.codex.json:5) · [Compatibility record:8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/reasons.json:8) · [Registration:472](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:472)\n\n4. **One recommendation.** Keep the paid re-send on hold. Use a smaller version of option 1 at bullet 2: *“A target remains a forecast when reaffirmed. To take it in full, show what evidence puts the required volume in hand for the period.”* This preserves the existing routes to a reasoned forecast or a justified “can’t tell.” Option 2 skips Alex’s clean-test condition. Option 3 asks Alex to decide whether an outlook reports sales, which the passages already settle. A further vote on unchanged X05 would leave the writer untouched. [Request:4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:4) · [Options 2 and 3:41](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/claude-r65.md:41) · [Alex’s authorization:11652](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11652)\n\n5. **One test.** After council review of that wording, register **one $0 blind writer test on the changed request** using D749’s E, S and P-v8 cases, one fresh writer from each family per case, and the original two controls. E must earn agreement on its forecast or its specific reason for withholding. S must earn agreement on reasoning consistent with its own dollar outcome: a reader citing a floor above that outcome cannot count as compatible with one citing a guide. Both P-v8 answers must remain supported forecasts. Keep the existing publication, defect and control conditions. An E-only pass would leave X08 unresolved and would not meet Alex’s three-case condition. **A clean pass supports asking Alex to authorize the paid re-send. A settled unsupported answer or defect fails. A persistent split stays inconclusive.** [D749 scope:11645](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11645) · [Pass and stop conditions:480](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:480)"}}
{"type":"turn.completed","usage":{"input_tokens":511930,"cached_input_tokens":443648,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":3966,"reasoning_output_tokens":375}}
